HIMSSON 0.0 — PRODUCT IDENTITY & CORE DEFINITION
Status: LOCKED
Product: HIMSSON
Parent Company: MANKO Innovations
Country: Tanzania
Initial Commercial Market: Dar es Salaam
Document Purpose: Establish the foundational identity and definition of HIMSSON before architecture, UX, and development decisions are made.
 
1. What HIMSSON Is
HIMSSON is a school management and operating platform designed for private schools in Tanzania.
The platform is intended to bring the major operational activities of a school into one structured digital environment rather than requiring school management to depend on disconnected books, spreadsheets, paper files, messaging applications, and manual processes.
HIMSSON is therefore not defined as simply:
•	a student database;
•	a school fee collection application;
•	an attendance application;
•	a report-card generator; or
•	an SMS platform.
It is a connected school operating system in which these functions work together around the same underlying school, people, academic, financial, and operational records.
 
2. Target Education Market
The original project document positioned HIMSSON around private primary schools.
The product definition was subsequently expanded and locked to support:
•	Private Primary Schools
•	Private Secondary Schools
•	Private Advanced-Level Schools
The initial commercial focus remains Dar es Salaam, Tanzania, while the architecture should avoid unnecessary geographic hard-coding so the platform can later expand beyond Dar es Salaam.
Important architectural implication
HIMSSON must not be designed around a primary-school-only structure such as:
Class 1 → Class 2 → Class 3 → ... → Class 7
Instead, education level, academic structure, classes/forms, streams, subjects, and related academic structures must be configurable.
 
3. Core Problems HIMSSON Solves
HIMSSON is built around three major operational problems.
3.1 Lost Money
Schools can lose financial control through:
•	fee fraud;
•	missing or inconsistent receipts;
•	unclear payment records;
•	unpaid balances;
•	manual reconciliation; and
•	weak visibility into outstanding fees.
HIMSSON therefore treats school finance as a core operational subsystem.
 
3.2 Lost Time
Schools spend significant time performing repetitive administrative work such as:
•	attendance recording;
•	marks entry;
•	calculating results;
•	preparing report cards;
•	communicating with parents;
•	preparing records; and
•	searching through paper files.
HIMSSON aims to reduce this administrative workload through connected digital workflows.
 
3.3 Paper Trail
Schools depend heavily on physical documents and manual records.
HIMSSON aims to reduce this dependency through:
•	digital student records;
•	digital financial records;
•	digital academic records;
•	digital documents;
•	digital receipts;
•	downloadable reports; and
•	structured school data.
 
4. HIMSSON's Core Promise
HIMSSON's product promise is:
Make school management simpler, more controlled, more transparent, and less dependent on paper.
The original commercial framing includes three major outcomes:
Control Money
Improve fee visibility, payment recording, receipts, balances, and financial accountability.
Save Time
Reduce repetitive administrative work through connected digital workflows.
Eliminate Paper
Move important school records and operational documents into a structured digital environment.
 
5. Primary Product Outcomes
HIMSSON should ultimately enable a school to:
1.	Know who its students are.
2.	Know who is responsible for each student.
3.	Know what each student owes.
4.	Know what each student has paid.
5.	Know which students are absent.
6.	Know their academic performance.
7.	Communicate with parents efficiently.
8.	Store important school records digitally.
9.	Generate required reports.
10.	Export school data when necessary.
These outcomes are more important than any individual screen.
 
6. Core Product Domains
HIMSSON should be treated as a connected platform consisting of major domains rather than a collection of unrelated features.
Domain 1 — School & People
Students, parents, staff, teachers, administrators, identities, and relationships.
Domain 2 — Money
Fees, billing, payments, receipts, balances, arrears, and financial reporting.
Domain 3 — Academics
Classes, subjects, attendance, marks, grading, results, positions, report cards, and academic history.
Domain 4 — Communication
Announcements, fee reminders, attendance notifications, events, holidays, SMS/WhatsApp communication, and delivery tracking.
Domain 5 — Documents & School Operations
Student documents, school documents, inventory, exports, backups, operational records, and paperless workflows.
 
7. Product Architecture Principle
The domains above must remain interconnected.
For example:
Student
→ belongs to a school
→ belongs to an academic structure
→ has a parent/guardian relationship
→ receives fee obligations
→ makes or receives payment records
→ has attendance
→ has academic results
→ may have documents
→ may trigger communication.
This means HIMSSON should maintain single sources of truth wherever possible.
Data should be entered once and reused across relevant workflows.
 
8. V1 Product Direction
The original V1 scope includes the following major capabilities:
P0 — Core Operations
•	Student Management
•	Fees & Payments
•	Attendance
•	Marks & Report Cards
•	Announcements
•	User Roles
•	Data Export
P1 — Growth & Trust
•	Admission
•	Inventory
•	Multi-Term Records
•	Dashboards
•	Swahili/English Toggle
Tanzania-Specific Innovation
•	USSD
•	Document Library
•	Auto Backup + Undo
•	Offline First + Low-Data
•	Paperless School Operations
The exact implementation boundaries of these capabilities will be defined in later locked documents.
 
9. V1 Exclusions
The following are explicitly outside the initial V1 scope:
•	Bus tracking
•	Biometrics
•	Online classes
•	CCTV
•	AI chatbot
•	Full e-learning platform
These exclusions exist to protect the core product from unnecessary scope expansion.
 
10. Commercial Positioning
HIMSSON is intended to be an affordable school-management platform suitable for the Tanzanian private-school market.
The original project direction identified a target pricing concept of approximately:
TZS 500–1,000 per student per term
This is a commercial direction rather than a final locked pricing structure.
Pricing, packages, billing model, subscriptions, and commercial terms will be defined separately.
 
11. Foundational Product Statement
HIMSSON is therefore defined as:
A Tanzania-focused school management and operating platform for private Primary, Secondary, and Advanced-level schools, designed to control school finances, save administrative time, improve communication, and replace fragmented paper-based processes with one connected digital system.
This definition is the foundation for all subsequent HIMSSON architecture and product decisions.
 
12. LOCKED DECISION
HIMSSON is not being built as a collection of independent school-management features.
It is being built as a connected school operating platform.
All future architecture, database, UX, security, workflows, integrations, and screen decisions must support this definition.
 
Status: LOCKED — HIMSSON 0.0
 
HIMSSON 0.1 — PRODUCT PRINCIPLES & DESIGN RULES
Status: LOCKED
Product: HIMSSON
Purpose: Establish the non-negotiable principles that govern product design, architecture, UX, development, and future expansion.
 
1. Purpose
HIMSSON must be developed deliberately.
The platform should not be built page-by-page and then repeatedly redesigned when later requirements expose architectural problems.
Every major feature must therefore be evaluated against the principles in this document.
 
2. Principle One — School Operations Come First
HIMSSON exists to operate a school.
Technology, visual design, automation, integrations, and advanced features must support actual school workflows.
The system should prioritize:
•	clarity;
•	speed;
•	reliability;
•	accountability;
•	accessibility;
•	operational usefulness.
A visually impressive feature that makes school work harder is not considered a successful feature.
 
3. Principle Two — M-Pesa First
The Tanzanian payment environment is a core product consideration.
HIMSSON should prioritize practical local payment methods, particularly:
•	M-Pesa;
•	mobile money;
•	USSD.
Card-first or international payment assumptions should not determine the financial architecture.
The payment system must eventually support the full financial chain:
Fee Structure
→ Student Charge
→ Payment Initiation
→ Provider Confirmation
→ Payment Record
→ Receipt
→ Student Balance/Ledger
→ Financial Reporting
Payment integration is therefore treated as a financial subsystem, not merely a payment button.
 
4. Principle Three — Offline Is a Product Requirement
Offline capability is not a cosmetic enhancement.
HIMSSON targets environments where:
•	internet connectivity may be unreliable;
•	mobile data may be expensive;
•	schools may use low-end Android devices;
•	staff may work in areas with weak connectivity.
Therefore, offline-first behavior must be considered at architectural level.
The system must eventually define:
•	what can work offline;
•	what data is cached;
•	how changes are queued;
•	how synchronization works;
•	what happens when conflicts occur;
•	how users know whether information is synced.
Offline functionality must not simply be added after the main application is completed.
 
5. Principle Four — Low-End Android Support
HIMSSON is designed for practical Tanzanian school environments.
The product should remain usable on devices such as:
•	entry-level Tecno devices;
•	entry-level Infinix devices;
•	Android devices with approximately 2 GB RAM;
•	limited storage;
•	slower mobile connections.
This means the platform should avoid unnecessary:
•	heavy animations;
•	oversized assets;
•	excessive network requests;
•	bloated interfaces;
•	unnecessary background processing.
 
6. Principle Five — Swahili Matters
HIMSSON operates in Tanzania.
The product should support both:
•	Swahili
•	English
Swahili should be treated as a first-class language rather than an afterthought.
Communication sent to parents, particularly SMS, should support clear Swahili messaging.
The language architecture should allow future localization without rebuilding the application.
 
7. Principle Six — One Source of Truth
The same underlying information should not be independently recreated in multiple modules.
For example:
Marks should be entered once.
That information should then support:
Marks
→ Total
→ Average
→ Grade
→ Position
→ Teacher Comment
→ Report Card
→ Academic History
Similarly:
A payment should become one authoritative financial record that can support:
Payment
→ Receipt
→ Student Balance
→ Parent View
→ Accountant View
→ Financial Reporting
 
8. Principle Seven — Security Is Architectural
Security cannot depend solely on hiding buttons or pages.
HIMSSON will eventually support:
•	multiple schools;
•	multiple users;
•	different roles;
•	financial records;
•	student information;
•	documents;
•	academic records.
Therefore, authorization must eventually operate at the backend/data layer.
A user who is not authorized to access another school's information must not be able to obtain that information by manipulating frontend requests.
 
9. Principle Eight — Design Consistency
HIMSSON must have a unified design system.
Individual pages must not independently invent:
•	colors;
•	typography;
•	buttons;
•	cards;
•	inputs;
•	tables;
•	badges;
•	navigation patterns;
•	spacing;
•	shadows;
•	radius;
•	notifications;
•	modal behavior.
A page is considered part of HIMSSON, not an independent mini-product.
The HIMSSON design system will therefore be defined before large-scale screen production.
 
10. Principle Nine — Mobile Practicality
The system must work effectively on mobile devices because many school staff members will interact with the system through phones.
Important workflows should not assume desktop-only usage.
Examples include:
•	attendance;
•	parent communication;
•	payment verification;
•	student lookup;
•	announcements;
•	basic administrative actions.
 
11. Principle Ten — Paperless by Design
The paperless objective is not simply:
"Allow users to upload PDFs."
HIMSSON should progressively replace paper processes with structured digital workflows.
Examples:
•	digital student records;
•	digital receipts;
•	digital report cards;
•	digital documents;
•	digital attendance;
•	digital academic history;
•	downloadable reports.
 
12. Principle Eleven — Do Not Overbuild V1
HIMSSON should solve the highest-value school problems before introducing secondary technologies.
The platform should resist unnecessary scope such as:
•	biometrics;
•	CCTV;
•	e-learning;
•	AI chatbot;
•	complex transportation systems;
•	unrelated enterprise features.
The question for every proposed feature is:
Does this materially improve the core operation of a Tanzanian private school?
 
13. Principle Twelve — Build the Architecture Before the Screens
HIMSSON should follow:
Define → Architect → Design → Build → Test → Verify
rather than:
Build page → discover missing structure → patch → redesign → repeat.
The database, authorization model, school structure, academic model, financial model, and core workflows must be sufficiently defined before dependent UI is finalized.
 
14. Principle Thirteen — Do Not Let UI Decisions Define the Business Model
A dropdown, page, dashboard, or card should never silently determine business rules.
Business rules belong in the underlying product architecture.
For example:
A school selector must not merely change the label shown on screen.
It must eventually change the authorized operational context.
 
15. Principle Fourteen — Every Major Decision Must Be Locked
When a foundational product decision is made, it should be documented before implementation.
Locked decisions become part of the HIMSSON baseline.
Future implementation should conform to the locked baseline unless the decision is deliberately reopened and revised.
 
16. Development Discipline
HIMSSON development should follow:
DISCUSS → DEFINE → CHALLENGE → LOCK → DOCUMENT → BUILD → TEST → VERIFY → NEXT
This process is intended to prevent:
•	accidental scope expansion;
•	architecture drift;
•	repeated redesign;
•	inconsistent UI;
•	security assumptions;
•	undocumented business rules.
 
17. LOCKED DECISION
The following are established as HIMSSON foundational principles:
1.	School operations first.
2.	M-Pesa/mobile money first.
3.	USSD is strategically important.
4.	Offline-first is architectural.
5.	Low-end Android support matters.
6.	Swahili and English are first-class languages.
7.	One source of truth should govern core records.
8.	Security must operate at the data/backend layer.
9.	HIMSSON requires a unified design system.
10.	Mobile practicality is mandatory.
11.	Paperless operation is a core objective.
12.	V1 scope must remain disciplined.
13.	Architecture precedes large-scale UI implementation.
14.	Business rules must not be created accidentally by UI.
15.	Major product decisions must be explicitly locked and documented.
 
Status: LOCKED — HIMSSON 0.1


 
HIMSSON 0.2 — CORE PRODUCT ARCHITECTURE
Status: LOCKED
Product: HIMSSON
Purpose: Define the major architectural domains and the relationships between them before detailed role, school, academic, and financial structures are implemented.
 
1. Architectural Objective
HIMSSON must function as one connected system.
The architecture should prevent individual features from becoming isolated modules that maintain duplicate or contradictory information.
The platform is therefore organized around interconnected operational domains.
 
2. HIMSSON Core Domains
The initial architecture consists of five major domains.
                    		        HIMSSON
            			                  │
       ┌───────────────┼────────────────┐
       │                                             │                                               │
   PEOPLE &                         MONEY                             ACADEMICS
    SCHOOL                                  │                                               │
       │                                             │                                               │
       └───────────────┼────────────────┘
                                                       │
                                        COMMUNICATION
                                                       │
                             DOCUMENTS & OPERATIONS
These domains are interconnected rather than isolated.
 
3. Domain A — School & People
This domain establishes the people and institutional structures that everything else depends on.
It will ultimately contain concepts such as:
•	organization;
•	schools;
•	users;
•	students;
•	parents/guardians;
•	teachers;
•	accountants;
•	administrators;
•	staff;
•	relationships;
•	admissions;
•	student identity.
The detailed organization and multi-school structure is defined separately in HIMSSON 0.4.
 
4. Domain B — Money
The financial domain manages the school's economic records.
It is expected to include:
•	fee structures;
•	academic-period charges;
•	student balances;
•	payment records;
•	receipts;
•	arrears;
•	payment reconciliation;
•	financial reports;
•	payment notifications.
The system should maintain a reliable financial record rather than simply displaying payment information.
 
5. Financial Source-of-Truth Model
The intended conceptual chain is:
FEE STRUCTURE
      ↓
STUDENT CHARGE
      ↓
PAYMENT INITIATION
      ↓
PROVIDER CONFIRMATION
      ↓
PAYMENT RECORD
      ↓
RECEIPT
      ↓
STUDENT LEDGER / BALANCE
      ↓
REPORTING
This prevents the payment page from becoming the financial source of truth.
The authoritative financial record must exist at data level.
 
6. Domain C — Academics
The academic domain manages student learning records.
It is expected to include:
•	academic years;
•	terms;
•	classes/forms;
•	streams;
•	subjects;
•	subject assignments;
•	attendance;
•	marks;
•	grading;
•	examination results;
•	positions;
•	teacher comments;
•	report cards;
•	academic history.
The exact academic hierarchy will be defined later.
 
7. Academic Source-of-Truth Model
The intended conceptual chain is:
MARK ENTRY
    ↓
VALIDATED MARK
    ↓
TOTAL
    ↓
AVERAGE
    ↓
GRADE
    ↓
POSITION / RANKING
    ↓
COMMENT
    ↓
REPORT CARD
    ↓
ACADEMIC HISTORY
A report card should therefore be a generated representation of authoritative academic records rather than a separate manually maintained record.
 
8. Domain D — Communication
Communication is treated as a platform service rather than isolated messaging features.
Potential communication events include:
•	absence;
•	fee reminders;
•	payment confirmation;
•	announcements;
•	school events;
•	holidays;
•	academic notices;
•	administrative messages.
The communication system should eventually support:
EVENT
  ↓
MESSAGE RULE
  ↓
RECIPIENT
  ↓
CHANNEL
  ↓
DELIVERY
  ↓
DELIVERY STATUS
Potential channels include:
•	SMS;
•	WhatsApp;
•	in-app notifications.
The exact provider architecture will be defined later.
 
9. Domain E — Documents & School Operations
This domain supports the paperless objective.
It may include:
•	student documents;
•	admission documents;
•	school documents;
•	digital records;
•	inventory;
•	exports;
•	operational reports;
•	backup/recovery mechanisms.
Documents must eventually have clear ownership and access rules.
 
10. Cross-Domain Relationships
The most important architectural principle is that these domains interact around shared authoritative entities.
Example:
STUDENT
 │
 ├── SCHOOL
 │
 ├── PARENT / GUARDIAN
 │
 ├── CLASS / ACADEMIC CONTEXT
 │
 ├── FEE CHARGES
 │      └── PAYMENTS
 │             └── RECEIPTS
 │
 ├── ATTENDANCE
 │
 ├── MARKS
 │      └── RESULTS
 │             └── REPORT CARD
 │
 ├── COMMUNICATION
 │
 └── DOCUMENTS
This relationship is central to HIMSSON.
 
11. Event-Driven Thinking
Where appropriate, important system events should trigger related processes.
For example:
Attendance Event
Student marked absent
        ↓
Attendance record saved
        ↓
Absence notification generated
        ↓
Parent notified
        ↓
Delivery status recorded
Payment Event
Payment confirmed
        ↓
Payment record created
        ↓
Student balance updated
        ↓
Receipt generated
        ↓
Parent notified
Academic Event
Marks finalized
        ↓
Results calculated
        ↓
Report card generated
        ↓
Academic history updated
These examples describe the intended architecture, not yet-final implementation details.
 
12. Architectural Independence
Each domain should have clear responsibilities.
For example:
•	Communication should not become the owner of student records.
•	Report cards should not become the owner of marks.
•	Payment pages should not become the owner of financial truth.
•	UI components should not become the owner of business rules.
This separation reduces future duplication and inconsistency.
 
13. Data Ownership Principle
Every important piece of information should have an identifiable authoritative source.
Examples:
Information	Authoritative Domain
Student identity	School & People
Parent relationship	School & People
Fee obligation	Money
Payment	Money
Receipt	Money
Attendance record	Academics
Mark	Academics
Result	Academics
Report card	Academics / generated output
Message delivery	Communication
Student document	Documents
 
14. Architectural Consequence
Because HIMSSON supports multiple private-school education levels and is intended to support multiple schools under an organization, the architecture must avoid assumptions that only work for one school.
The system must ultimately support:
•	school-specific data;
•	school-specific configuration;
•	school-specific users and permissions;
•	school-specific academic structures;
•	school-specific financial structures;
•	school-specific communication;
•	school-specific documents.
The precise organization and authorization model is locked separately in HIMSSON 0.4.
 
15. UX Consequence
The architecture means the UI should eventually be organized around workflows rather than simply the original feature list.
For example, instead of thinking only:
"Build a Fees page."
we should think:
Fee structure → student charges → payment → receipt → balance → arrears → reporting → communication.
Similarly:
Marks → calculation → result → report card → history.
This produces a more coherent product.
 
16. LOCKED DECISION
HIMSSON is architecturally defined as a connected multi-domain school operating platform consisting of:
1.	School & People
2.	Money
3.	Academics
4.	Communication
5.	Documents & School Operations
Each domain must have clear data ownership and must interact with the others through controlled relationships and workflows.
 
Status: LOCKED — HIMSSON 0.2
 
HIMSSON 0.3 — PRODUCT DEVELOPMENT & ARCHITECTURE DISCIPLINE
Status: LOCKED
Product: HIMSSON
Purpose: Establish how HIMSSON will be designed and developed so later implementation does not force avoidable architectural or UX reconstruction.
 
1. The Problem This Document Solves
HIMSSON is a large operational system.
If development begins immediately with individual screens, later requirements can expose missing structures and force:
•	database redesign;
•	navigation redesign;
•	permission redesign;
•	financial restructuring;
•	academic restructuring;
•	repeated UI work;
•	security patches;
•	inconsistent user experiences.
HIMSSON will therefore be developed from the underlying product model outward.
 
2. Development Philosophy
The project follows:
DISCUSS → DEFINE → CHALLENGE → LOCK → DOCUMENT → BUILD → TEST → VERIFY → NEXT
Each stage has a purpose.
DISCUSS
Explore the requirement and possible interpretations.
DEFINE
Turn the discussion into explicit product rules.
CHALLENGE
Identify weaknesses, contradictions, edge cases, and future problems.
LOCK
Agree on the decision.
DOCUMENT
Create a permanent project record.
BUILD
Implement only what has been sufficiently defined.
TEST
Test expected and unexpected behavior.
VERIFY
Confirm that implementation matches the locked decision.
NEXT
Move to the next architectural decision.
 
3. No Uncontrolled Roaming
Development must not move into unrelated areas simply because an implementation tool suggests them.
For every phase, there should be:
•	defined scope;
•	defined objective;
•	known dependencies;
•	expected output;
•	verification criteria;
•	stopping conditions.
A builder should not be allowed to silently redefine the product.
 
4. Architecture Before Dependent UI
The following must be sufficiently defined before dependent screens are finalized:
•	organization structure;
•	school structure;
•	identity;
•	roles;
•	authorization;
•	academic model;
•	financial model;
•	communication model;
•	document ownership;
•	navigation architecture.
This prevents screens from becoming accidental architecture.
 
5. Screen Design Is Not Product Design
A screen is only a representation of an underlying workflow.
For example:
Fee Screen
The screen represents a larger financial workflow:
Fee Structure
      ↓
Charge
      ↓
Payment
      ↓
Confirmation
      ↓
Receipt
      ↓
Balance
      ↓
Reporting
Academic Screen
Class
  ↓
Subject
  ↓
Marks
  ↓
Calculation
  ↓
Result
  ↓
Report Card
The underlying workflow must be understood before the screen is finalized.
 
6. Design Constitution
HIMSSON must eventually have one authoritative design system.
The design constitution should define, at minimum:
•	logo treatment;
•	color palette;
•	typography;
•	backgrounds;
•	cards;
•	border radius;
•	shadows;
•	buttons;
•	inputs;
•	tables;
•	badges;
•	statuses;
•	icons;
•	navigation;
•	mobile navigation;
•	modals;
•	notifications;
•	charts;
•	loading states;
•	empty states;
•	error states;
•	confirmation patterns;
•	PDF/report styling.
Individual pages must consume this system rather than inventing alternatives.
 
7. UX Architecture Before Page Production
The original feature list should not automatically become the navigation structure.
The project should first define:
•	user journeys;
•	role-specific workflows;
•	navigation hierarchy;
•	screen families;
•	dashboard responsibilities;
•	mobile behavior;
•	desktop behavior;
•	shared components;
•	global actions.
Only then should detailed screens be produced.
 
8. Security Before Sensitive Data UI
Sensitive modules such as:
•	student records;
•	fees;
•	payments;
•	academic results;
•	documents;
•	staff records;
must have an authorization model before their interfaces are considered complete.
A page showing a record does not establish whether the user is allowed to access that record.
Authorization must eventually be enforced at the appropriate backend/data boundary.
 
9. Offline-First Must Be Designed Early
Offline support cannot be treated as a final enhancement.
Before development of offline-dependent workflows, the project must define:
•	locally available data;
•	local write behavior;
•	sync queue;
•	retry behavior;
•	conflict handling;
•	sync status;
•	failed synchronization;
•	user feedback;
•	recovery behavior.
The exact offline implementation will be defined during technical architecture.
 
10. Integrations Are Systems, Not Buttons
External integrations should be treated as subsystems.
Examples:
M-Pesa
Not:
"Add an M-Pesa button."
Instead:
Payment initiation → provider response → confirmation → reconciliation → financial record → receipt → notification.
SMS
Not:
"Add SMS."
Instead:
Event → recipient selection → message generation → provider → delivery result → delivery record.
WhatsApp
The same principle applies.
Integrations must have defined ownership, failure handling, retries, and records.
 
11. Testing Philosophy
Testing must verify more than visual appearance.
For each major feature, verification should consider:
Functional
Does the workflow work?
Authorization
Can the correct user access it?
Isolation
Can an unauthorized user access another school's information?
Data Integrity
Does the correct underlying record change?
Edge Cases
What happens with missing, duplicate, invalid, or unusual information?
Failure
What happens if a provider, network, or synchronization process fails?
Mobile
Does the workflow remain usable on low-end mobile devices?
Offline
Does the workflow behave correctly when connectivity disappears?
 
12. Change Control
A locked decision should not be silently modified during implementation.
If development exposes a genuine problem with a locked decision, the correct process is:
Existing Decision
      ↓
Problem Identified
      ↓
Reopen Decision
      ↓
Challenge / Re-evaluate
      ↓
New Decision
      ↓
Document Revision
      ↓
Implementation
This creates traceability.
 
13. Documentation as a Product Asset
Project documentation is not secondary administrative material.
It is part of HIMSSON's architecture.
Each major locked decision should record:
•	what was decided;
•	why it was decided;
•	what it means;
•	what it affects;
•	what it does not yet define;
•	examples;
•	architectural implications;
•	implementation constraints.
This prevents important decisions from becoming dependent on conversation history.
 
14. Development Checkpoint Principle
Each development phase should end with a verification checkpoint.
A checkpoint should answer:
1.	What was supposed to be built?
2.	What was actually built?
3.	Does it match the locked definition?
4.	What remains incomplete?
5.	What was discovered?
6.	Did any assumption change?
7.	Is the next phase safe to begin?
 
15. Stop Conditions
A phase should stop when its defined objective is complete and verified.
Do not continue expanding a phase simply because additional features are technically possible.
This is particularly important when working with AI-assisted builders, where additional prompts can unintentionally modify previously completed areas.
 
16. AI Builder Discipline
AI builders are implementation tools, not product owners.
They may propose:
•	new components;
•	different navigation;
•	alternative data structures;
•	additional features;
•	visual redesigns.
Such proposals must not automatically become HIMSSON requirements.
The locked product documentation remains the authority.
Implementation prompts should therefore contain:
•	scope;
•	constraints;
•	existing decisions;
•	expected behavior;
•	security requirements;
•	verification requirements;
•	explicit exclusions;
•	stopping conditions.
 
17. No Premature Screen-by-Screen Development
The project should not proceed as:
Build Page 1
→ Build Page 2
→ Build Page 3
→ Discover architecture problem
→ Redesign
→ Patch
→ Repeat
Instead:
Product Definition
      ↓
Architecture
      ↓
Domain Model
      ↓
Authorization
      ↓
UX Architecture
      ↓
Design System
      ↓
Screen Specifications
      ↓
Implementation
      ↓
Testing
      ↓
Verification
 
18. HIMSSON Development Principle
The guiding principle is:
Build the system that the school needs, not merely the pages that the interface appears to require.
Every page must ultimately have a defined purpose within a larger operational workflow.
 
19. LOCKED DECISION
HIMSSON development will follow a controlled architecture-first process.
The project will:
•	define before building;
•	challenge assumptions before locking;
•	document every major locked decision;
•	architect workflows before dependent screens;
•	enforce security at the correct data boundary;
•	treat integrations as systems;
•	treat offline support as architectural;
•	use one design constitution;
•	test functionality, security, integrity, failure, mobile, and offline behavior;
•	use explicit phase checkpoints;
•	prevent AI builders from silently redefining the product.
 
Status: LOCKED — HIMSSON 0.3
 
HIMSSON 0.4 — ORGANIZATION, AUTHORITY & MULTI-SCHOOL ACCESS
Status: 🔒 LOCKED
Decision Area: Product Architecture / Ownership / Authorization
Version: HIMSSON V1 Product Definition
Decision: Approved
 
1. Purpose
HIMSSON shall use an organization-centric, owner-controlled multi-school model.
The system must support a private school owner or education organization operating:
•	One school
•	Multiple schools
•	Multiple schools across different education levels
•	Multiple related schools that may optionally be linked where appropriate
The architecture must support this from the beginning rather than designing HIMSSON as a single-school application and attempting to add multi-school support later.
 
2. Core Organizational Structure
The fundamental HIMSSON hierarchy shall be:
Organization → Schools → School Administration → Operational Staff → Parents/Students
Conceptually:
ORGANIZATION / SCHOOL GROUP
│
├── SCHOOL A
│   ├── School Administration
│   ├── Teachers
│   ├── Accountants
│   └── Parents / Students
│
├── SCHOOL B
│   ├── School Administration
│   ├── Teachers
│   ├── Accountants
│   └── Parents / Students
│
└── SCHOOL C
    ├── School Administration
    ├── Teachers
    ├── Accountants
    └── Parents / Students
An organization may contain only one school or may contain multiple schools.
 
3. Why an Organization Layer Is Required
Although the product may be used by an individual school owner, HIMSSON shall not make the individual person the fundamental structural container.
Instead, the technical/product structure should recognize an Organization as the owner of the school's operational environments.
This allows HIMSSON to support both:
Single-school owner
Organization
└── School
and:
Multi-school owner
Organization
├── Primary School
├── Secondary School
└── Advanced School
without requiring a fundamental architectural redesign.
The person responsible for the organization may be an Owner, Director, Managing Director, Proprietor, or another appropriate business title, while the underlying authority model remains stable.
 
4. Owner / Director Authority
The organization-level authority shall be represented by an Owner / Director-level authority layer.
The displayed business title may eventually be configurable to accommodate terminology used by different private-school organizations.
Examples include:
•	Owner
•	Director
•	Managing Director
•	Proprietor
•	School Director
These titles should not automatically create different technical permission systems.
The underlying authorization model should remain consistent.
 
5. School Independence
Each school under an organization shall remain an independent operational environment.
For example:
ORGANIZATION
│
├── MANKO PRIMARY SCHOOL
│
├── MANKO SECONDARY SCHOOL
│
└── MANKO ADVANCED SCHOOL
Each school may independently maintain:
•	Students
•	Parents/guardians
•	Teachers
•	Accountants
•	Classes/forms
•	Subjects
•	Attendance
•	Academic records
•	Fee structures
•	Payments
•	Receipts
•	Reports
•	Documents
•	Announcements
•	School settings
The existence of a common owner does not mean that all school data is automatically merged.
 
6. Schools Must Not Automatically Share Operational Data
A fundamental security and product principle is:
Linked does not mean merged.
If an organization owns multiple schools, one school must not automatically gain unrestricted access to another school's operational records.
For example:
Organization
│
├── Primary School
│
└── Secondary School
The Primary School administrator must not automatically be able to view:
•	Secondary School students
•	Secondary School payments
•	Secondary School attendance
•	Secondary School academic records
•	Secondary School staff
•	Secondary School reports
simply because both schools belong to the same organization.
Cross-school access must be explicitly authorized.
 
7. Optional School Linking
HIMSSON shall support the concept of optional relationships/linking between schools belonging to the same organization.
The owner/director may choose to link schools where there is a legitimate operational reason.
Examples:
Organization
│
├── Primary School
│       ↕
│    LINKED
│       ↕
└── Secondary School
or:
Organization
│
├── Primary School
├── Secondary School
└── Advanced School
       │
       └── Linked School Group
However, linking does not automatically grant unrestricted data access.
The exact capabilities provided through school linking shall be defined separately during the detailed architecture and authorization phases.
 
8. One User Identity Across Multiple Schools
HIMSSON shall support one user identity with authorized access to multiple schools.
A person should not need completely separate login accounts merely because they have legitimate responsibilities in multiple schools.
For example:
USER IDENTITY
│
├── School A — Authorized
│
├── School B — Authorized
│
└── School C — Authorized
The user's actual access level may differ between schools.
 
9. Multi-School School Switcher
Where a user has authorized access to multiple schools, HIMSSON shall provide a school-switching mechanism.
Example:
Current School:
MANKO PRIMARY SCHOOL ▼

Available:
• MANKO PRIMARY SCHOOL
• MANKO SECONDARY SCHOOL
• MANKO ADVANCED SCHOOL
The user logs in once and selects the school context they wish to operate within.
 
10. School Context Is More Than a UI Dropdown
The school switcher shall not be treated merely as a visual navigation component.
Selecting a school must establish the user's active school context.
When the user selects:
MANKO SECONDARY SCHOOL
the application's operational context should become the Secondary School for all relevant operations.
This applies to:
•	Dashboard
•	Students
•	Admissions
•	Fees
•	Payments
•	Attendance
•	Academics
•	Reports
•	Documents
•	Communication
•	Staff
•	School settings
•	Other school-scoped functions
The backend/security layer must enforce this context.
A user must not be able to bypass school authorization simply by manipulating frontend navigation or requests.
 
11. Role and Permission Principle
HIMSSON shall not determine access solely from a user's role name.
Access should conceptually be determined by:
Identity + Role + Scope + Permissions
For example:
USER
│
├── ROLE
│     └── Owner / Director
│
├── SCHOOL SCOPE
│     ├── School A
│     ├── School B
│     └── School C
│
└── PERMISSIONS
      ├── Management
      ├── Finance
      ├── Reports
      └── Other authorized functions
This allows HIMSSON to support more precise authorization without creating an excessive number of technical roles.
 
12. Proposed Authority Layers
HIMSSON shall conceptually use the following authority hierarchy:
Level 1 — Organization / Owner / Director
Organization-level authority.
Potential responsibilities include:
•	Managing owned schools
•	Viewing authorized organization-level information
•	Switching between authorized schools
•	Managing school access
•	Assigning school administrators
•	Managing school relationships/linking
•	Accessing permitted reports
•	Organization-level configuration
•	Future subscription/billing management
The exact permissions will be formally defined later.
 
Level 2 — School Administration
School Administrator
School-scoped administrative authority.
Potential responsibilities include:
•	Student management
•	Admissions
•	Staff administration
•	School operations
•	Attendance oversight
•	Academic administration
•	Communication
•	Documents
•	School-level reporting
•	School settings
The School Administrator is responsible for a specific school and does not automatically control other schools within the organization.
 
Level 3 — Operational Staff
Includes roles such as:
Teacher
Teacher access shall be restricted according to the school and relevant teaching/class/subject assignments.
Accountant
Accountant access shall primarily cover authorized financial functions within the relevant school or schools.
Exact assignment and permission rules will be defined during the detailed authorization phase.
 
Level 4 — Parent
Parent access is fundamentally different from staff access.
A parent should primarily access information relating to their own child or children.
The parent does not receive general school-wide administrative access.
 
13. Multi-School Parent Scenario
The architecture should also allow a single parent identity to be associated with children attending different schools within the same organization, where appropriate.
Example:
PARENT
│
├── Child A
│     └── Primary School
│
└── Child B
      └── Secondary School
The parent may use one HIMSSON identity while the system determines which child/school information they are authorized to access.
This capability must preserve school-level data isolation.
 
14. Example: Different Permissions Across Schools
A single user may have different access levels in different schools.
Example:
USER: Organization Staff Member

School A
├── Full Management
├── Finance
└── Reports

School B
├── Finance
└── Reports

School C
└── Reports Only
The system should therefore support:
Role + School Scope + Functional Permissions
rather than assuming that one role gives identical access everywhere.
 
15. Owner Access vs School Administrator Access
The Owner/Director and School Administrator are separate authority concepts.
The Owner/Director has organization-level authority.
The School Administrator has school-level administrative authority.
An Owner/Director may be granted broad access to a school, but the architecture should not depend on the assumption that organization ownership automatically means unrestricted access to every operational function.
This distinction provides better security and allows more flexible future authorization policies.
 
16. Security Principle
HIMSSON shall be designed as a multi-tenant system with strict school-level data isolation.
The system must prevent unauthorized cross-school access at the backend/data-security layer.
Frontend visibility alone is not sufficient.
If a user is authorized for School A but not School B, the system must enforce that restriction even if the user attempts to access School B through:
•	manipulated URLs
•	direct requests
•	modified client-side state
•	API calls
•	other frontend bypass methods
Detailed RLS/security implementation will be defined in a later architecture phase.
 
17. What Is Locked by This Decision
The following principles are now locked:
1.	HIMSSON uses an organization-centric structure.
2.	One organization may operate one or multiple schools.
3.	Schools remain independent operational environments.
4.	The product supports an Owner/Director authority layer above School Administration.
5.	One user identity may have authorized access to multiple schools.
6.	Users with multi-school access can switch their active school context.
7.	School switching must affect the actual application/data context, not merely the UI.
8.	School access must be enforced by the backend/security architecture.
9.	Schools belonging to the same organization do not automatically share operational data.
10.	Schools may be optionally linked where appropriate.
11.	Linked does not mean merged.
12.	Authorization will ultimately be based on identity + role + scope + permissions.
13.	Parent access remains child-focused and must respect school-level authorization.
14.	The exact cross-school sharing/linking capabilities remain to be defined later.
 
18. Items Deliberately Not Yet Defined
This decision does not yet finalize:
•	Exact Owner/Director permissions
•	Exact School Administrator permissions
•	Exact Teacher permissions
•	Exact Accountant permissions
•	Exact Parent permissions
•	Cross-school data-sharing rules
•	Student transfer rules between schools
•	Multi-school parent workflows
•	Staff working across multiple schools
•	Organization-level financial reporting
•	Organization-level dashboards
•	Subscription/billing architecture
•	Campus/branch architecture
•	Detailed RLS implementation
These must be addressed in their appropriate future architecture discussions rather than being assumed here.
 
19. Architectural Principle
The fundamental HIMSSON organizational model is:
ORGANIZATION
     │
     ├── SCHOOL A
     │     ├── Administration
     │     ├── Teachers
     │     ├── Accountants
     │     └── Parents / Students
     │
     ├── SCHOOL B
     │     ├── Administration
     │     ├── Teachers
     │     ├── Accountants
     │     └── Parents / Students
     │
     └── SCHOOL C
           ├── Administration
           ├── Teachers
           ├── Accountants
           └── Parents / Students
With:
Identity → Role → School Scope → Permissions
as the fundamental access-control concept.
 
20. Final Decision Statement
HIMSSON shall be built as an organization-centric, multi-school platform for private schools. An owner or organization may operate one or multiple schools, including schools across Primary, Secondary and Advanced education levels. Each school remains independently managed and data-isolated, while authorized users may access multiple schools through a single HIMSSON identity and switch between authorized school contexts. Optional school linking may be supported, but linking shall never imply unrestricted data sharing or operational merging.

 
HIMSSON 0.5 — SCHOOL STRUCTURE & OPERATIONAL MODEL
Status: LOCKED
Product: HIMSSON
Parent Company: MANKO Innovations
Related Decision: HIMSSON 0.4 — Organization, Authority & Multi-School Access
Purpose: Define what a “School” represents inside HIMSSON and establish the structural relationship between organizations, schools, education levels, and future operational layers.
 
1. Purpose
HIMSSON 0.4 established the organization-centric model:
Organization → one or more Schools
HIMSSON 0.5 now defines what a School means within that structure.
This decision is foundational because students, teachers, classes, academic records, fees, attendance, documents, reports, and permissions will ultimately operate within a school context.
 
2. Definition of a School
A School is defined as:
An independently operated educational environment within an Organization.
A School has its own operational context and may maintain its own:
•	school identity;
•	students;
•	parents/guardians;
•	teachers;
•	staff;
•	academic structures;
•	attendance records;
•	academic records;
•	fee structures;
•	payment records;
•	documents;
•	announcements;
•	reports;
•	operational configuration.
A School therefore represents an actual operational environment, not merely a category or education level.
 
3. HIMSSON Supports Multiple Organizational Models
HIMSSON will support both:
Model A — Multiple Education Levels Within One School
ORGANIZATION
└── MANKO INTERNATIONAL SCHOOL
    ├── Primary
    ├── Secondary
    └── Advanced
And:
Model B — Separate Schools for Different Education Levels
ORGANIZATION
├── MANKO PRIMARY SCHOOL
├── MANKO SECONDARY SCHOOL
└── MANKO HIGH SCHOOL
And therefore, most importantly:
Model C — Mixed Organizational Structure
ORGANIZATION
│
├── SCHOOL A
│   ├── Primary
│   └── Secondary
│
├── SCHOOL B
│   └── Advanced
│
└── SCHOOL C
    └── Primary
Model C is the HIMSSON standard.
The architecture must be capable of representing all three situations.
 
4. Education Level Is Not Automatically a School
This is a critical architectural distinction.
HIMSSON must not assume:
Primary = School
Secondary = School
Advanced = School
Instead:
Education Level is a configurable structural dimension within a School.
A School may support:
•	Primary only;
•	Secondary only;
•	Advanced only;
•	Primary + Secondary;
•	Secondary + Advanced;
•	Primary + Secondary + Advanced;
•	or another valid combination supported by the platform.
Whether an education level is represented as part of an existing School or as its own School depends on the institution's actual organizational structure.
 
5. Example — One School With Multiple Levels
An institution may operate under one school identity:
MANKO INTERNATIONAL SCHOOL

Education Levels:
├── Primary
├── Secondary
└── Advanced
In this case, the three levels operate inside one School context.
They may still have different:
•	classes/forms;
•	subjects;
•	teachers;
•	fee structures;
•	academic rules;
•	reporting requirements.
The fact that they belong to one School does not require them to use identical academic structures.
 
6. Example — Separate Schools
Another organization may operate:
MANKO EDUCATION GROUP
│
├── MANKO PRIMARY SCHOOL
├── MANKO SECONDARY SCHOOL
└── MANKO HIGH SCHOOL
Each is a separate School.
Each maintains its own operational environment.
The organization-level owner/director may have authorized access to all three.
 
7. School Independence
The following principle from HIMSSON 0.4 remains locked:
Schools remain operationally independent even when they belong to the same Organization.
Therefore, School A must not automatically access or inherit School B's:
•	students;
•	attendance;
•	marks;
•	payments;
•	balances;
•	documents;
•	staff records;
•	reports;
•	operational settings.
An organization-level user may have access to multiple schools, but that access is an authorization decision, not automatic data merging.
 
8. School Context
Every school-dependent operational record should ultimately have a clear school context.
Conceptually:
Organization
      ↓
School
      ↓
Operational Record
Examples:
School
 └── Student

School
 └── Fee Structure

School
 └── Payment

School
 └── Attendance Record

School
 └── Mark

School
 └── Document
This provides the foundation for strict school-level data isolation.
 
9. Education-Level Context
Where a School supports multiple education levels, relevant academic records may additionally require an education-level context.
Conceptually:
School
   ↓
Education Level
   ↓
Academic Structure
   ↓
Student / Class / Subject / Record
For example:
MANKO INTERNATIONAL SCHOOL
│
├── Primary
│   ├── Standard 1
│   ├── Standard 2
│   └── ...
│
├── Secondary
│   ├── Form I
│   ├── Form II
│   └── ...
│
└── Advanced
    ├── Form V
    └── Form VI
The exact academic hierarchy is intentionally not defined in this document.
 
10. Academic Years Are School-Contextual
Academic years belong to the operational context of a School.
For example:
MANKO PRIMARY SCHOOL
└── Academic Year 2027

MANKO SECONDARY SCHOOL
└── Academic Year 2027
Even if two schools use the same calendar year, their academic records remain independent.
The exact academic-year configuration and period model will be defined later.
 
11. Classes, Forms and Streams Must Remain Flexible
HIMSSON must support different educational structures.
Examples may include:
Primary
Standard 1
Standard 2
Standard 3
...
Secondary
Form I
Form II
Form III
...
Advanced
Form V
Form VI
Schools may additionally use streams such as:
A
B
C
or:
Science
Arts
Commerce
The architecture must not hard-code one naming convention.
The exact class/form/stream model will be defined in a subsequent architectural decision.
 
12. Campus / Branch
Campus or Branch is recognized as a potentially important future structural layer.
Possible structures include:
Organization
└── School
    ├── Main Campus
    └── West Campus
or:
Organization
├── School A
│   ├── Campus 1
│   └── Campus 2
└── School B
However, HIMSSON will not require every School to have a Campus/Branch layer.
Campus/Branch support will therefore be designed so that it can be introduced without forcing unnecessary complexity on schools that operate from one location.
 
13. School Relationships
Two Schools within the same Organization may eventually have defined relationships.
Examples could include:
•	student progression;
•	transfer;
•	shared organizational oversight;
•	shared documents;
•	cross-school reporting;
•	staff relationships;
•	parent relationships.
However:
A relationship does not mean automatic data sharing.
Any cross-school access or sharing must be explicitly defined and authorized.
 
14. School as a Security Boundary
The School is a fundamental authorization boundary.
A user accessing:
School A
must not automatically receive access to:
School B
simply because both schools belong to the same Organization.
The eventual authorization model must account for:
Identity + Role + Scope + Permissions
as established in HIMSSON 0.4.
 
15. School Configuration
A School should eventually be able to maintain its own configuration.
Potential configuration areas include:
•	school identity;
•	branding;
•	contact information;
•	education levels;
•	academic calendar;
•	fee structures;
•	grading configuration;
•	communication settings;
•	document requirements;
•	operational settings.
The exact configuration model will be defined later.
 
16. What This Decision Does Not Define
To avoid premature architecture, the following remain intentionally open:
•	exact campus/branch architecture;
•	exact education-level data model;
•	exact academic-year model;
•	number and structure of terms;
•	class/form hierarchy;
•	stream hierarchy;
•	subject hierarchy;
•	examination structure;
•	student progression;
•	student transfers;
•	shared teachers;
•	cross-school parent workflows;
•	cross-school student relationships;
•	school registration fields;
•	school codes and numbering conventions.
These will be addressed in subsequent decisions.
 
17. Locked Principles
The following are now locked:
0.5-A
A School is an independently operated educational environment within an Organization.
0.5-B
HIMSSON supports Organizations containing one or multiple Schools.
0.5-C
A School may contain one or multiple education levels.
0.5-D
Education Level is not automatically equivalent to School.
0.5-E
HIMSSON supports separate Schools for different education levels.
0.5-F
HIMSSON also supports multiple education levels within one School.
0.5-G
HIMSSON therefore supports the mixed Model C structure.
0.5-H
Schools remain operationally independent even when owned by the same Organization.
0.5-I
School-dependent records must ultimately be scoped to the correct School.
0.5-J
Academic structures must support Primary, Secondary, and Advanced education without hard-coding one model.
0.5-K
Campus/Branch is a potential future structural layer but is not mandatory for every School.
0.5-L
Cross-school relationships do not automatically grant cross-school data access.
 
18. Final Decision
HIMSSON adopts Model C: Flexible Mixed School Structure.
The canonical conceptual model is:
ORGANIZATION
│
├── SCHOOL
│   ├── EDUCATION LEVEL(S)
│   ├── ACADEMIC STRUCTURE
│   ├── PEOPLE
│   ├── FINANCE
│   ├── ACADEMICS
│   └── OPERATIONS
│
├── SCHOOL
│   └── EDUCATION LEVEL(S)
│
└── SCHOOL
    └── EDUCATION LEVEL(S)
This allows HIMSSON to represent real-world private-school organizations without forcing them into an artificial structure.
 
STATUS: LOCKED — HIMSSON 0.5
 
HIMSSON 0.6-A — Class/Form, Stream & Advanced-Level Combination Model
Status: LOCKED
Document Type: Product Architecture / Academic Structure
Parent Section: HIMSSON 0.6 — Academic Structure
Applies To: Private Primary, Secondary, and Advanced-Level Schools in Tanzania
 
1. Purpose
HIMSSON must support the academic structures of Tanzanian private schools without forcing Primary, Secondary, and Advanced-level education into the same visible hierarchy.
Primary, Secondary, and Advanced-level schools share common academic concepts, but their operational structures differ.
In particular, Advanced-level education may organize students through subject combinations such as:
•	PCM — Physics, Chemistry, Mathematics
•	PCB — Physics, Chemistry, Biology
•	CBG — Chemistry, Biology, Geography
•	HGL — History, Geography, Language
Therefore, HIMSSON must distinguish between:
•	the student's academic group;
•	an optional stream;
•	an optional subject combination;
•	the actual subjects assigned to the student.
The architecture must remain flexible enough to support all three education levels without creating separate incompatible academic systems.
 
2. Core Architectural Decision
HIMSSON will use a flexible Academic Group concept as the primary cohort/class structure.
An Academic Group may represent concepts such as:
•	Standard 1
•	Standard 4
•	Standard 7
•	Form I
•	Form II
•	Form IV
•	Form V
•	Form VI
The displayed terminology may vary according to the school's education level and configuration, while the underlying architecture remains consistent.
The conceptual structure is:
Academic Group
    ├── optional Stream
    └── optional Subject Combination
Neither Stream nor Subject Combination is mandatory for every Academic Group.
 
3. Academic Group
An Academic Group represents the primary academic cohort to which students belong during an academic period/year.
Examples:
Primary
└── Standard 4
Secondary
└── Form II
Advanced
└── Form V
The Academic Group must not be hard-coded to Primary terminology.
The same underlying concept must support:
•	Standard
•	Grade
•	Form
•	other school-configured academic group labels where required.
This prevents the architecture from becoming Primary-specific.
 
4. Stream
A Stream is an optional subdivision of an Academic Group.
Examples:
Standard 4
├── Stream A
└── Stream B
or:
Form II
├── Stream A
├── Stream B
└── Stream C
A school may operate an Academic Group without streams:
Form IV
or with streams:
Form IV
├── Stream A
└── Stream B
Therefore:
Stream is optional and must never be assumed to exist.
 
5. Subject Combination
A Subject Combination is a separate academic concept from Stream.
It represents a defined set of subjects that normally belong together for an academic pathway.
Example:
Subject Combination: PCM

├── Physics
├── Chemistry
└── Mathematics
Another:
Subject Combination: PCB

├── Physics
├── Chemistry
└── Biology
Subject combinations are particularly important for Advanced-level education but the architecture must not technically prevent future use in other contexts.
 
6. Combination Is Not Student Identity
A Subject Combination is a reusable school configuration.
It is not a permanent property of a student.
The correct relationship is:
Student
    ↓
Academic Enrollment
    ↓
Subject Combination
Not:
Student
    ↓
Permanent Combination
This distinction is essential because a student's academic placement can change between academic years or periods.
For example:
2027
Form V
PCM
may later become:
2028
Form VI
PCM
while another student may move to a different academic pathway according to the school's rules.
 
7. Combination Configuration
Subject combinations are school-configurable.
A school may define:
PCM
├── Physics
├── Chemistry
└── Mathematics
PCB
├── Physics
├── Chemistry
└── Biology
HGL
├── History
├── Geography
└── Language
The system must not hard-code a fixed national list of combinations.
Each school's configuration may determine:
•	combination name;
•	combination code;
•	subjects included;
•	education level;
•	applicable academic groups;
•	active/inactive status;
•	academic-year applicability where required.
 
8. Stream and Combination May Coexist
HIMSSON must support schools where both Stream and Subject Combination are used.
For example:
Form V
├── PCM
│   ├── Stream A
│   └── Stream B
│
└── PCB
    └── Stream A
A student could therefore have:
Student
├── Education Level: Advanced
├── Academic Year: 2027
├── Academic Group: Form V
├── Combination: PCM
└── Stream: A
However, neither Stream nor Combination should be required universally.
 
9. Primary Structure
Primary education may use a simple Academic Group + optional Stream model.
Example:
Primary
└── Standard 4
    ├── Stream A
    └── Stream B
Student placement:
Amani
├── Education Level: Primary
├── Academic Year: 2027
├── Academic Group: Standard 4
└── Stream: A
A Primary student normally does not require a Subject Combination.
 
10. Secondary Structure
Secondary education may similarly use Academic Group + optional Stream.
Example:
Secondary
└── Form II
    ├── Stream A
    └── Stream B
Student placement:
Amani
├── Education Level: Secondary
├── Academic Year: 2027
├── Academic Group: Form II
└── Stream: B
A Subject Combination is not automatically required merely because the student is in Secondary.
 
11. Advanced-Level Structure
Advanced-level education must support Subject Combination as a first-class concept.
Example:
Advanced
└── Form V
    ├── PCM
    ├── PCB
    └── HGL
Student placement:
Amani
├── Education Level: Advanced
├── Academic Year: 2027
├── Academic Group: Form V
└── Subject Combination: PCM
Where the school additionally uses streams:
Amani
├── Education Level: Advanced
├── Academic Year: 2027
├── Academic Group: Form V
├── Subject Combination: PCM
└── Stream: A
 
12. Subject Catalogue vs Subject Offering
HIMSSON must distinguish between a school's general subject catalogue and the actual teaching context.
Subject Catalogue
A Subject represents a subject known to the school.
Examples:
•	Mathematics
•	English
•	Physics
•	Chemistry
•	Biology
•	History
•	Geography
The existence of a Subject does not automatically mean it is being taught to every student.
Subject Offering
A Subject Offering represents the actual teaching instance of a subject within an academic context.
Conceptually:
Subject
    ↓
Subject Offering
    ├── Academic Year
    ├── Academic Period
    ├── Education Level
    ├── Academic Group
    ├── Combination (optional)
    └── Teacher Assignment
This allows HIMSSON to distinguish:
"The school offers Physics"
from:
"Physics is being taught to Form V PCM during this academic year/period and is assigned to Teacher X."
This distinction is important for marks, attendance, teacher assignment, reporting, and academic history.
 
13. Student Academic Enrollment
A student's academic placement must be represented through an Academic Enrollment rather than by permanently attaching the student to a class, stream, or combination.
Conceptually:
Student
└── Academic Enrollment
    ├── School
    ├── Academic Year
    ├── Academic Period / Enrollment Context
    ├── Education Level
    ├── Academic Group
    ├── Stream (optional)
    └── Subject Combination (optional)
This allows HIMSSON to preserve academic history.
Example:
2026
Standard 3
Stream A
followed by:
2027
Standard 4
Stream B
and later:
2038
Form V
PCM
Each placement remains historically meaningful.
 
14. Subject Selection Rule
HIMSSON will use inheritance from the academic structure as the default subject-selection mechanism, with controlled individual student overrides.
The normal flow is:
Academic Structure
        ↓
Subject Combination / Academic Group
        ↓
Defined Subjects
        ↓
Student Academic Enrollment
        ↓
Student Subjects
In practical terms:
PCM
├── Physics
├── Chemistry
└── Mathematics
        ↓
Student enrolled in PCM
        ↓
Student receives:
Physics
Chemistry
Mathematics
The system should not require administrators to manually assign the same subjects to every student when the academic structure already defines them.
 
15. Controlled Individual Subject Overrides
HIMSSON must nevertheless allow controlled individual subject enrollment where the school explicitly permits an exception.
Example:
Standard Combination
PCM
├── Physics
├── Chemistry
└── Mathematics
A particular student may have an approved academic exception.
The system may therefore support:
Student
├── inherited subjects
│   ├── Physics
│   ├── Chemistry
│   └── Mathematics
│
└── approved individual override
The override must affect the individual student's academic enrollment only.
It must not modify the shared PCM combination.
This distinction is critical.
Incorrect behavior
Changing one student's subjects modifies PCM for every student.
Correct behavior
The student's enrollment contains an explicit individual override while the shared PCM definition remains unchanged.
 
16. Override Governance
Individual subject overrides should not become an uncontrolled second academic structure.
Therefore:
•	inherited subjects are the default;
•	overrides are exceptional;
•	overrides belong to the student's enrollment/context;
•	overrides do not alter the shared combination;
•	the system should retain enough information to identify that a subject was individually added, removed, or otherwise overridden;
•	where school policy requires approval, the system should support an approval/status concept rather than silently changing the academic structure.
The exact approval workflow will be defined in a later academic-enrollment phase.
 
17. Canonical Academic Placement Model
The canonical HIMSSON placement model is:
Student
    ↓
Academic Enrollment
    ├── School
    ├── Academic Year
    ├── Education Level
    ├── Academic Group
    ├── Stream (optional)
    ├── Subject Combination (optional)
    └── Individual Subject Overrides (optional)
This model supports:
Primary
Student
└── Standard 4
    └── Stream A
Secondary
Student
└── Form II
    └── Stream B
Advanced
Student
└── Form V
    └── PCM
Advanced + Stream
Student
└── Form V
    ├── PCM
    └── Stream A
Advanced + Individual Override
Student
└── Form V
    ├── PCM
    ├── Stream A
    └── Approved Subject Override
 
18. Architectural Principles Locked by 0.6-A
The following principles are now authoritative:
1.	HIMSSON uses a flexible Academic Group concept.
2.	Academic Group supports Standard, Grade, Form, and other configured terminology.
3.	Display terminology may vary while the underlying architecture remains consistent.
4.	Stream is an optional subdivision of an Academic Group.
5.	Subject Combination is separate from Stream.
6.	Subject Combination is optional.
7.	Subject Combination is especially important for Advanced-level education.
8.	Subject Combination is a reusable school configuration.
9.	Subject Combination is not a permanent student attribute.
10.	A student references a combination through Academic Enrollment.
11.	Combinations are school-configurable.
12.	The same combination may exist across multiple academic years.
13.	Stream and Subject Combination may coexist.
14.	Subject Catalogue and Subject Offering are separate concepts.
15.	Student academic placement is represented through Academic Enrollment.
16.	Subject assignment normally inherits from the academic structure.
17.	Individual student subject overrides are permitted where the school explicitly allows them.
18.	Individual overrides must not modify the shared academic structure.
19.	HIMSSON must support Primary, Secondary, and Advanced without forcing one visible hierarchy.
20.	Academic architecture must remain reusable across schools and organizations.
 
19. Architectural Consequence
This decision prevents HIMSSON from becoming a Primary-school system with Secondary and Advanced features patched on later.
Instead, the platform has one flexible academic architecture capable of representing:
PRIMARY
Standard / Grade
    ↓
Optional Stream
SECONDARY
Form
    ↓
Optional Stream
ADVANCED
Form
    ↓
Subject Combination
    ↓
Optional Stream
while retaining a common underlying model:
School
    ↓
Education Level
    ↓
Academic Year
    ↓
Academic Group
    ├── Stream (optional)
    ├── Subject Combination (optional)
    └── Subject Offerings
            ↓
Student Academic Enrollment
            ↓
Student Subjects
            ↓
Marks / Attendance / Results / Academic History
 
20. Status
HIMSSON 0.6-A is LOCKED.
Any future implementation must follow this model.
If a later requirement appears to conflict with this architecture, the requirement must be reviewed against the locked model rather than solved through an isolated UI or database patch.
Changes to this architecture require an explicit reopen → evaluate → revise → relock process.
 
HIMSSON 0.6-B — Student Enrollment, Promotion, Transfer & Academic History
Status: LOCKED
Document Type: Product Architecture / Student Academic Lifecycle
Parent Section: HIMSSON 0.6 — Academic Structure
Applies To: Private Primary, Secondary, and Advanced-Level Schools in Tanzania
 
1. Purpose
HIMSSON must represent not only where a student is currently studying, but also the student's complete academic journey through the platform.
A student may:
•	enter a school;
•	progress through academic groups;
•	change streams;
•	change subject combinations;
•	move between education levels;
•	transfer between schools;
•	leave and later return;
•	complete or graduate;
•	or withdraw.
The architecture must preserve these events without destroying historical information.
The foundational rule is:
A student's current academic placement must never overwrite the student's historical academic placement.
 
2. Student vs Academic Enrollment
HIMSSON separates the Student from the student's Academic Enrollment.
Student
The Student represents the person.
Examples of persistent student information may include:
•	Student ID / admission number;
•	name;
•	date of birth;
•	gender where required;
•	contact information;
•	parent/guardian relationships;
•	identification information;
•	documents;
•	emergency information;
•	general student information.
Academic Enrollment
Academic Enrollment represents the student's participation in a particular school and academic context.
Conceptually:
Student
    ↓
Academic Enrollment
    ├── Organization
    ├── School
    ├── Academic Year
    ├── Education Level
    ├── Academic Group
    ├── Stream (optional)
    ├── Subject Combination (optional)
    ├── Subjects / Overrides
    └── Enrollment Status
A student may therefore have multiple historical enrollments.
 
3. Enrollment Is Time-Bound
An Academic Enrollment belongs to a specific academic context.
Example:
Enrollment 001
Academic Year: 2026
School: MANKO Private School
Level: Primary
Group: Standard 3
Stream: A
Status: Completed
followed by:
Enrollment 002
Academic Year: 2027
School: MANKO Private School
Level: Primary
Group: Standard 4
Stream: B
Status: Active
The Student record remains the same.
The Academic Enrollment changes.
 
4. Current Enrollment and Historical Enrollment
HIMSSON must distinguish between:
•	the student's current active enrollment;
•	previous completed enrollments;
•	future/planned enrollments where applicable;
•	withdrawn enrollments;
•	transferred enrollments;
•	completed/graduated enrollments.
Example:
Student
│
├── 2024 Enrollment — Completed
├── 2025 Enrollment — Completed
├── 2026 Enrollment — Completed
└── 2027 Enrollment — Active
Academic history must be represented by actual enrollment records rather than reconstructed solely from audit logs.
 
5. Enrollment Status
HIMSSON will use controlled enrollment states rather than relying on free-text descriptions.
The conceptual lifecycle includes:
ADMITTED
   ↓
ENROLLED
   ↓
ACTIVE
   ↓
COMPLETED / PROMOTED / GRADUATED
Alternative outcomes may include:
ACTIVE
 ├── TRANSFERRED
 ├── WITHDRAWN
 └── OTHER AUTHORIZED ADMINISTRATIVE OUTCOME
The exact status taxonomy may be refined during the detailed student lifecycle implementation phase.
 
6. Student Status vs Enrollment Status
HIMSSON must not overload a single status field with unrelated concepts.
A Student represents the person.
An Enrollment represents the student's participation in a particular school/academic context.
For example:
Student
│
├── Enrollment A → Completed
├── Enrollment B → Completed
└── Enrollment C → Transferred
The student record remains available even after an individual enrollment ends.
This distinction is essential for academic history and records retention.
 
7. Promotion
Promotion is treated as the creation of a new Academic Enrollment.
Example:
2026
Standard 3
Stream A
        ↓
     PROMOTION
        ↓
2027
Standard 4
Stream A
The 2026 enrollment remains intact.
The 2027 enrollment becomes the student's new active enrollment.
HIMSSON must not convert the historical Standard 3 record into Standard 4.
 
8. Promotion Is a Decision, Not Simply a Number Increment
HIMSSON must not hard-code academic progression as:
Standard 3 → Standard 4
Form I → Form II
Form V → Form VI
as the only possible progression mechanism.
Schools may have:
•	different structures;
•	repeat-year policies;
•	special placement rules;
•	remedial placement;
•	exceptional promotion;
•	different education-level transitions.
The system may recommend a likely next Academic Group, but authorized school personnel must be able to approve or modify the actual placement according to school policy.
 
9. Promotion Preserves Academic Context
A promotion operation may change:
•	Academic Year;
•	Education Level;
•	Academic Group;
•	Stream;
•	Subject Combination;
•	applicable subject structure.
Example:
2027
Form IV
Stream B
        ↓
PROMOTION
        ↓
2028
Form V
Combination: PCM
The previous enrollment remains historically intact.
 
10. Advanced-Level Promotion
Advanced-level progression requires particular flexibility.
A student may move:
Form V → Form VI
while retaining:
PCM → PCM
But HIMSSON must also technically support an authorized combination change:
Form V
PCM

        ↓

Form VI
PCB
The previous combination remains attached to the historical enrollment.
The new combination belongs to the new enrollment.
 
11. Internal School Transfer
HIMSSON must support transfer between schools belonging to the same Organization.
Example:
MANKO Organization
│
├── MANKO Primary School
│
└── MANKO Secondary School
A student may move from:
School A
Standard 7
to:
School B
Form I
The correct architecture is:
ONE STUDENT
│
├── School A Enrollment
│       ↓
│    Transfer Out
│
└── School B Enrollment
        ↓
      Active
The old enrollment is not edited to become the new School B enrollment.
 
12. Agreed Cross-School Identity Model
When a student moves between schools within the same Organization, HIMSSON will maintain one Student identity where the system has established that the records represent the same person.
The student may therefore have:
Student
│
├── School A Enrollment
└── School B Enrollment
rather than unnecessarily creating:
Student A → School A

Student B → School B
for the same person.
This prevents unnecessary duplicate identities within the Organization.
However, identity matching must not rely solely on names.
The detailed identity-matching and merge rules will be defined in a future identity/data-governance phase.
 
13. School Operational Independence
Maintaining one student identity does not merge the schools.
School A and School B remain separate operational environments.
Therefore:
Organization
│
├── School A
│   └── Student Enrollment A
│
└── School B
    └── Student Enrollment B
The existence of the same Student identity does not automatically expose School A's operational records to School B.
This follows the locked HIMSSON 0.4 principle:
Cross-school relationships do not automatically grant cross-school data access.
 
14. Cross-School Transfer Security
When a student transfers between schools:
•	the source school's historical enrollment remains scoped to the source school;
•	the destination school's enrollment is scoped to the destination school;
•	authorization remains school-aware;
•	historical school records are not automatically exposed;
•	any future cross-school sharing must be explicitly authorized.
This prevents a transfer mechanism from accidentally becoming a cross-school data-access mechanism.
 
15. External Transfer-Out
HIMSSON must also support a student leaving the Organization for another institution.
Example:
HIMSSON School
      ↓
External Transfer
      ↓
Another School
The HIMSSON enrollment becomes appropriately closed with a transfer outcome.
The Student record is not deleted merely because the student leaves.
HIMSSON does not create an operational enrollment for the external institution because that institution is outside the Organization's platform scope.
 
16. Transfer-In
A school must be able to enroll a student who previously attended another institution.
Example:
External School
      ↓
Transfer-In
      ↓
HIMSSON School
      ↓
Form II / Stream A
The HIMSSON school creates its own Academic Enrollment.
Previous-school information may be recorded as relevant, but HIMSSON must not pretend that it possesses the complete operational history of an external institution.
 
17. Withdrawal
Withdrawal closes the student's current enrollment without deleting the historical record.
Example:
2027
Form II
Stream A
Active

        ↓
     WITHDRAWAL
        ↓

2027
Form II
Stream A
Withdrawn
The system should retain relevant withdrawal information such as:
•	effective date;
•	withdrawal category/reason;
•	authorized by;
•	notes where appropriate.
The exact withdrawal taxonomy may be defined later.
 
18. Re-Enrollment
A student who previously left a school and later returns should receive a new Academic Enrollment.
Example:
2025
Form II
Withdrawn

        ↓
     Student leaves

        ↓
     Student returns

        ↓

2028
Form V
Active
HIMSSON must not erase the gap in the student's history by simply reactivating an old historical enrollment.
 
19. Academic History
Academic History must be reconstructable from the student's historical enrollment records and associated academic records.
Example:
Student
│
├── 2024
│   └── Standard 2 / Stream A
│
├── 2025
│   └── Standard 3 / Stream A
│
├── 2026
│   └── Standard 4 / Stream B
│
└── 2027
    └── Standard 5 / Stream B
Advanced example:
Student
│
├── 2030
│   └── Form IV
│
├── 2031
│   └── Form V / PCM
│
└── 2032
    └── Form VI / PCM
This history will later support:
•	student profiles;
•	report cards;
•	transcripts;
•	transfer documentation;
•	graduation records;
•	academic analytics;
•	parent/student views.
 
20. Academic Results Must Retain Enrollment Context
Historical marks and results must not depend on the student's current class.
The correct relationship is:
Student
   ↓
Academic Enrollment
   ↓
Academic Context
   ↓
Subject Enrollment / Offering
   ↓
Assessment
   ↓
Marks
   ↓
Results
Therefore, when HIMSSON retrieves a student's historical result, it must know the academic context in which that result was produced.
For example:
A student's 2026 Standard 4 result must remain a Standard 4 result even after the student becomes a Standard 5 student in 2027.
 
21. Financial Records Must Also Preserve Historical Context
Academic placement must not retroactively alter historical financial records.
For example:
2026
Standard 4
Fee Structure A
must remain historically associated with the relevant 2026 financial records even after the student becomes:
2027
Standard 5
Fee Structure B
Changing the student's current Academic Enrollment must never rewrite the financial history of the previous enrollment.
This maintains the separation between current academic state and historical financial truth.
 
22. Enrollment Change vs Data Correction
HIMSSON must distinguish between an actual lifecycle event and an administrative correction.
Actual academic event
A student genuinely moves:
Standard 3 → Standard 4
This creates a new enrollment.
Administrative correction
An administrator entered:
Stream A
when the student was actually:
Stream B
This is a data correction to the relevant enrollment record, subject to authorization and audit controls.
The system must not create fake promotion or transfer events merely to correct inaccurate data.
 
23. Authorized Enrollment Changes
Academic enrollment changes are sensitive administrative actions.
The architecture therefore follows:
Identity
   +
Role
   +
Scope
   +
Permission
        ↓
Authorized Enrollment Action
A user who can view a student does not automatically have authority to:
•	promote the student;
•	transfer the student;
•	change the student's school;
•	change the student's combination;
•	withdraw the student.
The detailed role-permission matrix will be defined in a later authorization phase.
 
24. Bulk Promotion
Schools may need to promote large numbers of students simultaneously.
HIMSSON should therefore eventually support controlled Bulk Promotion.
Example:
2026
Standard 3
       ↓
PROMOTE SELECTED STUDENTS
       ↓
2027
Standard 4
The system must create new enrollments rather than overwrite the existing records.
Bulk promotion should eventually support:
•	selection;
•	preview;
•	validation;
•	exception detection;
•	confirmation;
•	execution;
•	result summary;
•	audit trail.
Example result:
100 students selected

96 eligible
2 missing required information
1 already promoted
1 blocked by enrollment conflict
 
25. Promotion Exceptions
Students within the same Academic Group may not all follow the same path.
For example:
Standard 3
100 students

95 → Promoted to Standard 4
3  → Repeat Standard 3
1  → Transferred
1  → Withdrawn
HIMSSON must support these different outcomes without forcing every student into the same destination.
 
26. No Silent Enrollment Overwrites
This is a hard architectural rule.
If a real academic lifecycle event occurs, HIMSSON must not perform:
Old Enrollment
Standard 3
      ↓
EDIT
      ↓
Standard 4
Instead:
Old Enrollment
Standard 3
      ↓
Completed / Promoted
      ↓
New Enrollment
Standard 4
The historical record remains intact.
 
27. Canonical Student Academic Lifecycle
The resulting lifecycle is:
STUDENT RECORD
      │
      ↓
ADMISSION / ENROLLMENT
      │
      ↓
ACTIVE ACADEMIC ENROLLMENT
      │
      ├───────────────┐
      │               │
      ↓               ↓
  PROMOTION       TRANSFER
      │               │
      ↓               ↓
NEW ENROLLMENT   NEW ENROLLMENT
      │
      ↓
CONTINUED STUDY
      │
      ├── WITHDRAWAL
      ├── TRANSFER
      ├── COMPLETION
      └── GRADUATION
The Student identity remains distinct from the enrollment lifecycle.
 
28. Canonical Architecture
Combining HIMSSON 0.4, 0.5, 0.6-A and 0.6-B:
ORGANIZATION
      │
      ├── SCHOOL A
      │      │
      │      ├── EDUCATION LEVEL
      │      │
      │      ├── ACADEMIC YEAR
      │      │
      │      └── ACADEMIC STRUCTURE
      │
      └── SCHOOL B
             │
             └── ACADEMIC STRUCTURE


STUDENT
   │
   ├── Enrollment — School A
   │      ├── Academic Year
   │      ├── Education Level
   │      ├── Academic Group
   │      ├── Stream
   │      └── Combination
   │
   └── Enrollment — School B
          ├── Academic Year
          ├── Education Level
          ├── Academic Group
          ├── Stream
          └── Combination
This provides one coherent foundation for the entire student lifecycle.
 
29. Locked Architectural Principles
The following principles are now authoritative:
1.	Student and Academic Enrollment are separate concepts.
2.	A student may have multiple Academic Enrollment records.
3.	Academic Enrollment is time-bound and context-specific.
4.	Current placement must never overwrite historical placement.
5.	Promotion creates a new enrollment.
6.	Promotion is not hard-coded as simple numeric progression.
7.	Promotion may change Education Level, Academic Group, Stream, or Subject Combination where authorized.
8.	Advanced-level promotion supports both combination continuity and authorized combination changes.
9.	Internal school transfer creates a new enrollment.
10.	The historical enrollment remains attached to its original school.
11.	One Student identity may persist across schools within the same Organization when the system establishes that the records represent the same person.
12.	Maintaining one Student identity does not merge school operational data.
13.	Cross-school relationships do not automatically grant cross-school data access.
14.	External transfer-out closes the relevant HIMSSON enrollment appropriately.
15.	Transfer-in creates a new HIMSSON enrollment.
16.	Withdrawal preserves the historical enrollment.
17.	Re-enrollment creates a new enrollment.
18.	Academic History is built from historical enrollment records and associated academic records.
19.	Historical academic results retain their original enrollment context.
20.	Historical financial records retain their original academic/financial context.
21.	Real enrollment events must be distinguished from administrative data corrections.
22.	Enrollment changes are permission-controlled.
23.	Bulk promotion creates new enrollments rather than overwriting historical records.
24.	Bulk promotion must support validation and exceptions.
25.	No silent enrollment overwrites are permitted.
26.	Student lifecycle and enrollment lifecycle must not be collapsed into one overloaded status field.
27.	Cross-school identity matching must not rely solely on names.
28.	Detailed identity matching, merge rules, permission matrices, transfer workflows, and retention policies will be defined in later architecture phases.
 
30. Status
HIMSSON 0.6-B — Student Enrollment, Promotion, Transfer & Academic History is LOCKED.
This section is now part of the authoritative HIMSSON architecture.
Any implementation must preserve the distinction between:
STUDENT IDENTITY
        ↓
ACADEMIC ENROLLMENT
        ↓
ACADEMIC HISTORY
and must never solve lifecycle changes by overwriting historical enrollment data.
Future changes to this architecture require an explicit:
REOPEN → EVALUATE → REVISE → DOCUMENT → RELOCK
process.
 
HIMSSON 0.6-C — Subjects, Subject Enrollment, Teaching Assignments & Assessment Structure
Status: LOCKED
Document Type: Product Architecture / Academic Structure
Parent Section: HIMSSON 0.6 — Academic Structure
Depends On: HIMSSON 0.6-A and 0.6-B
Applies To: Private Primary, Secondary, and Advanced-Level Schools in Tanzania
 
1. Purpose
HIMSSON must define a structured relationship between:
•	subjects offered by a school;
•	the actual academic context in which a subject is taught;
•	the students who study the subject;
•	teachers assigned to teach it;
•	assessments conducted for the subject;
•	and marks produced from those assessments.
The architecture must support Primary, Secondary, and Advanced-level education without assuming that every school teaches subjects in the same way.
The core academic chain is:
Subject
    ↓
Subject Offering
    ↓
Teacher Assignment
    ↓
Assessment
    ↓
Student Mark
    ↓
Validated Result
    ↓
Report Card
The student connects to this chain through:
Student
    ↓
Academic Enrollment
    ↓
Subject Enrollment
    ↓
Subject Offering
 
2. Subject vs Subject Offering
HIMSSON must distinguish between a Subject and a Subject Offering.
Subject
A Subject is a reusable catalogue entity representing a subject known to the school.
Examples:
•	Mathematics
•	English
•	Physics
•	Chemistry
•	Biology
•	Geography
•	History
Subject Offering
A Subject Offering represents the actual deployment of that subject within a specific academic context.
Conceptually:
Subject Offering
├── School
├── Academic Year
├── Academic Period
├── Education Level
├── Academic Group
├── Stream (optional)
├── Subject Combination (optional)
└── Subject
Therefore:
"Mathematics exists in the school"
is different from:
"Mathematics is being taught to Form II Stream A during the 2027 academic year."
 
3. Subject Catalogue
Each school maintains its own Subject Catalogue.
Example:
SCHOOL
│
└── SUBJECT CATALOGUE
    ├── Mathematics
    ├── English
    ├── Physics
    ├── Chemistry
    ├── Biology
    └── Geography
Subject records should use structured identities rather than arbitrary text entered independently throughout the application.
Potential subject information may include:
•	subject name;
•	subject code;
•	display name;
•	education-level applicability;
•	active/inactive state;
•	other school-defined metadata.
The exact master-data field specification may be refined during implementation.
 
4. Subject Reuse
A Subject may be reused across multiple academic years.
Example:
Mathematics
│
├── 2026
│   ├── Standard 4 Offering
│   └── Standard 5 Offering
│
├── 2027
│   ├── Standard 4 Offering
│   └── Standard 5 Offering
│
└── 2028
    └── ...
HIMSSON must not create an unrelated new Subject entity merely because the teaching context changes.
The Subject is the reusable catalogue concept.
The Subject Offering represents its actual academic deployment.
 
5. Subject Offering
A Subject Offering represents the actual teaching instance of a subject.
Conceptually:
Subject Offering
├── School
├── Academic Year
├── Academic Period
├── Education Level
├── Academic Group
├── Stream (optional)
├── Subject Combination (optional)
└── Subject
This allows HIMSSON to distinguish multiple offerings of the same subject.
Example:
Mathematics
may produce:
2027
├── Standard 4 → Mathematics → Teacher A
├── Standard 5 → Mathematics → Teacher B
├── Form I → Mathematics → Teacher C
└── Form II → Mathematics → Teacher D
The school still has one Mathematics subject.
It has multiple Subject Offerings.
 
6. Subject Enrollment
Subject Enrollment is a first-class persistent academic record.
It represents a particular student's participation in a subject within an Academic Enrollment.
The canonical relationship is:
Student
    ↓
Academic Enrollment
    ↓
Subject Enrollment
    ↓
Subject Offering
Example:
Amani
 ↓
Form V / PCM
 ↓
Subject Enrollment
 ├── Physics
 ├── Chemistry
 └── Mathematics
This creates an explicit historical boundary between:
•	what the school offers;
•	what the student's academic structure provides;
•	and what the individual student actually studies.
 
7. Why Subject Enrollment Is Persistent
Subject Enrollment must remain a real record because a student's subject participation may change.
Possible events include:
•	subject change;
•	approved individual override;
•	transfer;
•	combination change;
•	subject withdrawal;
•	academic progression;
•	exceptional subject placement.
Without a persistent Subject Enrollment record, HIMSSON would be forced to infer historical subject participation from current configuration.
That would be unsafe.
 
8. Subject Inheritance
Subject assignment normally follows the academic structure.
The standard flow is:
Academic Group / Combination
        ↓
Defined Subjects
        ↓
Student Academic Enrollment
        ↓
Subject Enrollment
Example:
PCM
├── Physics
├── Chemistry
└── Mathematics
A student enrolled in PCM normally receives Subject Enrollment records for:
•	Physics;
•	Chemistry;
•	Mathematics.
Administrators should not have to manually recreate the same subject structure for every student.
 
9. Individual Subject Overrides
HIMSSON supports controlled individual subject overrides.
The model is:
Academic Structure
      ↓
Inherited Subjects
      ↓
Individual Override
      ↓
Final Subject Enrollment
For example:
PCM
├── Physics
├── Chemistry
└── Mathematics

Student
└── Approved Subject Exception
The exception belongs to the individual student's academic context.
It must not modify the shared PCM configuration.
 
10. Subject Enrollment History
Subject Enrollment must retain the academic context in which the student studied the subject.
Example:
2027
Form V
PCM
│
├── Physics
├── Chemistry
└── Mathematics
Then:
2028
Form VI
PCM
│
├── Physics
├── Chemistry
└── Mathematics
Even though the subjects are identical, the two sets belong to different academic contexts.
Historical marks must remain attached to the correct historical subject context.
 
11. Teacher Assignment
Teachers are assigned to Subject Offerings, not permanently to Subjects.
Example:
Mathematics
      ↓
Subject Offering
      ↓
Form II / Stream A / 2027
      ↓
Teacher: Mr. John
Another:
Mathematics
      ↓
Subject Offering
      ↓
Form II / Stream B / 2027
      ↓
Teacher: Ms. Jane
The same Subject can therefore have different teachers across different academic contexts.
 
12. Teacher vs Teacher Assignment
The Teacher represents the person/user.
Teacher Assignment represents the operational relationship between that teacher and a Subject Offering.
Conceptually:
Teacher
   ↓
Teacher Assignment
   ↓
Subject Offering
This allows a teacher to have multiple teaching responsibilities without making the Subject itself responsible for teacher ownership.
 
13. Multiple Teacher Assignments
HIMSSON must not assume that one Subject Offering always has exactly one teacher.
A school may have:
•	co-teachers;
•	assistant teachers;
•	substitute teachers;
•	practical/laboratory teachers;
•	temporary teachers.
Therefore:
Subject Offering
       ↓
Teacher Assignments
       ├── Teacher A
       ├── Teacher B
       └── Teacher C
Where appropriate, the school may designate a Primary Teacher.
The exact teacher-role taxonomy will be defined later.
 
14. Teacher Assignment Is Context-Specific
A teacher may teach the same subject in different academic contexts and years.
Example:
2026
Mathematics / Form II
Teacher A
Then:
2027
Mathematics / Form II
Teacher B
The historical 2026 assignment must remain intact.
A teacher change must not rewrite historical teaching responsibility.
 
15. Assessment
HIMSSON uses Assessment as the parent academic concept for evaluating student performance.
An assessment is not limited to a formal examination.
Examples include:
•	Quiz;
•	Class Test;
•	Assignment;
•	Mid-Term Examination;
•	Terminal Examination;
•	Practical;
•	Project;
•	Oral Assessment;
•	Mock Examination;
•	Annual Examination.
The system should therefore use:
Assessment
├── Quiz
├── Assignment
├── Test
├── Examination
├── Practical
└── Project
rather than building the academic architecture exclusively around "Exams."
 
16. Assessment Context
An Assessment must belong to a defined academic context.
Conceptually:
Assessment
├── School
├── Academic Year
├── Academic Period
├── Academic Group / Context
├── Subject Offering
├── Assessment Type
├── Assessment Name
├── Maximum Score
├── Assessment Date
└── Status
Example:
Mathematics
└── Mid-Term Test
    ├── 2027
    ├── Term 1
    ├── Form II
    └── Maximum: 100
Or:
Physics
└── Practical Assessment
    ├── 2027
    ├── Term 2
    ├── Form V
    └── Maximum: 50
 
17. Assessment Types Are Configurable
HIMSSON must not hard-code one universal assessment list.
Schools may configure or use different assessment types according to their academic operations.
Examples include:
•	Quiz;
•	Test;
•	Assignment;
•	Mid-Term;
•	Terminal;
•	Practical;
•	Project;
•	Mock;
•	Annual;
•	other authorized assessment types.
The detailed configuration mechanism will be defined during the assessment implementation phase.
 
18. Mark
A Mark represents a student's result for a specific Assessment.
Conceptually:
Assessment
      ↓
Student Mark
      ├── Student
      ├── Assessment
      ├── Raw Score
      ├── Maximum Score
      ├── Status
      └── Audit Metadata
Example:
Assessment:
Mathematics Mid-Term Test
Maximum: 100

Amani:
Raw Score = 78
A Mark must not be represented merely as:
Student → Mathematics → 78
because that loses assessment context.
 
19. Raw Score and Maximum Score
HIMSSON must preserve the original scoring context.
Example:
Raw Score: 38
Maximum Score: 50
The percentage may then be calculated:
38 / 50 × 100 = 76%
The raw score and maximum score remain the underlying assessment data.
A percentage must not be the only stored representation of the result.
 
20. Mark Historical Context
A historical mark must remain traceable to:
Student
 ↓
Academic Enrollment
 ↓
Subject Enrollment
 ↓
Subject Offering
 ↓
Assessment
 ↓
Mark
This allows HIMSSON to answer:
•	Which student?
•	Which academic year?
•	Which academic period?
•	Which school?
•	Which academic group?
•	Which subject?
•	Which assessment?
•	What was the original score?
without relying on the student's current placement.
 
21. Mark Corrections
Academic marks are sensitive records.
If a mark must be corrected, the correction should be controlled and traceable.
Example:
Original Score: 78

Correction:
82

Reason:
Data-entry error
The exact audit implementation will be defined later.
The architectural principle is:
A mark correction must not silently destroy the history of the original entry.
 
22. Assessment Lifecycle
Assessments should eventually support a controlled lifecycle.
Conceptually:
DRAFT
   ↓
OPEN / PUBLISHED
   ↓
MARK ENTRY
   ↓
SUBMITTED
   ↓
VERIFIED / LOCKED
The exact workflow and permissions will be defined later.
The purpose of this structure is to prevent uncontrolled modification of academic results.
 
23. Assessment Locking
Once an assessment and its marks become official, the system should support locking.
This is necessary because results may already have been:
•	reviewed by school administration;
•	used for calculations;
•	included in report cards;
•	communicated to parents;
•	used for ranking;
•	archived.
Therefore, HIMSSON must distinguish between:
Mark Entry
and:
Official / Validated Result
The exact validation workflow belongs to a later academic-results phase.
 
24. Academic Period
Assessments must belong to an Academic Period.
Example:
Academic Year 2027
│
├── Period 1
│   ├── Mathematics Test
│   └── Physics Test
│
├── Period 2
│   ├── Mathematics Test
│   └── Physics Test
│
└── Period 3
    └── ...
HIMSSON must continue using the generic Academic Period concept established in 0.6 rather than hard-coding exactly three terms.
A school may configure the visible terminology appropriately.
 
25. Relationship to Attendance
Attendance will eventually connect to the same academic context.
Potentially:
Student
 ↓
Academic Enrollment
 ↓
Academic Context
 ↓
Attendance
However, the detailed attendance architecture is intentionally not locked by 0.6-C.
Attendance may operate at:
•	school level;
•	Academic Group level;
•	subject/lesson level;
and requires its own architecture decision.
 
26. Relationship to Report Cards
Report Cards are outputs of academic data.
They must not become an independent source of marks.
The intended chain is:
Subject Offerings
      ↓
Assessments
      ↓
Validated Marks
      ↓
Calculated Results
      ↓
Report Card
The Report Card presents academic information.
It does not own the underlying marks.
This preserves the HIMSSON 0.2 principle:
Report cards do not own marks.
 
27. Canonical Academic Chain
The academic structure established through 0.6-A, 0.6-B and 0.6-C is:
SCHOOL
  ↓
EDUCATION LEVEL
  ↓
ACADEMIC YEAR
  ↓
ACADEMIC PERIOD
  ↓
ACADEMIC GROUP
  ├── STREAM
  └── SUBJECT COMBINATION
          ↓
      SUBJECT
          ↓
   SUBJECT OFFERING
          ↓
   TEACHER ASSIGNMENT
          ↓
     ASSESSMENT
          ↓
    STUDENT MARK
          ↓
   VALIDATED RESULT
          ↓
      REPORT CARD
The student connects through:
STUDENT
  ↓
ACADEMIC ENROLLMENT
  ↓
SUBJECT ENROLLMENT
  ↓
SUBJECT OFFERING
  ↓
ASSESSMENT
  ↓
MARK
 
28. Example — Primary
School
└── Primary
    └── 2027
        └── Standard 4
            └── Stream A
                └── Mathematics
                    └── Subject Offering
                        └── Teacher
                            └── Assessment
                                └── Student Mark
A student:
Amani
└── Academic Enrollment
    └── Standard 4 / Stream A
        └── Subject Enrollment
            └── Mathematics
 
29. Example — Secondary
School
└── Secondary
    └── 2027
        └── Form II
            └── Stream B
                └── Mathematics
                    └── Subject Offering
                        └── Teacher
                            └── Assessment
                                └── Student Mark
 
30. Example — Advanced
School
└── Advanced
    └── 2027
        └── Form V
            └── PCM
                ├── Physics
                ├── Chemistry
                └── Mathematics
Each subject can have its own Subject Offering and Teacher Assignment.
A student's academic context becomes:
Student
└── Academic Enrollment
    ├── Form V
    └── PCM
        ├── Subject Enrollment: Physics
        ├── Subject Enrollment: Chemistry
        └── Subject Enrollment: Mathematics
 
31. Example — Individual Subject Override
Suppose PCM normally contains:
PCM
├── Physics
├── Chemistry
└── Mathematics
A student receives an authorized individual subject change.
The correct architecture is:
Student
└── Academic Enrollment
    ├── Combination: PCM
    │
    └── Subject Enrollments
        ├── Physics
        ├── Chemistry
        └── Authorized Individual Override
The shared PCM definition remains unchanged.
 
32. Locked Architectural Principles
The following principles are now authoritative:
1.	Subject is a reusable catalogue entity.
2.	Subject Offering represents the actual teaching context.
3.	Subject Enrollment is a first-class persistent academic record.
4.	Subject Enrollment represents the student's participation in a Subject Offering/context.
5.	Subject Enrollment is distinct from Subject Offering.
6.	Subject assignment normally inherits from the student's Academic Group/Combination structure.
7.	Individual subject overrides are supported.
8.	Individual overrides do not modify shared Subject Combinations.
9.	Subject Offerings are school/year/academic-context specific.
10.	Teacher Assignment belongs to the relevant Subject Offering.
11.	Multiple teachers may be assigned to one Subject Offering where the school permits.
12.	A teacher is not permanently attached to a Subject.
13.	Assessment is the parent concept for tests, examinations, practicals, projects, assignments, and similar evaluations.
14.	Assessment Types are configurable.
15.	Assessments belong to defined academic contexts.
16.	Marks belong to specific Assessments.
17.	Marks retain raw score and maximum score.
18.	Percentage is derived from the scoring context and must not be the only underlying value.
19.	Historical marks remain connected to their original academic context.
20.	Mark corrections must be controlled and traceable.
21.	Assessment workflows should support controlled progression toward verification/locking.
22.	Official/validated academic results must be distinguishable from unrestricted mark entry.
23.	Report Cards consume validated academic data and do not own marks.
24.	Attendance is related to academic context but is not fully defined by 0.6-C.
25.	Detailed grading, weighting, totals, averages, positions/ranking, result calculation, and report-card calculation remain separate architecture decisions.
 
33. Architectural Boundary
HIMSSON now has a clear separation between:
WHAT EXISTS
Subject
WHERE/WHEN IT IS TAUGHT
Subject Offering
WHO STUDIES IT
Subject Enrollment
WHO TEACHES IT
Teacher Assignment
WHAT WAS ASSESSED
Assessment
WHAT THE STUDENT SCORED
Mark
WHAT THE OFFICIAL ACADEMIC RESULT IS
Validated Result
This prevents one database record or UI screen from becoming responsible for multiple unrelated academic truths.
 
34. Status
HIMSSON 0.6-C — Subjects, Subject Enrollment, Teaching Assignments & Assessment Structure is LOCKED.
The following chain is now part of the authoritative HIMSSON architecture:
Student
 ↓
Academic Enrollment
 ↓
Subject Enrollment
 ↓
Subject Offering
 ↓
Assessment
 ↓
Mark
 ↓
Validated Result
 ↓
Report Card
Subject, teaching, assessment, and result responsibilities must remain separated according to this architecture.
Any future change that conflicts with this model requires:
REOPEN → EVALUATE → REVISE → DOCUMENT → RELOCK
 
HIMSSON 0.6-D — Assessment Calculation, Grading, Totals, Averages, Positions & Academic Results
Status: LOCKED
Product: HIMSSON
Parent Architecture: HIMSSON 0.6 — Academic Structure
Depends On: 0.6-A, 0.6-B, 0.6-C
 
1. Purpose
HIMSSON 0.6-D defines how recorded student marks are transformed into academic results.
This includes the conceptual architecture governing:
•	assessment calculations;
•	score normalization;
•	assessment weighting;
•	subject results;
•	grades;
•	pass/fail outcomes;
•	overall academic results;
•	totals and averages;
•	optional student positions/rankings;
•	missing and exceptional marks;
•	result verification and locking;
•	recalculation;
•	historical result integrity.
This section deliberately defines the calculation framework, rather than forcing every HIMSSON school to use one universal grading or ranking methodology.
 
2. Core Principle
HIMSSON must provide a configurable academic calculation engine rather than hard-code one school's grading, weighting, ranking, or result methodology.
Different schools may use different academic policies.
Therefore, HIMSSON must separate:
1.	Academic data
2.	Calculation rules
3.	Calculated results
4.	Official/finalized results
The user interface must never become the owner of academic calculation logic.
 
3. Academic Calculation Chain
The conceptual academic result chain is:
ASSESSMENT
    ↓
STUDENT MARK
    ↓
NORMALIZATION
    ↓
WEIGHTING / CALCULATION
    ↓
SUBJECT RESULT
    ├── Score
    ├── Percentage
    ├── Grade
    └── Pass / Fail
          ↓
    OVERALL RESULT
    ├── Total
    ├── Average
    ├── Grade
    ├── Status
    └── Position (optional)
          ↓
      REPORT CARD
Not every school must use every stage.
For example, a school may use:
Marks
 ↓
Grade
 ↓
Report Card
while another may use:
Marks
 ↓
Weighted Result
 ↓
Grade
 ↓
Pass / Fail
 ↓
Position
 ↓
Report Card
The architecture must support both.
 
4. Raw Score Must Be Preserved
HIMSSON must preserve the original assessment score.
A student mark should conceptually retain:
Student Mark
├── Student
├── Assessment
├── Raw Score
├── Maximum Score
├── Derived Percentage
├── Status
└── Audit Metadata
For example:
Raw Score: 37
Maximum Score: 50
may produce:
Percentage: 74%
The system must not replace the original 37/50 with only 74%.
This preserves the original academic evidence and allows calculations to be reproduced or audited.
 
5. Score Normalization
HIMSSON must be able to normalize assessments with different maximum scores.
Conceptually:
Percentage =
(Raw Score ÷ Maximum Score) × 100
Examples:
18 / 20  → 90%
42 / 50  → 84%
75 / 100 → 75%
27 / 30  → 90%
The normalized percentage is a derived value, not a replacement for the original score.
 
6. Assessment Weighting
HIMSSON must support both:
6.1 Unweighted Calculation
Example:
Assessment 1 = 70
Assessment 2 = 80
Assessment 3 = 60

Average = 70
6.2 Weighted Calculation
Example:
Assessment 1
Score: 70
Weight: 20%

Assessment 2
Score: 80
Weight: 30%

Assessment 3
Score: 60
Weight: 50%
The resulting weighted contribution is:
14 + 24 + 30 = 68
Therefore:
Subject Result = 68
The weighting methodology must be configurable rather than embedded permanently into the application code.
 
7. Subject Result
A Subject Result represents the calculated academic outcome for a student in a specific subject and academic context.
Conceptually:
Subject Result
├── Student
├── Subject Enrollment
├── Academic Year
├── Academic Period
├── Academic Context
├── Calculated Score
├── Percentage
├── Grade
├── Pass / Fail Status
├── Calculation Rule Version
└── Result Status
A subject result belongs to the academic context from which it was calculated.
For example:
Student
└── Form II
    └── Mathematics
        └── Term 1
            └── Subject Result
 
8. Grade Calculation
HIMSSON must support configurable grading scales.
The system must not assume that all schools use:
A = 80–100
B = 70–79
C = 60–69
D = 50–59
F = 0–49
That may be one school's configuration, but it is not a universal HIMSSON rule.
A school may instead configure another valid grading structure.
Conceptually:
Grading Scale
├── Grade Definition
├── Minimum Boundary
├── Maximum Boundary
├── Display Label
├── Optional Description
└── Ordering / Priority
The grading engine applies the appropriate configured scale to the calculated result.
 
9. Grade and Pass/Fail Are Separate Concepts
HIMSSON must not assume:
Grade = Pass/Fail
Instead:
Calculated Score
       ↓
   Grade Rule
       ↓
     Grade

Calculated Score
       ↓
Pass/Fail Rule
       ↓
   Status
This allows a school to define its own academic policy.
For example:
Score: 68
Grade: C
Status: PASS
The grade and pass/fail outcome are therefore separate calculated properties.
 
10. Exceptional Mark States
A numeric zero must not automatically be treated as an absent or missing mark.
For example:
0 marks
may mean that a student participated and legitimately scored zero.
That is different from:
ABSENT
Therefore HIMSSON must support explicit mark/result states.
Potential states include:
RECORDED
ABSENT
EXEMPT
MISSING
INCOMPLETE
WITHHELD
The exact operational status catalogue may be refined during the detailed assessment workflow design.
The critical rule is:
A missing or exceptional academic state must never be silently converted into a numeric zero.
 
11. Overall Academic Result
Subject results and overall academic results are separate concepts.
Example:
Mathematics     74
Physics         81
Chemistry       77
Biology         69
English         72
These may contribute to an overall academic result.
Conceptually:
Subject Results
      ↓
Overall Calculation Rule
      ↓
Overall Result
The overall result may contain:
Overall Result
├── Total
├── Average
├── Grade
├── Pass / Fail
├── Position (if enabled)
├── Calculation Rule Version
└── Result Status
HIMSSON must not universally assume:
Overall Average =
sum of subject percentages ÷ number of subjects
The actual methodology must be configurable.
 
12. Advanced-Level Calculation
Advanced-level academic structures require particular flexibility.
For example:
Form V
└── PCM
    ├── Physics
    ├── Chemistry
    └── Mathematics
HIMSSON must not automatically assume that an overall result is simply the arithmetic average of all subjects.
Advanced-level result calculations may depend on:
•	subject combinations;
•	required subjects;
•	weighting;
•	school-defined rules;
•	combination-specific academic methodology.
Therefore:
Advanced-level result calculation must support configurable combination-aware rules.
The exact Advanced-level result methodology is intentionally not hard-coded in 0.6-D.
 
13. Ranking and Position
Ranking is an optional feature.
A school may choose to use:
1st
2nd
3rd
or may choose to report academic performance without student positions.
Therefore:
Ranking Enabled = TRUE / FALSE
must be supported at the appropriate configuration level.
If ranking is disabled:
No position calculation
No official position displayed
 
14. Ranking Scope
When ranking is enabled, HIMSSON must not automatically assume that students are ranked across the entire school.
Possible controlled ranking scopes include:
Stream
Academic Group
Class / Form
Subject Combination
Education Level
School
The ranking engine must use an explicitly defined scope.
For example:
Form V
├── PCM
│   ├── Student A
│   ├── Student B
│   └── Student C
│
└── PCB
    ├── Student D
    ├── Student E
    └── Student F
A school may decide that PCM students are ranked within PCM rather than against PCB students.
The ranking scope must therefore be an explicit academic configuration.
 
15. Tie Handling
When two or more students have identical ranking values, HIMSSON must apply an explicitly configured tie methodology.
Potential methods include:
Competition Ranking
1st
1st
3rd
Dense Ranking
1st
1st
2nd
Sequential Ranking
1st
2nd
3rd
HIMSSON must not silently select a tie methodology as an irreversible business rule.
The final ranking configuration will define the permitted method.
 
16. Result Calculation and Recalculation
Academic calculations may change while marks are being entered or corrected.
Therefore HIMSSON must distinguish between:
Working / Draft Calculation
Results are recalculated as permitted academic data changes.
Official Calculation
The result has passed the school's verification process.
Locked Result
The official result is protected from ordinary modification.
Conceptually:
MARK ENTRY
    ↓
CALCULATION
    ↓
RESULT READY
    ↓
VERIFICATION
    ↓
LOCKED
A locked result must not be casually overwritten.
 
17. Controlled Academic Corrections
If an underlying mark is discovered to be incorrect after result locking, HIMSSON must support an authorized correction process.
Conceptually:
Locked Result
      ↓
Authorized Correction
      ↓
Audit Record
      ↓
Recalculation
      ↓
Verification
      ↓
New Official Result
This preserves academic accountability.
A correction must not silently erase the fact that a previous official result existed.
 
18. Historical Calculation Integrity
This is a mandatory architectural rule.
Academic results must preserve the calculation configuration under which they became official.
Example:
2026
A = 80–100
Student:
82 → A
Later, in 2027, the school changes its scale:
A = 85–100
The student's historical 2026 result must remain:
82 → A
It must not automatically become:
82 → B
Therefore:
Finalized academic results must retain a reference to the calculation-rule version used to produce them.
Conceptually:
2026 Result
    ↓
Calculation Rule Version 1
    ↓
Official Result
A later configuration change creates:
Calculation Rule Version 2
without rewriting historical official results.
 
19. Calculation Configuration
The eventual calculation configuration layer should conceptually contain:
ACADEMIC CALCULATION CONFIGURATION
│
├── Assessment Rules
├── Weighting Rules
├── Grading Scale
├── Pass / Fail Rules
├── Overall Result Rules
├── Ranking Rules
└── Result Locking Rules
These configurations must be associated with the appropriate academic context.
The exact configuration inheritance model remains subject to further architecture definition.
 
20. Data Ownership
The academic calculation engine does not become the owner of raw academic data.
Ownership remains separated:
Assessment
    → Assessment structure

Student Mark
    → Student's recorded assessment performance

Calculation Rules
    → How performance is interpreted

Subject Result
    → Calculated subject outcome

Overall Result
    → Calculated academic outcome

Report Card
    → Presentation/official reporting of validated results
The report card does not become the source of truth for marks.
 
21. Architectural Invariants
The following rules are locked:
1.	HIMSSON supports configurable academic calculation.
2.	HIMSSON supports both weighted and unweighted assessment calculations.
3.	Raw scores and maximum scores are preserved.
4.	Derived percentages do not replace original assessment scores.
5.	Grading scales are configurable.
6.	Grade and pass/fail are separate concepts.
7.	Missing/absent/exempt states are distinct from numeric zero.
8.	Subject results and overall results are separate concepts.
9.	Overall calculation methodology is not universally hard-coded.
10.	Advanced-level calculations may use combination-aware rules.
11.	Ranking is optional.
12.	Ranking scope is configurable within controlled academic contexts.
13.	Tie handling is configurable.
14.	Official results can be locked.
15.	Corrections to locked results require controlled authorization and auditability.
16.	Recalculation must preserve academic integrity.
17.	Historical official results must preserve the calculation-rule version used to generate them.
18.	Changing future grading configuration must not rewrite historical official results.
19.	Academic calculation logic belongs to the academic/business layer, not the UI.
20.	Report Cards consume validated academic results rather than becoming the owner of those results.
 
22. Deliberately Deferred Decisions
The following are not yet fully defined by 0.6-D:
•	exact grading scale configuration structure;
•	exact weighting formula model;
•	exact overall-result formula model;
•	exact Advanced-level result methodology;
•	exact pass/fail rule structure;
•	exact ranking configuration hierarchy;
•	exact ranking scope combinations;
•	exact tie-breaking implementation;
•	exact result status catalogue;
•	exact result-lock workflow;
•	exact calculation-rule inheritance between Organization, School, Education Level, Academic Year, and Academic Period;
•	exact handling of incomplete assessments in final calculations;
•	exact report-card calculation/display structure.
These should be resolved through subsequent academic architecture decisions rather than guessed during implementation.
 
23. Canonical HIMSSON Academic Result Architecture
SCHOOL
  ↓
EDUCATION LEVEL
  ↓
ACADEMIC YEAR
  ↓
ACADEMIC PERIOD
  ↓
ACADEMIC GROUP
  ├── STREAM
  └── SUBJECT COMBINATION
          ↓
      SUBJECT
          ↓
   SUBJECT OFFERING
          ↓
      ASSESSMENT
          ↓
    STUDENT MARK
          ↓
   CALCULATION ENGINE
          ↓
    SUBJECT RESULT
          ↓
   OVERALL CALCULATION
          ↓
    OVERALL RESULT
          ↓
 POSITION / RANKING
   (if enabled)
          ↓
      REPORT CARD
Supporting configuration:
ACADEMIC CALCULATION CONFIGURATION
├── Assessment Calculation
├── Weighting
├── Grading
├── Pass / Fail
├── Overall Result
├── Ranking
└── Result Locking
 
24. Final Decision
HIMSSON 0.6-D is LOCKED at the framework level.
The platform will provide a configurable, versioned, auditable academic calculation architecture capable of supporting different private-school academic methodologies across Primary, Secondary, and Advanced-level education.
HIMSSON will preserve academic truth first:
Record the original academic evidence → apply the appropriate configured calculation rules → produce validated results → preserve the rule version → protect official historical results.
No single school's grading, weighting, averaging, ranking, or result methodology will be hard-coded into HIMSSON's foundational architecture.

 
HIMSSON 0.6-E — Report Cards, Result Publication & Academic Result Presentation
Status: LOCKED
Product: HIMSSON
Parent Architecture: HIMSSON 0.6 — Academic Structure
Depends On: 0.6-A, 0.6-B, 0.6-C, 0.6-D
 
1. Purpose
HIMSSON 0.6-E defines how validated academic results are transformed into official school-facing and student/parent-facing academic publications.
This section establishes the architecture for:
•	Report Cards;
•	academic result presentation;
•	result publication;
•	publication visibility;
•	result withdrawal;
•	report-card history;
•	digital report cards;
•	printable/PDF report cards;
•	academic comments;
•	authorized result access;
•	result publication notifications.
The purpose is to ensure that HIMSSON maintains a clear separation between:
1.	Academic data
2.	Calculated academic results
3.	Official result status
4.	Publication status
5.	Report-card presentation
 
2. Core Principle
The Report Card is a presentation and publication artifact. It is not the source of truth for academic results.
The academic calculation system remains the authoritative source of academic results.
The conceptual flow is:
Assessment
    ↓
Student Mark
    ↓
Calculation Engine
    ↓
Subject Result
    ↓
Overall Result
    ↓
Verification / Locking
    ↓
Result Publication
    ↓
Report Card
A Report Card consumes official academic results.
It must not independently calculate or maintain a competing version of those results.
 
3. Academic Result vs Report Card
The system must distinguish between an academic result and its presentation.
For example:
Official Academic Result
Mathematics = 76
Grade = B
Status = PASS
may be presented through:
Digital Report Card
or:
PDF Report Card
The underlying academic result remains the same.
Therefore:
Official Result
    ├── Web/App Presentation
    └── PDF/Printable Presentation
This prevents HIMSSON from creating separate academic truth inside PDF or report-card systems.
 
4. Report Card as a Period-Specific Publication
A Report Card represents an academic result publication for a defined context.
A typical Report Card may represent:
Student
↓
School
↓
Academic Year
↓
Academic Period
↓
Official Results
For example:
Student: John Doe
School: MANKO International School
Academic Year: 2026
Academic Period: Term 2
The Report Card therefore has clear academic context.
 
5. Report Card Content
A Report Card may contain student and academic information such as:
Student Information
Student Name
Student / Admission Number
School
Education Level
Academic Group
Stream
Academic Year
Academic Period
Subject Results
Subject
Score / Result
Percentage
Grade
Status
Overall Results
Depending on school configuration:
Total
Average
Overall Grade
Overall Status
Position
Only values applicable to the school's academic configuration should be displayed.
HIMSSON must not force every school to display every possible result field.
 
6. Configurable Report-Card Presentation
HIMSSON must support controlled report-card configuration.
Different schools may require different presentation formats.
For example:
Subject | Score | Grade
Another school may require:
Subject | CA | Examination | Total | Grade
Another may require:
Subject | Percentage | Grade | Comment
Advanced-level schools may require combination-aware presentation.
Therefore:
HIMSSON supports configurable report-card presentation while preserving a standardized underlying academic data architecture.
Configuration must not allow the presentation layer to redefine academic truth.
 
7. Controlled Template Architecture
HIMSSON should use a controlled Report Card Template system.
Conceptually:
REPORT CARD TEMPLATE
├── Student Information Section
├── Academic Context Section
├── Subject Results Section
├── Overall Result Section
├── Comment Section
├── Attendance Section (where applicable)
└── School / Authorization Section
Templates may determine:
•	what information is displayed;
•	display order;
•	permitted fields;
•	school branding;
•	terminology;
•	selected result components.
Templates must not redefine the underlying academic calculations.
 
8. Result Status and Publication Status
HIMSSON must separate academic result status from publication status.
Result Status
Conceptually:
DRAFT
CALCULATED
VERIFIED
LOCKED
Publication Status
Conceptually:
UNPUBLISHED
PUBLISHED
WITHDRAWN
This allows situations such as:
Result:
LOCKED

Publication:
UNPUBLISHED
The school can therefore finalize academic results without immediately making them visible to parents or students.
 
9. Result Publication
Publication is an explicit controlled event.
The conceptual lifecycle is:
MARK ENTRY
    ↓
CALCULATION
    ↓
RESULT READY
    ↓
VERIFICATION
    ↓
LOCKED
    ↓
PUBLISHED
    ↓
VISIBLE TO AUTHORIZED USERS
Publication must not occur merely because a calculation has completed.
A calculated result is not automatically a published result.
 
10. Publication Authorization
The ability to publish academic results must be permission-controlled.
A user having access to academic records does not automatically have permission to publish official results.
Publication authority must follow HIMSSON's broader authorization model:
Identity
+
Role
+
Scope
+
Permission
School-level academic publication must remain within the relevant School context.
 
11. Student and Parent Visibility
A student or parent should only be able to access an academic result when:
1.	the user is authorized to access that student;
2.	the result belongs to the correct School/context;
3.	the result has been published.
Conceptually:
Authorized User
      +
Correct Student Relationship
      +
Correct School Scope
      +
Published Result
      ↓
Accessible Report Card
A parent must not be able to access another student's report card by manipulating identifiers or URLs.
 
12. Academic Comments
HIMSSON must distinguish different types of academic comments.
Subject Comment
Associated with an individual subject result.
Example:
Mathematics
76
B
"Good improvement."
Teacher / Class Comment
Associated with the student's broader academic performance for the period.
School / Head Comment
An official school-level comment associated with the published academic result.
These should not be collapsed into one unrestricted comment field.
 
13. Result Withdrawal
A published result may need to be withdrawn through an authorized process.
For example:
PUBLISHED
    ↓
WITHDRAWN
Withdrawal must be controlled and auditable.
The system must not simply delete the historical publication event.
Where correction is required:
Published Result
      ↓
Authorized Withdrawal
      ↓
Correction
      ↓
Recalculation
      ↓
Verification
      ↓
Re-publication
 
14. Report-Card History
Previously published report cards must remain historically traceable.
If an academic result is legitimately corrected after publication, HIMSSON must not silently replace the historical record.
Conceptually:
Report Card
├── Version 1
│   └── Original Publication
│
└── Version 2
    └── Corrected Publication
The exact versioning implementation remains a technical design decision, but the historical principle is locked:
A later correction must not erase the existence of an earlier official publication.
 
15. Digital and Printable Report Cards
HIMSSON must support both:
Digital Report Card
Accessible through authorized HIMSSON interfaces.
Printable / PDF Report Card
Generated from the same official academic result data.
Conceptually:
Official Academic Result
        ↓
   Report Card
      /    \
     /      \
Digital     PDF/Print
The PDF is a presentation format, not a separate academic data source.
 
16. Communication Integration
Result publication may trigger a communication event.
For example:
Result Published
      ↓
Communication Event
      ↓
Recipient
      ↓
SMS / WhatsApp / App Notification
      ↓
Delivery Status
This follows the communication architecture defined in HIMSSON 0.2:
Event
 ↓
Message Rule
 ↓
Recipient
 ↓
Channel
 ↓
Delivery
 ↓
Delivery Status
The Academic domain owns the publication event.
The Communication domain owns:
•	message generation;
•	channel selection;
•	delivery;
•	delivery status.
Academic Results must not directly become the owner of SMS or WhatsApp delivery logic.
 
17. Publication Does Not Equal Notification Delivery
HIMSSON must distinguish:
Result Published
from:
Parent Successfully Notified
For example:
Result Published
        ↓
Notification Attempt
        ↓
SMS Sent
        ↓
Delivery Confirmed
or:
Result Published
        ↓
Notification Attempt
        ↓
Delivery Failed
A notification failure must not automatically invalidate the academic result publication.
 
18. Academic Security
Academic results are school-controlled records and must follow HIMSSON's authorization architecture.
Access must respect:
Identity
+
Role
+
School Scope
+
Student Relationship
+
Publication Status
+
Permission
School isolation must remain enforced at the data/backend layer.
The report-card UI must never be treated as the security boundary.
 
19. Relationship With Academic History
Report Cards must not be confused with Academic History or future Transcript functionality.
The conceptual architecture is:
ACADEMIC HISTORY
│
├── Official Results
│
├── Report Cards
│
└── Transcript
    (future dedicated capability)
A Report Card represents a particular academic publication.
Academic History represents the student's longitudinal academic record.
A future Transcript capability may consume historical academic records across:
•	academic years;
•	academic periods;
•	schools;
•	subjects;
•	enrollments;
•	official results.
Therefore, Report Cards must not become a replacement for Academic History.
 
20. Relationship With 0.6-D
0.6-D established the calculation architecture:
Student Marks
      ↓
Calculation
      ↓
Subject Results
      ↓
Overall Results
      ↓
Optional Ranking
0.6-E builds the publication layer on top:
Official Results
      ↓
Verification / Locking
      ↓
Publication
      ↓
Report Card
      ↓
Authorized Student / Parent / School User
The Report Card does not recalculate the result.
 
21. Architectural Invariants
The following rules are locked:
1.	Report Cards are presentation and publication artifacts.
2.	Official academic results remain the source of truth.
3.	Report Cards do not own marks or academic calculations.
4.	Report-card presentation may be configurable within controlled HIMSSON structures.
5.	Academic result status and publication status are separate.
6.	A result may be locked without being published.
7.	Publication is an explicit controlled event.
8.	Publication authority is permission-controlled.
9.	Parents and students only access results that are both authorized and published.
10.	Published results may be withdrawn through an authorized process.
11.	Publication withdrawal must remain auditable.
12.	Previously published report cards remain historically traceable.
13.	Corrected publications must not silently erase previous publication history.
14.	Digital and PDF/printable report cards use the same official academic result source.
15.	Communication handles notifications and delivery; Academic Results only generate publication events.
16.	Notification failure does not automatically invalidate academic publication.
17.	Subject, teacher/class, and school/head comments are distinct concepts.
18.	School and student data isolation applies to report-card access.
19.	Report Cards do not replace Academic History.
20.	Report Cards do not replace a future dedicated Transcript capability.
21.	UI presentation is not the security boundary.
22.	Report-card generation must respect the locked academic calculation and result architecture established in 0.6-D.
 
22. Canonical Report-Card Architecture
STUDENT
   ↓
ACADEMIC ENROLLMENT
   ↓
ACADEMIC CONTEXT
   ↓
ASSESSMENTS
   ↓
STUDENT MARKS
   ↓
CALCULATION ENGINE
   ↓
OFFICIAL ACADEMIC RESULTS
   ↓
VERIFICATION
   ↓
LOCKING
   ↓
PUBLICATION
   ↓
REPORT CARD
   ├── Digital
   └── PDF / Printable
           ↓
AUTHORIZED USERS
   ├── School
   ├── Student
   └── Parent / Guardian
Communication operates alongside publication:
RESULT PUBLISHED
      ↓
COMMUNICATION EVENT
      ↓
MESSAGE
      ↓
CHANNEL
      ↓
DELIVERY
      ↓
DELIVERY STATUS
 
23. Final Decision
HIMSSON 0.6-E is LOCKED.
HIMSSON will treat academic results and their publication as separate but connected concerns.
The system will preserve the following principle:
Calculate and validate academic truth first. Lock the official result. Publish it through a controlled process. Present it through the Report Card. Notify users through the Communication domain. Preserve the history of what was officially published.
This architecture ensures that Report Cards remain reliable, auditable, secure presentation artifacts rather than becoming a second, conflicting academic database.

 
HIMSSON 0.6-F — Student Attendance Architecture
Status: LOCKED
Product: HIMSSON — School Management & Operating Platform
Architecture Area: Academics / Student Operations
Applies To: Private Primary, Secondary & Advanced-Level Schools
Market Context: Tanzania
Initial Commercial Market: Dar es Salaam
 
1. Purpose
This document defines the official architecture and operating principles for Student Attendance within HIMSSON.
The objective is to provide schools with a reliable, simple and practical digital attendance system while preserving the architectural strength required by HIMSSON.
Attendance must support the real operating practices of Tanzanian private schools without forcing schools into unnecessarily complex educational technology workflows.
HIMSSON therefore follows the principle:
Digitize and strengthen familiar school attendance practices before attempting to reinvent them.
The system may be technically sophisticated underneath, but the everyday teacher experience must remain simple.
 
2. Attendance Scope
HIMSSON 0.6-F covers Student Attendance.
It does not define Staff Attendance.
Staff attendance remains a future School Operations capability.
Student attendance supports two attendance contexts:
1.	Daily / School Attendance
2.	Subject / Lesson Attendance
These are related attendance records but are not automatically interchangeable.
 
3. Daily / School Attendance
Daily Attendance is the primary and default attendance model in HIMSSON.
A school must be able to record attendance for a student on a school day without requiring:
•	a timetable;
•	lesson configuration;
•	subject scheduling;
•	classroom scheduling;
•	teacher scheduling.
The basic workflow should remain simple:
Teacher
   ↓
Select Class / Academic Group
   ↓
Select Date
   ↓
View Students
   ↓
Mark Attendance
   ↓
Save / Sync
Daily attendance is the primary attendance experience because it is appropriate across Primary, Secondary and Advanced-level schools.
 
4. Subject / Lesson Attendance
HIMSSON also supports attendance at subject or lesson level.
Subject attendance may be used by schools that require more detailed attendance tracking, particularly Secondary and Advanced-level institutions.
Conceptually:
School
   ↓
Academic Group
   ↓
Subject Offering
   ↓
Lesson / Attendance Context
   ↓
Student Attendance
Subject attendance is optional.
A school must not be forced to configure a timetable or lesson structure simply to use daily attendance.
 
5. Daily and Subject Attendance Are Separate Records
Daily attendance and subject attendance must be related but independent.
A subject-level absence must not automatically overwrite the student's daily attendance status.
Example:
Daily Attendance
Student: Amina
Status: PRESENT
Subject attendance:
Mathematics → ABSENT
Biology → PRESENT
English → PRESENT
This is valid.
Likewise, a school may record:
Daily Attendance: ABSENT
without having any subject-level attendance records.
The relationship between the two may support reporting and analysis later, but HIMSSON must not assume that one automatically determines the other.
 
6. Attendance Status Model
HIMSSON uses a controlled core attendance status model:
•	PRESENT
•	ABSENT
•	LATE
•	EXCUSED
These are the primary operational statuses.
The system must not create an excessive number of attendance statuses for individual reasons.
For example, HIMSSON should not create separate statuses such as:
•	MEDICAL_ABSENT
•	FAMILY_ABSENT
•	FUNERAL_ABSENT
•	TRANSPORT_ABSENT
Instead, attendance status and absence reason are separate concepts.
 
7. Attendance Status vs. Absence Reason
Attendance status answers:
What happened?
Absence reason answers:
Why did it happen?
Example:
Status: ABSENT
Reason: Medical
Another example:
Status: ABSENT
Reason: Family matter
The reason is optional.
A teacher must be able to mark a student absent even when the reason is not yet known.
 
8. Optional Attendance Time Data
HIMSSON may record actual attendance times without making them mandatory.
Possible data includes:
•	Check-in time
•	Check-out time
These should supplement attendance status rather than replace it.
Example:
Status: LATE
Check-in: 08:27
This allows HIMSSON to support schools that need arrival/departure information without forcing every school to operate a check-in/check-out system.
 
9. Partial-Day Attendance
HIMSSON should be capable of representing partial-day attendance situations without creating excessive attendance statuses.
Examples include:
•	student arrives late;
•	student leaves before the end of the school day;
•	student attends only part of the day;
•	student is authorized to leave school;
•	student returns after an appointment.
These situations should primarily be represented through attendance records, time data, reasons and controlled notes rather than creating dozens of specialized statuses.
 
10. Attendance Eligibility and Enrollment
Attendance must respect the student's Academic Enrollment.
Attendance cannot be calculated independently of enrollment context.
For example, if a student joins a school on 15 March, HIMSSON must not treat January and February as unexplained absences.
Similarly, when a student withdraws, future attendance obligations must stop appropriately.
Conceptually:
Academic Enrollment
        ↓
Attendance Eligibility
        ↓
Attendance Records
        ↓
Attendance Calculations
This directly connects 0.6-F with the enrollment architecture established in HIMSSON 0.6-B.
 
11. Student Identity Across Schools
A Student identity may persist across schools within the same Organization where HIMSSON has established that the records represent the same person.
However, attendance remains:
•	school-scoped;
•	enrollment-scoped;
•	context-specific.
Maintaining one Student identity does not merge attendance records between schools.
Cross-school relationships do not automatically provide cross-school attendance access.
 
12. Attendance Authorization
Attendance recording and correction must follow HIMSSON's authorization model:
Identity + Role + Scope + Permission
A teacher must not automatically receive attendance access to every student in the school.
Access should respect the teacher's authorized academic context.
For example:
Teacher A
↓
Form I A
↓
Mathematics
does not automatically mean:
Teacher A
↓
Form III B
↓
All Students
Attendance permissions must therefore be scoped appropriately.
 
13. Attendance Corrections
Attendance must support controlled corrections.
Teachers and authorized school users may make legitimate mistakes.
Example:
Original:
Asha → ABSENT

Correction:
Asha → LATE
The current attendance record should reflect the corrected value.
The system should retain audit information such as:
•	previous value;
•	new value;
•	person who made the change;
•	date/time of change;
•	reason where required.
Attendance correction must therefore be convenient for legitimate users while remaining auditable.
 
14. Attendance Register
HIMSSON should provide a familiar attendance-register representation.
Example:
FORM II A — OCTOBER

Student       1   2   3   4   5   6   7
------------------------------------------
Asha          P   P   A   P   P   P   P
Baraka        P   L   P   P   A   P   P
Charles       A   A   P   P   P   P   P
This familiar representation is important because HIMSSON should improve existing school practices rather than force schools to learn an unfamiliar attendance model.
The digital register should support summaries such as:
•	Present days;
•	Absent days;
•	Late days;
•	Excused days;
•	Attendance percentage.
 
15. Attendance Calculations
Attendance calculations must account for the student's actual attendance eligibility.
The system must not blindly calculate attendance against calendar days.
Relevant factors may include:
•	active enrollment period;
•	school attendance days;
•	school holidays;
•	authorized non-attendance;
•	withdrawal;
•	re-enrollment;
•	applicable attendance context.
The exact calculation rules should remain configurable where appropriate.
 
16. "Not Expected" / Non-Attendance Context
HIMSSON should be capable of distinguishing situations where a student was not expected to have an attendance record from genuine absence.
Examples may include:
•	student not yet enrolled;
•	student already withdrawn;
•	school holiday;
•	other authorized non-attendance contexts.
These situations should not necessarily become teacher-facing attendance statuses.
Where appropriate, they should be handled through eligibility and calculation logic.
The principle is:
Do not turn every exceptional situation into an attendance button.
 
17. Offline Attendance
Attendance must support HIMSSON's offline-first architecture.
A teacher should be able to record attendance even when internet connectivity is temporarily unavailable.
Conceptually:
Teacher Device
      ↓
Local Attendance Record
      ↓
Connection Returns
      ↓
Synchronization
      ↓
Server
      ↓
Confirmed Record
Offline attendance is not considered an advanced optional feature.
It is part of HIMSSON's practical operating model for Tanzanian schools.
 
18. Low-End Device Practicality
Attendance must be designed for practical use on lower-end Android devices.
The attendance experience should prioritize:
•	fast loading;
•	minimal data usage;
•	large and clear touch targets;
•	minimal unnecessary imagery;
•	minimal animation;
•	fast saving;
•	clear synchronization status;
•	reliable operation under poor connectivity.
The goal is to allow a teacher to complete attendance quickly without fighting the application.
 
19. Parent Communication
Attendance may generate communication events for parents or guardians.
Examples:
Student absent
        ↓
Attendance Event
        ↓
Communication Service
        ↓
SMS / WhatsApp / In-App
        ↓
Delivery Status
Communication remains responsible for:
•	message generation rules;
•	channel selection;
•	delivery;
•	delivery status.
Attendance is responsible only for producing the relevant event.
 
20. Parent Notification Controls
Attendance notifications should be configurable by the school.
Possible notification events include:
•	Student absent;
•	Student late;
•	Student leaves early.
Schools may eventually choose between:
•	immediate notification;
•	daily summary;
•	no notification for selected events.
HIMSSON should avoid notification overload.
Repeated corrections to the same attendance record should not unnecessarily produce multiple confusing parent notifications.
 
21. Primary, Secondary and Advanced-Level Application
The underlying attendance architecture is shared across all supported education levels.
Primary
The normal experience should primarily be:
Class
↓
Students
↓
Daily Attendance
Subject attendance may remain optional.
Secondary
Schools may use:
Daily Attendance
+
Subject Attendance
Advanced
Subject/lesson attendance may become more useful because students operate within subject combinations and specialized subject structures.
However, the same underlying attendance architecture remains applicable.
HIMSSON must not create three unrelated attendance systems.
 
22. Timetable Independence
Attendance must not depend on the existence of a timetable.
The architecture should allow future integration with a timetable system:
Attendance
      ↓
Optional Timetable Integration
      ↓
Subject / Lesson Attendance
The reverse dependency is prohibited:
Timetable
      ↓
Required before Attendance
A school must be able to use HIMSSON attendance without configuring a timetable.
 
23. Attendance Reporting
Initial attendance reporting should remain practical rather than becoming an excessive analytics platform.
Core reporting should support:
Teacher
•	Today's attendance.
Class Teacher / Responsible Staff
•	Class attendance history;
•	attendance percentage;
•	absent/late students.
School Administration
•	School attendance overview;
•	class-level attendance;
•	student attendance history.
Parent / Guardian
•	Their child's attendance information, subject to authorization and publication/access rules.
Advanced predictive attendance analytics are not required for the core HIMSSON attendance architecture.
 
24. Deliberate Non-Scope
The following are deliberately outside the core 0.6-F scope:
•	Staff attendance;
•	biometric attendance;
•	fingerprint attendance;
•	facial recognition;
•	RFID attendance;
•	dedicated attendance hardware;
•	mandatory timetable dependency;
•	predictive attendance AI;
•	complex attendance scoring systems.
These may be considered independently in future phases if there is a clear commercial and operational reason.
 
25. Architectural Relationship to Other HIMSSON Domains
Attendance must integrate with the wider HIMSSON architecture.
Academic Enrollment
Defines the student's attendance eligibility and academic context.
Academic Structure
Provides:
•	Education Level;
•	Academic Year;
•	Academic Period;
•	Academic Group;
•	Stream;
•	Subject Offering where applicable.
Subject Architecture
Provides context for subject/lesson attendance.
Communication
Handles attendance-triggered notifications and delivery.
Documents & Operations
May later consume attendance information for administrative reporting.
Attendance does not own these domains.
 
26. Canonical Attendance Model
The conceptual structure is:
STUDENT
   ↓
ACADEMIC ENROLLMENT
   ↓
ATTENDANCE ELIGIBILITY
   ↓
┌─────────────────────────────┐
│                             │
│  DAILY ATTENDANCE           │
│                             │
│  Student                    │
│  Date                       │
│  Status                     │
│  Reason (optional)          │
│  Check-in (optional)        │
│  Check-out (optional)       │
│                             │
└─────────────────────────────┘

              OR / AND

┌─────────────────────────────┐
│                             │
│  SUBJECT ATTENDANCE         │
│                             │
│  Student                    │
│  Subject Offering           │
│  Lesson / Context           │
│  Date / Time                │
│  Status                     │
│  Reason (optional)          │
│                             │
└─────────────────────────────┘
The two attendance contexts remain related but independent.
 
27. Attendance Status Model
The core operational statuses are:
PRESENT
ABSENT
LATE
EXCUSED
Additional non-attendance situations should preferably be handled through eligibility, context or calculation rules rather than uncontrolled expansion of the status list.
 
28. Attendance Correction Model
Conceptually:
Attendance Record
       ↓
Current Status
       ↓
Authorized Correction
       ↓
New Status
       ↓
Audit Record
The system must preserve the correction history without making the normal user experience unnecessarily complicated.
 
29. Core Product Principle
The defining principle of HIMSSON Attendance is:
Technically strong underneath. Operationally familiar on the surface.
HIMSSON should provide:
•	reliable digital records;
•	enrollment-aware calculations;
•	authorization;
•	auditability;
•	offline capability;
•	communication integration;
•	school-level reporting;
while keeping the teacher's everyday task simple:
Open the class, see the students, mark who is here, save.
 
30. Final Lock
HIMSSON 0.6-F — Student Attendance Architecture is hereby LOCKED.
This lock establishes the architectural foundation for student attendance across:
•	Private Primary Schools;
•	Private Secondary Schools;
•	Private Advanced-Level Schools.
Future attendance implementation must comply with this document.
Any proposal that materially changes:
•	attendance ownership;
•	daily vs. subject attendance;
•	status model;
•	enrollment dependency;
•	authorization model;
•	offline behavior;
•	correction/audit principles;
•	communication responsibility;
must reopen and formally review HIMSSON 0.6-F before implementation.
No implementation should silently reinterpret these decisions.
 
HIMSSON 0.6-G — Academic Context, Calendar, Academic Year, Academic Period & School-Year Operations
Status: LOCKED
Parent Architecture: HIMSSON 0.6 — Academic Structure
Depends On: HIMSSON 0.4, 0.5, 0.6-A, 0.6-B, 0.6-C, 0.6-D, 0.6-E, 0.6-F

1. Purpose
HIMSSON must support private schools in Tanzania that operate under different academic calendars, curricula, and academic cycles.
A School may contain Primary, Secondary, and Advanced-level education. These levels may share an academic calendar, or they may operate under different calendars. International programmes may also follow different academic cycles.
HIMSSON must therefore avoid assuming that:
•	Every School begins its academic year in January.
•	Every Academic Year follows January–December.
•	Every School uses exactly three terms.
•	Every education level must have its own Academic Context.
•	All education levels and programmes within one School must share one calendar.
•	Every difference in scheduling requires a separate Academic Context.
This document defines how HIMSSON represents academic operating contexts, calendars, academic years, academic periods, operating days, and calendar events.
2. Core Locked Principle
An Academic Context is a school-scoped academic operating environment in which a group of learners can coherently operate under the same fundamental academic framework.
An Academic Context may contain one or multiple education levels.
Education Level alone does not automatically create a separate Academic Context.
A separate Academic Context is required when groups of learners operate under materially different academic conditions that cannot safely and coherently share the same academic operating framework.
3. Canonical Academic Time Hierarchy
The conceptual hierarchy is:
ORGANIZATION
↓
SCHOOL
↓
ACADEMIC CONTEXT
↓
ACADEMIC CALENDAR
↓
ACADEMIC YEAR
↓
ACADEMIC PERIOD
↓
OPERATING / NON-OPERATING DAYS
↓
CALENDAR EVENTS
Each level has a distinct responsibility. These concepts must not be collapsed into one overloaded entity.
4. Academic Context and Academic Calendar
Each Academic Context operates under one coherent academic calendar model.
A School may contain multiple Academic Contexts, each with its own academic calendar.
For example:
SCHOOL
├── ACADEMIC CONTEXT A
│   ├── Primary and Secondary
│   └── Calendar: January–December
│
└── ACADEMIC CONTEXT B
    ├── Advanced
    └── Calendar: June–May
This is valid when the Advanced-level cycle is materially different from the Primary and Secondary cycle.
HIMSSON must not force incompatible academic cycles into one Academic Context merely because the learners attend the same physical School.
5. Academic Context Does Not Mean Education Level
HIMSSON must not automatically implement the following structure:
Primary = Academic Context
Secondary = Academic Context
Advanced = Academic Context
Instead, one Academic Context may include multiple education levels when they share a sufficiently common academic framework.
Example:
SCHOOL
└── ACADEMIC CONTEXT A
    ├── Primary
    └── Secondary
This is valid if both levels can coherently share the same academic calendar and fundamental academic operating framework.
Another School may legitimately use separate contexts for each level.
The architecture supports both arrangements.
6. What Makes an Academic Difference Material?
A difference is material when allowing the affected groups to share one Academic Context would make the academic structure ambiguous, contradictory, or unsafe.
6.1 Academic Calendar or Cycle
Different academic cycles are a strong reason to separate contexts.
Example:
Context A: January–December
Context B: June–May
Another example:
Context A: Tanzania Programme, January–December
Context B: International Programme, September–August
Separate contexts are appropriate when these represent genuinely distinct academic cycles.
6.2 Academic Year Structure
If groups cannot coherently share the same Academic Year framework, separate contexts are required.
The displayed names alone do not determine whether contexts must be separated. What matters is the underlying academic cycle and operating model.
For example, 2026 and 2026/2027 are not inherently incompatible merely because their labels differ.
6.3 Academic Period Structure
One context may use Term 1, Term 2, and Term 3, while another uses Semester 1 and Semester 2.
Different names alone do not automatically require separate contexts.
However, materially incompatible academic period structures may require separation when they cannot coherently operate under one shared framework.
6.4 Curriculum or Programme
A materially different curriculum or programme may justify a separate context.
However, a different programme name alone does not automatically create one.
For example, a small enrichment programme can remain within an existing context if it shares the same academic year, periods, student enrollment framework, and overall academic operating model.
6.5 Fundamental Academic Operating Model
Other differences may justify separate contexts if they fundamentally change the academic operating framework, including substantial differences in progression, curriculum organization, academic cycle, or period structure.
This category must be used deliberately rather than as a general reason to create additional contexts.
7. Differences That Do Not Automatically Create Contexts
The following normally remain within an existing Academic Context:
•	Different classes, grades, standards, or forms.
•	Different streams.
•	Different subject combinations.
•	Different subjects.
•	Different teachers.
•	Different Subject Offerings.
•	Different assessments or examinations.
•	Different examination dates.
•	Different parent meeting dates.
•	Different school events.
•	Ordinary administrative or scheduling variations.
These differences are handled by their relevant academic or operational structures.
8. Academic Year
An Academic Year represents a recognized academic cycle within an Academic Context.
It is not necessarily the same as a calendar year.
Examples of displayed Academic Year names include:
•	2026
•	2026/2027
•	2026–2027
HIMSSON must not hard-code the January–December assumption.
The Academic Year model must support, at minimum, the conceptual properties of:
•	Academic Context relationship.
•	Display name.
•	Start date.
•	End date.
•	Sequence or ordering.
•	Status.
The final database schema and permitted status transitions will be defined during implementation planning.
9. Multiple Academic Years
An Academic Context may contain historical, current, and planned Academic Years.
Example:
Secondary / Tanzania Context
├── 2025 — Closed
├── 2026 — Active
└── 2027 — Planned
An Academic Context is the continuing academic operating environment. An Academic Year is a specific cycle within that environment.
Normally, one Academic Context should have one current operational Academic Year at a time. Any exception must be explicitly justified and designed rather than introduced accidentally.
10. Academic Period
An Academic Period is a configurable major subdivision of an Academic Year.
Examples include:
Academic Year 2026
├── Term 1
├── Term 2
└── Term 3
or:
Academic Year 2026/2027
├── Semester 1
└── Semester 2
HIMSSON must not hard-code exactly three terms or force every School to use the same period terminology.
The underlying concept remains Academic Period, while the displayed label may be Term, Semester, Period, or another supported label.
An Academic Period should conceptually define:
•	Name.
•	Sequence or ordering.
•	Start date.
•	End date.
•	Academic Year relationship.
•	Status.
11. Calendar Events
Calendar Events represent specific scheduled activities or operational occurrences.
Examples include:
•	School opening.
•	School closing.
•	Public or school holidays.
•	Mid-term breaks.
•	Parent meetings.
•	Graduation ceremonies.
•	Sports days.
•	Staff development days.
•	School ceremonies.
•	Other scheduled activities.
A Calendar Event does not become an Academic Period simply because it spans several days.
For example:
Term 1: January 8–April 5
    ├── School Opening
    ├── Mid-Term Break: February 15–19
    ├── Parent Meeting
    ├── Examination
    └── School Closing
Term 1 remains one Academic Period.
12. Academic Period vs Calendar Event
An Academic Period defines a major academic subdivision.
A Calendar Event defines something scheduled on or during a date range.
Example:
Academic Period:
Term 1
January 8–April 5

Calendar Event:
Mid-Term Break
February 15–19
The break does not divide Term 1 into multiple Academic Periods.
This distinction prevents calendar events from unnecessarily changing the underlying academic structure.
13. Operating and Non-Operating Days
HIMSSON must not rely on a manually entered number such as Teaching Days = 62 as the sole authoritative source of operating-day eligibility.
Operating-day eligibility must be derived from the relevant calendar and applicable operational rules.
Conceptually:
ACADEMIC PERIOD
      ↓
ACADEMIC CALENDAR
      ↓
OPERATING / NON-OPERATING DAYS
      ↓
ATTENDANCE ELIGIBILITY
The system must distinguish days on which a student is expected to attend from dates on which attendance is not expected.
The exact rules for weekends, public holidays, school-specific holidays, exceptional closures, and timetable-based operation will be specified in the relevant operational design.
14. Relationship to Attendance
Attendance remains the owner of attendance records and attendance calculations.
The academic calendar supplies the temporal and operational context needed to determine attendance eligibility.
Attendance must account for applicable factors, including:
•	School and Academic Context.
•	Academic Year and Period.
•	Operating and non-operating days.
•	Holidays and breaks.
•	Student enrollment dates.
•	Withdrawal and re-enrollment.
•	Authorized non-attendance.
•	Other applicable eligibility rules.
A student must not be treated as absent on a date when the student was not expected to attend.
This decision extends the principles locked in HIMSSON 0.6-F.
15. Relationship to Assessment
Assessments may reference the relevant Academic Context, Academic Year, Academic Period, Academic Group, and Subject Offering.
However, Assessment owns its own business records and rules.
The Calendar may display an examination date, but the Calendar does not own the examination's marks, scoring, verification, or results.
Conceptually:
CALENDAR
└── Examination Date / Event

ACADEMIC ASSESSMENT
├── Assessment
├── Student Marks
├── Verification
└── Results
A calendar representation of an examination must not replace or duplicate the authoritative assessment record.
16. Relationship to Money
The Money domain remains the source of truth for:
•	Fee Structures.
•	Student Charges.
•	Payment Initiation.
•	Confirmed Payments.
•	Receipts.
•	Student Ledgers.
•	Financial Balances.
•	Financial Reporting.
Financial records may reference the relevant academic context, year, period, or enrollment where required.
Academic Context and Calendar provide academic time context; they do not own financial truth.
17. Relationship to Communication
Calendar events may trigger communication events.
Example:
Parent Meeting Created
↓
Communication Event
↓
Recipient Selection
↓
SMS / WhatsApp / App Notification
↓
Delivery Status
The Calendar determines what is scheduled and when.
Communication owns recipient rules, channels, message delivery, and delivery status.
A notification failure does not automatically invalidate the underlying calendar event.
18. Examination Events
An examination may have both a calendar representation and an academic assessment record.
For example:
Calendar:
Mathematics Examination — June 20
The authoritative academic record may contain:
Assessment
├── Subject Offering
├── Assessment Type
├── Maximum Score
├── Assessment Date
├── Student Marks
└── Result
The two representations serve different purposes:
•	The calendar supports scheduling and visibility.
•	The Assessment domain supports academic processing and records.
They must be linked or reconciled appropriately where both exist, rather than treated as unrelated duplicate sources of truth.
19. Different Calendars Within One School
HIMSSON must support multiple academic calendars within one School when multiple Academic Contexts require them.
Example:
SCHOOL
├── Context A
│   └── Tanzania Programme
│       Calendar: January–December
│
└── Context B
    └── International Programme
        Calendar: September–August
Another valid example:
SCHOOL
├── Context A
│   └── Secondary Forms I–IV
│       Calendar: January–December
│
└── Context B
    └── Advanced Forms V–VI
        Calendar: June–May
This supports schools that combine education levels or programmes with different academic cycles.
20. Different Calendars for Groups Within One Context
HIMSSON should not normally allow materially incompatible academic cycles to operate inside one Academic Context.
For example:
Secondary Context
├── Form I–IV: January–December
└── Form V–VI: June–May
If these groups cannot coherently share the same Academic Year and Academic Period framework, they should normally be separated into distinct Academic Contexts.
The reason for separation is the incompatibility of their academic operating cycles, not simply the fact that the groups are different.
21. Slight Date Differences Do Not Automatically Require Separation
Minor differences in dates do not automatically justify separate Academic Contexts.
For example, one class may have a special examination extending beyond its normal teaching dates. Another group may have a parent meeting on a different date.
These are ordinary scheduling differences unless they reveal a fundamental incompatibility in the academic operating framework.
The central question is:
Can the affected groups still coherently share the same Academic Year and Academic Period framework?
•	If yes, they should normally remain in the same context.
•	If no, separate contexts should normally be established.
22. Academic Context Separation Test
HIMSSON should use the following decision logic when determining whether groups need separate contexts.
Question 1 — Academic Cycle: Do the groups operate on materially different academic cycles?
If yes, this is a strong reason for separate contexts.
Question 2 — Academic Year: Can they coherently share the same Academic Year framework?
If no, separate contexts are required.
Question 3 — Academic Periods: Can they coherently share the same Academic Period structure?
If no, separate contexts are required.
Question 4 — Curriculum or Programme: Do they follow materially different curriculum/programme structures?
If yes, evaluate whether those differences require separate contexts.
Question 5 — Fundamental Academic Rules: Do they have fundamental academic operating rules that cannot safely coexist?
If yes, separate contexts are appropriate.
Otherwise, keep the groups within the same Academic Context.
23. Preventing Context Proliferation
Academic Contexts must represent genuine academic operating boundaries, not every configuration difference.
HIMSSON must not automatically create new contexts for different:
•	Classes.
•	Streams.
•	Subjects.
•	Teachers.
•	Subject combinations.
•	Assessments.
•	Examination dates.
•	Parent meetings.
•	Calendar events.
The architecture should favour the smallest number of contexts that can safely and coherently represent the School's real academic operating models.
24. Academic Context Is School-Scoped
Academic Context belongs to a specific School.
Two Schools within the same Organization may have similarly named contexts, but those contexts remain operationally independent.
Example:
ORGANIZATION
├── School A
│   └── Tanzania Secondary Context
│
└── School B
    └── Tanzania Secondary Context
These are separate contexts, even if their labels and calendar patterns are identical.
This follows HIMSSON 0.4, which establishes that Schools are independent operational environments and that relationships between Schools do not automatically grant cross-school data access.
25. Student Relationship to Academic Context
A student's applicable academic calendar is determined through the student's authorized Academic Enrollment and the relevant academic structure.
Conceptually:
STUDENT
    ↓
ACADEMIC ENROLLMENT
    ├── School
    ├── Academic Context
    ├── Academic Year
    ├── Education Level
    ├── Academic Group
    ├── Stream (optional)
    └── Subject Combination (optional)
The student does not independently select an arbitrary academic calendar.
HIMSSON must use the authorized enrollment and context relationships to determine which academic time framework applies.
This must remain consistent with HIMSSON 0.6-B, which separates Student identity from time-bound Academic Enrollment.
26. Historical Integrity
Academic Years, Academic Periods, and their associated historical records must retain their original context.
Closing an Academic Year and creating a future year must not silently rewrite historical academic records.
For example:
2026 — Closed
2027 — Planned
Changes to the planned 2027 configuration must not automatically alter the official 2026 academic history.
Historical corrections must follow appropriate controlled procedures and preserve auditability.
27. Canonical Architecture
ORGANIZATION
│
▼
SCHOOL
│
──────────
▼                       ▼
ACADEMIC CONTEXT A      ACADEMIC CONTEXT B
│                       │
▼                       ▼
ACADEMIC CALENDAR A     ACADEMIC CALENDAR B
│                       │
▼                       ▼
ACADEMIC YEAR           ACADEMIC YEAR
│                       │
▼                       ▼
ACADEMIC PERIODS        ACADEMIC PERIODS
│                       │
▼                       ▼
OPERATING DAYS          OPERATING DAYS
│                       │
▼                       ▼
CALENDAR EVENTS         CALENDAR EVENTS
The student's enrollment determines which academic context and time framework applies to the student.
28. Domain Ownership Rules
Domain	Responsibility
Academic Context and Calendar	Academic time and operating calendar context
Academics	Academic structure, subjects, teaching, assessments, marks, and results
Attendance	Attendance records, eligibility application, and attendance calculations
Money	Fees, charges, payments, receipts, ledgers, and balances
Communication	Recipients, messages, channels, delivery, and delivery status
Documents and School Operations	Their respective records and operational workflows
The Calendar must not become an overloaded entity that owns every process with a date.
29. V1 Architectural Guardrails
HIMSSON V1 must:
1.	Avoid hard-coding January–December.
2.	Avoid hard-coding exactly three terms.
3.	Avoid equating Education Level with Academic Context.
4.	Avoid creating contexts for ordinary classes or forms.
5.	Avoid creating contexts for streams or subject combinations.
6.	Avoid creating contexts for subjects or teachers.
7.	Avoid creating contexts for individual assessments or calendar events.
8.	Support multiple academic calendars within one School where separate contexts require them.
9.	Keep materially incompatible academic cycles in separate contexts.
10.	Preserve historical Academic Years and official academic records.
11.	Provide calendar information to Attendance without transferring ownership of attendance records to Calendar.
12.	Keep Assessment and Money as owners of their respective business records.
13.	Prevent the user interface from becoming the authority for academic business rules.
14.	Avoid unnecessary context fragmentation.
15.	Enforce school-scoped access and data isolation.
30. Decisions Reserved for Later Architecture
This document does not fully define:
•	Exact database tables and field types.
•	Exact Academic Context creation and editing workflows.
•	Calendar versioning and amendment procedures.
•	Weekend and public-holiday calculation rules.
•	Exceptional closures and calendar overrides.
•	Timetable integration.
•	Detailed event categories and recurrence rules.
•	Exact permissions for calendar management.
•	Full enrollment-to-calendar validation rules.
•	Detailed fee-period alignment.
•	Detailed assessment-period validation.
•	Technical implementation of offline calendar synchronization.
These matters must be designed in their relevant phases without violating the locked principles in this document.
31. Final Locked Decision
HIMSSON models academic time through the hierarchy:
Academic Context → Academic Calendar → Academic Year → Academic Period → Operating Days and Calendar Events.
An Academic Context is a school-scoped academic operating environment. It may contain one or multiple education levels, but it must represent one coherent academic operating framework.
Materially incompatible academic cycles, calendars, or fundamental academic operating models require separate Academic Contexts.
Ordinary differences such as classes, streams, subjects, teachers, subject combinations, assessments, and individual calendar events do not automatically require separate contexts.
The Calendar provides temporal and operational context. The domain responsible for the underlying business process remains the source of truth.
HIMSSON 0.6-G — LOCKED.
 
HIMSSON 0.6-H — TIMETABLE, LESSONS & SCHEDULING ARCHITECTURE

Document ID: HIMSSON-0.6-H
Status: LOCKED
Decision Level: Standard Product Architecture
Product: HIMSSON School Operating System
Owner: MANKO Innovations
Scope: Timetable configuration, lesson scheduling, combined teaching, resource constraints, capacity validation, and escalation of unresolved mandatory-capacity violations.
 
1. Purpose
This document establishes the standard architecture for timetable and lesson scheduling in HIMSSON.
The architecture must support the operating realities of private schools while preventing genuine scheduling conflicts, preserving academic integrity, and allowing legitimate school-specific arrangements.
The system must support schools that use formal timetables and schools that operate without them. It must accommodate Primary, Secondary, and Advanced education, including streams, subject combinations, shared teaching, and different school calendars.
The governing principle is:
HIMSSON must be technically rigorous underneath and operationally simple for school staff.
The architecture defines what the system must do. Exact database schemas, interface layouts, notification intervals, and implementation algorithms may be finalized during technical design without changing these locked principles.
2. Timetable Configuration and Adoption
2.1 Optional timetable operation
A school may enable or disable formal timetable scheduling according to its operating model.
•	HIMSSON must remain usable by schools that do not maintain a formal timetable.
•	Daily student attendance must work independently of a timetable.
•	Subject or lesson attendance may be enabled according to the school's operating model.
•	When timetable scheduling is enabled, applicable scheduling constraints must be enforced.
•	Enabling a timetable must not silently change existing attendance records, subject assignments, enrollments, or academic history.
2.2 Independent operational capabilities
The following concepts are related but must not be treated as interchangeable:
•	Timetable configuration.
•	Scheduled lessons.
•	Actual dated lesson occurrences.
•	Student attendance.
•	Academic subject assignments.
•	Assessments and results.
A timetable may provide context to other school operations, but it does not become the owner of their records.
3. Flexible Timetable Structure
HIMSSON shall support flexible timetable assignment according to the academic organization of each school.
Applicable scheduling structures may include:
•	Academic group or class.
•	Stream.
•	Subject combination.
•	A supported combination of these structures.
•	Other explicitly supported school-specific grouping arrangements.
Examples include Primary classes sharing a common schedule or operating separate stream schedules; Secondary streams following different schedules; and Advanced groups following schedules based on combinations such as PCM or PCB.
A school must not be forced into one universal timetable structure.
3.1 Separate scheduling concepts
HIMSSON must distinguish conceptually between:
1.	Timetable structure: Days, periods, breaks, and applicable scheduling rules.
2.	Scheduled lesson: The academic teaching commitment, including its subject and participating academic group or groups.
3.	Dated lesson occurrence: The actual scheduled instance on a particular date and time.
These concepts may be implemented through related records or another technically appropriate structure, provided the distinctions and required behavior remain intact.
4. Combined Lessons and Shared Teaching
HIMSSON must support legitimate teaching arrangements involving multiple academic groups.
Two representations must be supported where appropriate:
•	One combined lesson serving multiple groups.
•	Separate group-specific lesson records linked to a shared teaching session.
The architecture must avoid duplicate teacher or room commitments, duplicate student counting, inconsistent attendance, and loss of group-specific academic context.
For example, Form V PCM and PCB students may attend a shared lesson with one teacher in one room. HIMSSON must recognize the shared teaching commitment while preserving individual student attendance and the correct academic context for each group.
A shared teaching arrangement must be explicitly represented and validated. Merely assigning the same teacher to two simultaneous lessons does not establish a legitimate combined lesson.
The system must distinguish changes affecting an entire shared session from changes affecting only one participating group.
5. Scheduling Conflict Prevention
HIMSSON shall apply controlled conflict prevention.
5.1 Approved conflict rules
Conflict or condition	Required behavior
Same teacher assigned to independent simultaneous lessons	Block
Same student group assigned to overlapping lessons	Block unless the overlap is explicitly legitimate
Same room booked twice	Block by default; apply authorized room-sharing rules
Advisory room-capacity excess	Warn; authorized user may proceed
Mandatory room-capacity violation	Never bypass through an ordinary timetable override
Legitimate combined lesson	Allow when represented and validated as shared teaching
Other explicitly supported exceptions	Apply the relevant rules, authorization, warnings, and audit requirements
5.2 Non-overridable conflicts
Some fundamental conflicts are non-overridable regardless of ordinary user permissions.
General administrator access must not provide a universal mechanism for bypassing scheduling integrity.
Only exceptions explicitly permitted by the applicable rules may be accepted. Where required, these exceptions must have appropriate authorization, justification, and audit history.
5.3 Validation integrity
Relevant constraints must be checked when creating, editing, moving, or otherwise changing timetable commitments.
Validation must consider the resulting schedule, applicable configuration, participating groups, teacher assignments, and resource commitments.
Concurrent changes must not silently create conflicting commitments. Where an earlier validation becomes outdated, the affected change must be revalidated before it is treated as confirmed.
6. Room Sharing, Capacity and Suitability
6.1 Room-sharing policy
One teaching session per room at a time is the default.
Authorized shared-room arrangements may be permitted under defined conditions. Sharing permission does not automatically authorize every overlapping booking.
HIMSSON must independently evaluate applicable constraints, including teacher availability, student-group overlap, capacity, and configured room suitability.
The system must support legitimate school arrangements without treating accidental double-booking as acceptable.
6.2 Room capacity classification
Each configured room-capacity rule must be classified as either:
•	Advisory: Exceeding the configured capacity produces a warning. An authorized user may proceed under the approved advisory policy.
•	Mandatory: The configured limit is a hard scheduling constraint that cannot be bypassed through an ordinary timetable override.
The school is responsible for establishing appropriate capacity values and classifications. HIMSSON enforces configured rules but does not certify the physical or legal safety of a room.
6.3 Capacity calculation for shared lessons
For a combined teaching session, expected attendance must account for the participating student population without counting the same student more than once.
Where the relevant population cannot be determined reliably, the system must not present the resulting capacity assessment as definitively verified.
6.4 Room suitability
HIMSSON shall support basic configurable room-suitability requirements, such as requiring a designated laboratory for a Chemistry practical when the school has configured that requirement.
Suitability rules may generate warnings or block scheduling according to their configured classification.
The V1 scope does not require a full facilities-management system.
7. Changing Mandatory Capacity
An appropriately authorized user may revise a room's configured capacity or its advisory/mandatory classification, subject to controlled change procedures.
Such a change must:
1.	Require the appropriate permissions.
2.	Record the reason and audit history.
3.	Identify affected future bookings before confirmation.
4.	Allow the authorized user to review the impact.
5.	Revalidate the affected bookings against the latest committed configuration.
6.	Flag bookings that violate the resulting mandatory rule.
A capacity change must not silently cancel, move, split, or rewrite existing bookings or completed historical records.
Changing a room's configuration is distinct from overriding an individual booking.
Ordinary scheduling overrides cannot bypass a mandatory capacity limit. Any permitted reclassification must occur through the authorized configuration-change process.
8. Affected Booking Status and Operational Readiness
HIMSSON must distinguish between:
•	Scheduling status: Whether a lesson is scheduled, rescheduled, or cancelled.
•	Constraint compliance: Whether the booking satisfies applicable mandatory and advisory rules.
•	Operational readiness: Whether the booking can be treated as ready under the applicable rules.
A future booking affected by a mandatory-capacity change remains recorded but must be flagged as requiring resolution until the issue is properly addressed.
An advisory warning must not be mislabeled as a mandatory violation.
Resolving a capacity issue does not automatically establish that teacher, student-group, room-sharing, or other scheduling conflicts have also been resolved.
The system must retain sufficient history to explain how a booking reached its current state.
9. Escalation of Unresolved Mandatory-Capacity Violations
9.1 General rule
An unresolved mandatory-capacity violation associated with an upcoming lesson must be highlighted and escalated to designated school authorities according to a configurable, deadline-based policy.
HIMSSON must not silently cancel the lesson or erase its history.
An unresolved violation remains open until verified resolution or a valid closure condition applies.
9.2 Escalation timing
Each school may configure applicable escalation deadlines, recipients, and notification settings.
When a violation is discovered after one or more deadlines have passed, HIMSSON must catch up immediately to the highest applicable missed escalation stage without sending a burst of notifications for every missed stage.
As the lesson approaches, escalation must advance without unnecessary waiting when the ordinary response interval would leave insufficient time for action.
Exact intervals and channel settings are implementation or configuration details.
9.3 Recipients and fallback routing
Escalation must cover both:
•	The person responsible for taking or coordinating corrective action.
•	The designated senior escalation authority.
If one person holds both responsibilities, notifications must be deduplicated while both responsibilities remain represented in the audit history.
Schools may configure an authorized fallback chain. HIMSSON must skip ineligible recipients, avoid unnecessary delays, deduplicate recipients, and flag routing gaps when no eligible recipient can be identified.
The system must never invent recipients, grant unauthorized permissions, or expose school data outside the permitted scope.
9.4 Acknowledgment is not resolution
Acknowledgment confirms awareness only.
It may suppress redundant reminders, but escalation must continue while the violation remains unresolved.
Notification delivery, acknowledgment, and resolution must be tracked as distinct events.
A failed notification must not clear the violation.
9.5 Verified closure
Closure requires revalidation of the latest committed booking state against the applicable mandatory-capacity rule.
A recorded change is not sufficient if the violation remains.
If the latest state cannot be verified, closure remains pending. The violation must remain visible, and applicable escalation must continue without duplicate alerts caused solely by repeated verification attempts.
A confirmed cancellation may close active escalation for the cancelled booking while preserving the historical violation and cancellation record.
Rescheduling must trigger evaluation against the confirmed new schedule. If the violation persists, escalation continues according to the applicable deadlines.
9.6 Unresolved violations after lesson start
If the lesson reaches its scheduled start time while the mandatory-capacity violation remains unresolved, HIMSSON must transition the violation into a distinct overdue-handling state.
The system must not automatically close the violation or treat the lesson as compliant merely because its start time has passed.
Overdue handling must follow a separately defined escalation policy until resolution or a valid closure condition applies.
Transitioning to overdue handling does not authorize the lesson to proceed.
10. Authorization, Audit and Communication
Scheduling changes, room configuration, permitted exceptions, and violation resolution must follow HIMSSON's established organization, school-scope, and permission model.
Escalation recipients do not automatically gain permission to change timetables or capacity settings.
The system must preserve relevant history, including:
•	Original and changed scheduling commitments.
•	Capacity configuration changes and reasons.
•	Affected bookings and impact-review outcomes.
•	Applied exceptions and authorization decisions.
•	Violation detection and status transitions.
•	Escalation stages, recipients, and routing gaps.
•	Notification attempts and delivery outcomes where available.
•	Acknowledgment, verification, resolution, cancellation, and overdue transitions.
The Communication domain owns notification delivery and retry behavior. It does not own the underlying scheduling constraint or determine whether a booking is compliant.
Obsolete queued notifications should be suppressed where possible after the relevant booking or violation changes, without deleting the audit history.
11. Offline and Low-End Device Considerations
Timetable and lesson workflows must remain practical for low-end Android devices and unreliable connectivity.
The architecture must support efficient timetable viewing and appropriate offline handling without weakening authoritative scheduling validation.
An offline change or local acknowledgment must not independently establish that a booking is compliant or that an escalation has been resolved.
After synchronization, the system must reconcile changes and revalidate the latest committed state before confirming compliance or closure.
12. Ownership of Records and Rules
The timetable provides scheduling context; it does not replace the ownership of other HIMSSON domains.
•	Academics owns subject assignments, assessments, marks, and academic results.
•	Attendance owns attendance records and attendance calculations.
•	Calendar and Academic Context provide the applicable academic and temporal context.
•	Communication handles notification delivery and delivery-status tracking.
•	Scheduling owns timetable commitments, scheduling validation, and the relevant capacity-violation lifecycle.
Cross-domain relationships must preserve each domain's source of truth.
13. Implementation Boundaries
This locked architecture does not prescribe:
•	A final database schema or exact entity count.
•	The final interface layout for timetable creation or conflict resolution.
•	Exact escalation intervals or notification templates.
•	A universal room-capacity value.
•	A universal recipient hierarchy for every school.
•	Every possible room-sharing or school-specific exception.
•	A full facilities-management subsystem.
•	Automatic lesson cancellation when a violation remains unresolved.
These details may be defined during technical design and implementation, provided they remain consistent with this document and the other locked HIMSSON decisions.
Features not resolved in this section must not be treated as approved merely because they are technically possible.
14. Final Locked Principles
1.	Formal timetable scheduling is optional; core school operations must not depend on its presence.
2.	Timetable structures must accommodate different education levels, groups, streams, and subject combinations.
3.	Legitimate combined teaching must be supported without duplicate commitments or loss of individual academic records.
4.	Fundamental scheduling conflicts must be blocked; only explicitly permitted exceptions may be accepted.
5.	Room sharing must be authorized and validated against independent constraints.
6.	Advisory capacity excess may produce a warning; mandatory capacity violations cannot be bypassed through ordinary overrides.
7.	Capacity changes require authorization, impact review, and revalidation of affected future bookings.
8.	Scheduling status, constraint compliance, and operational readiness must remain distinct.
9.	Unresolved mandatory-capacity violations must be escalated according to configurable deadlines.
10.	Acknowledgment and notification delivery must never be mistaken for resolution.
11.	Closure requires verification of the latest committed booking state.
12.	Violations that remain unresolved after lesson start must transition into overdue handling.
13.	Historical records and audit trails must be preserved.
14.	Implementation details may evolve, but they must not contradict these locked architectural requirements.
Final decision: HIMSSON 0.6-H — Timetable, Lessons & Scheduling Architecture is LOCKED at standard product-architecture level. Future changes require an explicit decision to reopen and revise this section.
 
HIMSSON 0.7 — COMMUNICATION & NOTIFICATIONS ARCHITECTURE

Document ID: HIMSSON-0.7
Status: LOCKED
Decision Level: Standard Product Architecture
Product: HIMSSON School Operating System
Owner: MANKO Innovations
Initial Market: Tanzania
Scope: School communications, notification events, recipient resolution, channels, delivery reliability, message history, privacy, and communication integrations.
 
1. Purpose
HIMSSON shall provide one shared communication and notification architecture serving all relevant school operations.
The system must make communication reliable, affordable, secure, and accessible to school administrators, teachers, students, parents, and guardians.
It must support Tanzanian school realities, including mobile-first access, unreliable internet connectivity, SMS delivery, and Swahili and English communication.
Communication is a shared platform capability, not an independent messaging implementation inside every school module.
2. Core Communication Architecture
Every notification shall follow a consistent lifecycle:
Operational Event → Notification Rule → Recipient Resolution → Channel Selection → Delivery → Delivery Status
2.1 Operational event
A source domain generates an event when a relevant business action occurs.
Examples include confirmed payments, qualifying attendance events, official result publication, and timetable changes.
The source domain remains responsible for determining whether the underlying event actually occurred.
2.2 Notification rule
HIMSSON evaluates whether the event requires communication and applies the relevant school configuration, message category, recipient rules, and channel preferences.
2.3 Recipient resolution
The system identifies eligible recipients using verified relationships, school scope, role permissions, and the requirements of the particular notification.
2.4 Channel selection
HIMSSON selects an eligible, supported, and appropriately configured delivery channel.
2.5 Delivery and status
The system queues and processes the message, records applicable delivery outcomes, and manages retries or permitted fallback behavior.
Communication history must distinguish message generation, queuing, delivery attempts, provider responses, delivery confirmation where available, and failure.
3. Source-of-Truth Ownership
The domain responsible for an operational event remains the authoritative source for that event.
Communication owns:
•	Notification rules and message orchestration.
•	Recipient resolution.
•	Channel selection and delivery.
•	Retry and fallback processing.
•	Message delivery-status tracking.
•	Communication history.
Communication does not own or independently determine the truth of financial transactions, attendance records, academic results, or timetable commitments.
For example, Finance confirms whether a payment succeeded. Communication may notify the relevant recipient of that confirmed event but must not independently declare the payment successful.
Likewise, a notification about published academic results does not create or validate those results.
4. Notification Categories
HIMSSON shall support a shared notification architecture for the following categories.
Category	Examples
Financial	Payment confirmations, receipt availability, fee-balance reminders
Attendance	Absence, lateness, attendance summaries
Academic	Official result publication, report-card availability, assessment announcements
School announcements	Meetings, closures, holidays, school events
Operational	Admissions updates, relevant timetable changes, administrative notices
Security and account	Sign-in verification, password recovery, important account-security alerts
The categories share the same communication infrastructure but may have different eligibility, urgency, delivery, and configuration rules.
Adding a new school module should not require creating an entirely separate messaging system.
5. Recipient Resolution, Authorization and School Isolation
Recipients must be determined from verified school relationships and applicable permissions.
HIMSSON shall enforce the following principles:
•	Parents and guardians may receive information only about students they are authorized to access.
•	Teachers receive information relevant to their school, assignments, and responsibilities.
•	School administrators and Owner/Director users receive communications consistent with their authority and school scope.
•	Multi-school access must not cause information from one school to be disclosed to an unrelated school.
•	Missing, incorrect, or ambiguous recipient relationships must not result in sending messages to unverified recipients.
•	Delivery processes must enforce the same relevant access boundaries as the originating school domain.
A recipient's ability to receive a message does not automatically grant permission to view or modify the underlying record.
6. Channels and Localization
HIMSSON shall use a mobile-first, SMS-capable, bilingual communication model.
6.1 In-app notifications
In-app notifications shall support users who access HIMSSON through its applications or supported interfaces.
They shall provide an accessible way to receive relevant updates without requiring a separate messaging application.
6.2 SMS
SMS shall be a key communication channel, particularly for parents and guardians who may have limited internet access or may not regularly use the application.
SMS usage must be cost-conscious and appropriate to the notification's importance and content.
6.3 Email
Email may be supported where appropriate and configured.
6.4 Future channels
The architecture must allow additional supported communication channels to be integrated without redesigning the core notification lifecycle.
6.5 Language support
HIMSSON shall support Swahili and English.
Swahili-first defaults shall be used where appropriate for the Tanzanian market. Schools and supported user preferences may determine the applicable language and template.
Messages must use clear, practical language suitable for their intended audience.
USSD remains a strategic access capability for suitable HIMSSON workflows. It is not automatically treated as a general notification channel; its detailed workflow architecture remains separate.
7. Delivery Reliability and Status Tracking
HIMSSON must distinguish the following delivery states and events where applicable:
•	Message generated.
•	Message queued.
•	Delivery attempted.
•	Provider acceptance or confirmation.
•	Delivery failure.
•	Retry pending.
•	Retry exhausted.
•	Delivery confirmed where reliable confirmation is available.
Provider acceptance must not automatically be represented as proof that the recipient received or read the message.
7.1 Retry behavior
The system shall support controlled retries for eligible delivery failures.
Retry policies must account for the message's validity, priority, provider behavior, and applicable configuration.
7.2 Duplicate prevention
Repeated operational events, synchronization retries, provider callbacks, or processing retries must not cause uncontrolled duplicate notifications.
HIMSSON should use appropriate deduplication and processing controls to prevent repeated delivery of the same logical notification where possible.
7.3 Obsolete messages
Where possible, the system shall suppress queued notifications that are no longer relevant because the underlying event or booking has materially changed.
Suppression must not erase the communication audit history.
7.4 Separation from operational truth
A failed notification must not reverse or alter the underlying school transaction.
Successful notification delivery must not be interpreted as confirmation that the recipient completed the relevant action.
8. School Configuration and Cost Management
Authorized school users shall be able to configure relevant notification behavior within their permissions.
Supported configuration may include:
•	Notification categories enabled for the school.
•	Eligible recipient groups.
•	Supported channels by category.
•	Language and template selection.
•	Reminder and repetition rules.
•	Delivery preferences and applicable limits.
Configuration must respect product-level security requirements and must not permit unauthorized disclosure or suppression of mandatory security and system-integrity notifications.
8.1 Cost-conscious delivery
HIMSSON shall support communication usage monitoring and cost-conscious delivery, especially for SMS.
The system should reduce unnecessary messages, duplicate notifications, and excessive reminders while preserving essential school communication.
Specific SMS providers, tariffs, and delivery quotas are implementation and commercial configuration matters.
9. Privacy, Data Minimization and Audit
Communication must follow HIMSSON's security, authorization, and school-isolation principles.
Messages shall contain only the information necessary for their purpose.
Sensitive financial, academic, or personal information must not be unnecessarily exposed through SMS, email, or other channels that may be visible to unintended persons.
The communication history shall preserve sufficient information to establish:
•	The originating event or notification reference.
•	The applicable notification category.
•	The intended recipient and relevant school context.
•	The selected channel.
•	Message creation and delivery-processing status.
•	Delivery attempts and outcomes where available.
•	Retry, fallback, suppression, or failure events where applicable.
Access to message history must respect school scope and role permissions.
The authoritative business record remains in its owning domain. Communication history records the notification process and its outcomes.
10. Offline Operation and External Integrations
HIMSSON shall support queued notification processing when connectivity or an external delivery provider is unavailable.
When service resumes, queued messages must be evaluated for continued validity and processed according to their applicable priority and policy.
Messages that have become obsolete should be suppressed where possible.
The architecture shall use consistent event and notification interfaces across school domains rather than allowing every module to implement independent messaging logic.
Examples include:
•	Finance emitting a confirmed-payment event.
•	Attendance emitting a qualifying attendance event.
•	Academics emitting an official-result-publication event.
•	Scheduling emitting a relevant timetable-change event.
Communication evaluates the applicable notification rules, resolves recipients, processes delivery, and records the outcome.
Provider integrations must not bypass authorization, school isolation, or source-of-truth ownership.
11. Relationship to Other HIMSSON Domains
Communication shall integrate with relevant HIMSSON domains while preserving their authority.
Domain	Relationship with Communication
Organization and Access	Supplies applicable identity, role, permission, and school-scope rules
Finance	Supplies authoritative payment and other financial events
Attendance	Supplies authoritative attendance events
Academics	Supplies authoritative assessment and result-publication events
Calendar and School Operations	Supplies relevant school events and operational updates
Timetable and Scheduling	Supplies relevant scheduling events and violation-escalation notifications
Communication	Applies notification rules, resolves recipients, delivers messages, and records outcomes
Communication must not replace the source domain's validation, transaction processing, or audit requirements.
For consequential events, such as payment confirmation and official result publication, the underlying business action must be valid independently of whether notification delivery succeeds.
12. Scope Boundaries
This architecture establishes the shared communication system without prescribing every implementation detail.
The following remain for technical design, configuration, or subsequent decisions:
•	Specific SMS providers and tariffs.
•	Exact retry intervals and escalation timing.
•	Final database schema and event payload definitions.
•	Individual message templates.
•	Exact sending hours for each notification category.
•	Detailed parent-consent and communication-preference workflows.
•	Provider-specific delivery guarantees.
•	Detailed USSD workflows.
•	Detailed operational rules for every notification category.
These details must be defined consistently with this locked architecture and applicable security and legal requirements.
13. Final Locked Principles
1.	HIMSSON shall use one shared communication and notification architecture across its school domains.
2.	Operational domains remain authoritative for their own business events and records.
3.	Communication owns notification orchestration, recipient resolution, channel delivery, retries, and delivery history.
4.	Recipients must be resolved using verified relationships, permissions, and school scope.
5.	The system shall support in-app notifications, SMS, email where appropriate, and future extensible channels.
6.	Swahili and English shall be supported, with Swahili-first defaults where appropriate for the Tanzanian market.
7.	Delivery status, provider acceptance, recipient acknowledgment where available, and underlying business completion must remain distinct.
8.	Controlled retries, duplicate prevention, and obsolete-message suppression shall be supported.
9.	School-configurable communication must respect authorization, privacy, and essential system-integrity requirements.
10.	Communication history must be auditable and accessible only within applicable permissions.
11.	Offline processing and external-provider failures must not corrupt authoritative school records.
12.	Exact providers, tariffs, intervals, templates, and detailed workflows may be decided during the appropriate implementation phase.
Final decision: HIMSSON 0.7 — Communication & Notifications Architecture is LOCKED at standard product-architecture level. Any material change to these principles requires an explicit decision to reopen and revise this section.
 
HIMSSON SCHOOL MANAGEMENT SYSTEM
Architecture Lock Document 0.8 — Security, Privacy & Data Governance Architecture
Document ID: HIMSSON-ARCH-0.8
Status: LOCKED — APPROVED
Product: HIMSSON
Parent Company: MANKO Innovations
Version: 1.0
1. Purpose
This document establishes the security, privacy, authorization, data-isolation, integrity, auditability, and governance principles that apply across HIMSSON.
These principles are mandatory architectural requirements for all current and future modules.
2. Identity and Authentication
1.	Each user must have a unique identity.
2.	Authentication establishes who the user is; authorization determines what that user may do.
3.	A user may access multiple linked schools only when explicitly authorized.
4.	School context must be correctly established and validated before school-scoped operations.
5.	Account recovery and authentication changes must follow controlled procedures.
6.	Credentials and authentication secrets must not be exposed in logs or ordinary application responses.
3. Authorization and Least Privilege
HIMSSON authorization follows:
Identity + Role + Scope + Permissions
The architectural role model includes:
•	Owner/Director: Organization-level authority within explicitly permitted organizational scope.
•	School Administrator: Administration of authorized school operations.
•	Teacher: School-scoped access limited by relevant teaching assignments and permissions.
•	Accountant: Authorized financial operations within the applicable school scope.
•	Parent/Guardian: Access limited to relevant students and explicitly permitted information.
•	Other roles: Must have explicitly defined responsibilities, scopes, and permissions.
No role automatically grants unrestricted access to all data or functions.
Authorization must be enforced by the backend. Hiding a page, button, menu item, or record in the interface is not a security control.
4. Organization and School Data Isolation
An Organization may operate multiple Schools, but this relationship does not automatically authorize unrestricted cross-school access or data sharing.
HIMSSON must protect school boundaries across:
•	User interfaces, APIs, and direct record access.
•	Guessed or manipulated record identifiers and URLs.
•	Search, reporting, and data exports.
•	Background jobs, integrations, and synchronization processes.
•	Administrative actions and related workflows.
Relationships alone do not grant access. Cross-school operations must have an explicitly authorized purpose, scope, and workflow.
5. Privacy and Appropriate Data Access
HIMSSON must apply data minimization and purpose-appropriate access.
Protected information includes student, parent/guardian, financial, academic, staff, and account-security records.
The architecture must support appropriate protection of data in transit and at rest, controlled disclosure, and restricted exports.
Data processing must comply with applicable Tanzanian laws and any other applicable legal requirements.
6. Data Integrity and Source of Truth
1.	Each domain remains authoritative for the records it owns.
2.	Consequential changes must be validated and authorized on the backend.
3.	Historical context must be preserved where required.
4.	Corrections, synchronization, concurrent updates, and recalculations must not bypass business rules or authorization.
5.	Client-side validation is supplementary, not a security boundary.
6.	One module must not silently overwrite another domain's authoritative records.
7. Auditability
HIMSSON must support appropriate audit trails for consequential actions, including:
•	Role, permission, and school-access changes.
•	Sensitive administrative actions and relevant sensitive-record access.
•	Financial and academic corrections.
•	Official result locking, publication, and withdrawal.
•	Timetable capacity configuration and authorized exceptions.
•	Exports, account-security changes, and other significant administrative actions.
Audit records should identify the actor, action, affected record or scope, time, and outcome where applicable.
Audit logs must be protected from unauthorized access and alteration. Logging must not unnecessarily expose credentials or sensitive personal information.
8. Data Lifecycle and Retention
The architecture must distinguish active, historical, corrected or superseded, archived, and deleted records as appropriate.
Retention and deletion rules must follow applicable legal, operational, and contractual requirements.
HIMSSON must not automatically delete records that require preservation for legitimate legal, financial, academic, or audit purposes. Equally, it must not retain personal data indefinitely without an appropriate basis.
Exact retention periods, archival procedures, and deletion workflows remain subject to later definition.
9. Integration Security
Integrations must:
•	Receive only the data and permissions necessary for their purpose.
•	Protect credentials and other integration secrets.
•	Validate incoming data, callbacks, and provider responses.
•	Preserve relevant audit trails and school-data boundaries.
•	Handle outages, retries, duplicate events, and reconciliation safely.
External providers must not become unrestricted access routes into HIMSSON.
10. Offline Access and Synchronization
Offline capability must respect the same authorization principles as online operation.
HIMSSON must minimize sensitive information stored locally, protect local sessions and data, and revalidate permissions when reconnecting.
Synchronization must reconcile conflicting changes without bypassing validation or silently overwriting authoritative records.
Sensitive data must not remain accessible through the application after logout or loss of authorization beyond what is explicitly permitted by the implemented security model.
Offline access does not extend or override user permissions.
11. Backup and Recovery
HIMSSON must provide for protected backups, restricted backup access, tested restoration, and preservation of required history and audit information.
Backup frequency, retention, recovery objectives, and restoration procedures must be defined before production deployment.
Backup existence alone is insufficient; recovery capability must be tested.
12. Security Incident Handling
HIMSSON must support a defined process for identifying, investigating, containing, and recovering from security incidents.
Relevant incidents include compromised accounts, unauthorized data exposure, suspicious administrative changes, and integration-related security failures.
Incident procedures must address evidence preservation and applicable notification or reporting obligations.
Operational incident-response procedures must be established before launch.
13. Deferred Implementation Decisions
The following remain implementation or production-readiness decisions rather than architectural locks:
•	Authentication methods, session controls, and account-recovery mechanics.
•	Detailed role-permission matrices and backend enforcement implementation.
•	Encryption configuration and key management.
•	Data-retention periods and deletion workflows.
•	Backup schedules, recovery objectives, and restoration testing.
•	Security monitoring and incident-response procedures.
•	Privacy notices and applicable legal workflows.
•	Security testing requirements and release acceptance criteria.
14. Final Architectural Rule
Every HIMSSON module must operate under backend-enforced authorization, explicit school isolation, least privilege, privacy-conscious processing, auditable consequential actions, controlled data lifecycle, secure integrations, and tested recovery.
No module may independently weaken these requirements.
Document Status: LOCKED — APPROVED
 
HIMSSON SCHOOL MANAGEMENT SYSTEM
Architecture Lock Document 0.9 — Data Management, Import, Export & Recovery Operations
Document ID: HIMSSON-ARCH-0.9
Status: LOCKED — APPROVED
Product: HIMSSON
Parent Company: MANKO Innovations
Version: 1.0
1. Purpose
This document establishes the common standards for managing data throughout HIMSSON, including data entry, bulk imports, exports, corrections, archival, migration, reconciliation, and operational recovery.
The objective is to preserve data quality, operational continuity, school-level isolation, and historical integrity across all modules.
2. Data Ownership and Validation
1.	Each domain remains responsible for validating and maintaining its authoritative records.
2.	Shared data-management mechanisms may provide common validation, import, export, and recovery capabilities, but must not bypass domain rules.
3.	Validation must cover required fields, data types, relationships, permissions, applicable business rules, and school or academic context.
4.	Data must be validated on the backend before it is committed.
5.	The system must distinguish validation errors, warnings, rejected records, and successfully committed records.
6.	Invalid data must not silently enter authoritative records.
7.	Changes affecting dependent records must trigger appropriate validation or reconciliation.
3. Bulk Import and Data Entry
HIMSSON must support controlled bulk-data operations where appropriate, particularly for school onboarding and recurring administrative tasks.
The standard import process is:
Prepare → Upload/Enter → Validate → Preview → Confirm → Commit → Reconcile → Report
Requirements:
•	Provide understandable templates or clearly defined input formats where appropriate.
•	Validate the full import against applicable business rules and user permissions.
•	Present errors and warnings before final confirmation.
•	Identify potential duplicates and conflicting records.
•	Allow users to correct invalid data and retry without unnecessarily repeating successful work.
•	Report which records were accepted, rejected, skipped, or require review.
•	Preserve an import history sufficient to investigate significant operations.
•	Prevent retries from unintentionally creating duplicate transactions or records.
Partial success must be explicit. The system must not present a partly completed import as wholly successful.
Whether an import is atomic or permits controlled partial commits must depend on the operation's risk and consistency requirements. Financial and other consequential operations require stricter commit and reconciliation rules.
4. Duplicate Detection and Identity Integrity
Duplicate detection must use appropriate identifying attributes and domain-specific rules rather than names alone.
The system must distinguish:
•	A confirmed existing record.
•	A possible duplicate requiring review.
•	A genuinely new record.
•	A correction to an existing record.
•	A legitimate new historical or contextual record for an existing person or entity.
For example, a student may retain the same identity while receiving a new academic enrollment. That new enrollment must not be mistaken for a duplicate student identity.
Automated matching must not silently merge uncertain identities or overwrite established records.
5. Data Export
HIMSSON must support authorized exports for legitimate school operations, reporting, administration, and record portability.
Exports must:
1.	Respect the requesting user's role, permissions, organization, and school scope.
2.	Include only the data necessary for the authorized purpose.
3.	Apply the same access restrictions as the underlying records.
4.	Preserve meaningful identifiers, relationships, dates, and contextual information where relevant.
5.	Clearly identify the exported dataset, applicable filters, and generation time where appropriate.
6.	Avoid exposing sensitive data through filenames, public links, logs, or uncontrolled storage.
7.	Provide a traceable record of significant exports where appropriate.
Export formats must suit the use case, including spreadsheet-compatible, PDF, and other structured formats where required.
An export is a representation of source data, not a replacement for the authoritative records inside HIMSSON. Editing an exported file must not change system records unless it is deliberately submitted through an authorized import or update workflow.
6. Corrections, Deletion, and Historical Integrity
HIMSSON must distinguish ordinary editable information from consequential records that require controlled correction.
•	Routine changes may update current values when permitted by the relevant domain.
•	Material financial, academic, enrollment, attendance, publication, and administrative corrections must follow applicable authorization and audit requirements.
•	Where historical truth matters, corrections must preserve the required original context and traceability.
•	Superseded or corrected records must not be represented as though the original event never occurred.
•	Deletion must follow domain rules, permissions, dependencies, and applicable retention requirements.
•	Records required for legal, financial, academic, or audit purposes must not be casually or irreversibly deleted.
A correction is not necessarily a new business event. The system must distinguish correcting an existing event from recording a genuinely new event.
7. Archival and Historical Data
HIMSSON must support access to historical information without treating every historical record as active operational data.
Archival must preserve the context necessary to interpret records correctly, including relevant school, academic year, enrollment, financial, and assessment relationships.
Archiving must not silently change official historical results, rewrite previous financial transactions, alter prior enrollment or attendance history, remove required audit trails, or transfer records across school boundaries.
Historical records must remain subject to authorization and applicable retention rules.
Exact archival schedules and storage implementation remain deferred.
8. Backup, Restoration, and Recovery Operations
Section 0.8 establishes the requirement for protected backups and tested restoration. This section defines the corresponding operational principles.
1.	Recovery operations must be restricted to explicitly authorized users or processes.
2.	Restoration must protect current data from accidental overwrite.
3.	The intended recovery scope must be established before execution.
4.	Restoration must preserve school isolation and applicable integrity constraints.
5.	Restored data must be validated before the recovered system or records are treated as operationally reliable.
6.	Recovery outcomes, failures, and consequential actions must be traceable.
7.	Restoring data must not silently duplicate financial transactions, messages, imports, or other consequential events.
8.	Recovery must account for changes made after the recovery point, where applicable.
A recovery operation must distinguish between restoring an entire environment, recovering selected records, and correcting a specific operational mistake. These are different operations and must not be treated as interchangeable.
9. Migration and School Onboarding
Moving existing school records into HIMSSON must follow a controlled migration process.
Source Assessment → Mapping → Validation → Trial Import → Reconciliation → Authorized Cutover → Post-Migration Verification
Requirements:
•	Identify source data, its ownership, quality, structure, and limitations.
•	Map source fields to HIMSSON's authoritative entities and relationships.
•	Preserve original identifiers or migration references where useful and lawful.
•	Identify missing, invalid, conflicting, and duplicated records.
•	Validate financial balances, enrollment relationships, academic history, and other material records according to the migration scope.
•	Record migration outcomes and unresolved exceptions.
•	Prevent accidental mixing of data belonging to different schools.
•	Establish a controlled cutover and recovery approach appropriate to the migration risk.
Imported historical information must not automatically be treated as a newly confirmed business event. For example, migrating a historical payment record must not trigger a second payment collection or create a misleading new-payment notification.
10. Reconciliation and Consistency
HIMSSON must support reconciliation wherever data can become inconsistent through imports, integrations, synchronization, migration, or interrupted operations.
Reconciliation must compare the relevant records or expected outcomes, identify discrepancies, and direct them into an authorized resolution process.
Examples include imported student records versus validated enrollments; payment-provider confirmations versus financial records; transactions versus ledger balances; assessment data versus official results; offline changes versus current records; and restored data versus integrity requirements.
Reconciliation must not automatically conceal discrepancies or overwrite valid records without the relevant domain's rules and authorization.
11. Failure Handling and Operational Visibility
Data-management operations must provide understandable outcomes and actionable failure information.
Common operational states include:
•	Not started.
•	In progress.
•	Completed.
•	Completed with exceptions.
•	Failed.
•	Awaiting review or reconciliation.
•	Recovery required.
Individual modules may use more specific states where necessary.
Interrupted operations must be safe to inspect and resume where supported. Retrying must not create duplicate or conflicting outcomes.
Users must receive clear information about what succeeded, what failed, and what action is required. Technical diagnostics must be protected from unauthorized disclosure.
12. Cross-Cutting Security and Audit Requirements
All data-management operations must comply with Sections 0.4 and 0.8, including organization and school isolation, backend-enforced permissions, least-privilege access, protection of sensitive information, auditability, controlled retention and deletion, and secure handling of files, integrations, and recovery data.
Administrative convenience must not override data integrity or access-control requirements.
13. Deferred Implementation Decisions
The following remain for implementation planning and technical design:
•	Exact import templates, file formats, and field mappings.
•	Duplicate-matching algorithms and review interfaces.
•	Which specific operations require atomic processing.
•	Export formats, size limits, and access-expiry mechanisms.
•	Archival schedules and retention periods.
•	Backup frequency, recovery objectives, and restore procedures.
•	Migration tools, cutover mechanics, and reconciliation reports.
•	Detailed operational status models and monitoring.
•	Storage architecture and implementation-specific performance limits.
14. Final Architectural Rule
HIMSSON must make data manageable without sacrificing its correctness, provenance, security, or historical meaning.
Every consequential data operation must be authorized, validated, appropriately traceable, explicit about its outcome, and recoverable or correctable according to the relevant domain's rules.
Document Status: LOCKED — APPROVED
 
HIMSSON SCHOOL MANAGEMENT SYSTEM
Architecture Lock Document 0.10 — User Experience, Navigation & Cross-Module Interaction Architecture
Document ID: HIMSSON-ARCH-0.10
Status: LOCKED — APPROVED
Product: HIMSSON
Parent Company: MANKO Innovations
Version: 1.0
1. Purpose
Establish a consistent, practical, mobile-first experience across HIMSSON while accommodating the different responsibilities of school owners, administrators, teachers, accountants, parents, and other authorized users.
The objective is to reduce operational friction, make important tasks easy to complete, and maintain consistency across all product domains.
2. Role-Aware Experience
The experience must adapt to the user's authorized responsibilities and current school context.
•	Owner/Director: Organization oversight, school switching, relevant summaries, and authorized organization-level operations.
•	School Administrator: School operations, people, records, configuration, and authorized oversight.
•	Teacher: Assigned classes, subjects, attendance, assessments, and other permitted teaching responsibilities.
•	Accountant: Authorized financial operations, payments, balances, reconciliation, and reporting.
•	Parent/Guardian: Relevant child information, permitted payments, announcements, and published academic information.
These are experience defaults, not substitutes for backend authorization. The interface must never expose information merely because a role's dashboard displays a shortcut.
3. Navigation and Information Architecture
HIMSSON must use a coherent navigation structure organized around recognizable school responsibilities and the five core product domains:
1.	School & People
2.	Money
3.	Academics
4.	Communication
5.	Documents & School Operations
Navigation must be consistent across modules, with labels understandable to the intended users.
Frequently performed tasks should be easy to reach. Less frequent administrative settings should remain discoverable without overwhelming daily operational screens.
The exact menu layout, icons, and page hierarchy remain UI-design decisions.
4. Organization and School Context
For users authorized to access multiple schools:
•	The active school must be clearly identifiable.
•	School switching must be deliberate and unambiguous.
•	Switching schools must update the active data context.
•	School-scoped screens, lists, forms, reports, and actions must respect the selected context.
•	The system must not silently carry an action into another school.
•	Organization-level views must be visibly distinguishable from individual-school views.
A displayed school selector is a context aid, not a security boundary. Backend authorization remains mandatory.
5. Task-Oriented Workflows
HIMSSON must organize operational screens around completing real tasks, not merely displaying database records.
Examples include enrolling a student, recording or reconciling a payment, marking attendance, entering marks, publishing results, sending an announcement, and importing existing school data.
Workflows should make the next valid action clear, explain relevant errors, preserve entered work where practical, and avoid unnecessary repetition of information.
Consequential actions must communicate their effects before confirmation where appropriate.
6. Cross-Module Consistency and Domain Ownership
Modules must share common interaction patterns without duplicating authoritative business logic.
Examples:
•	A payment recorded in Money may produce a notification through Communication.
•	A validated academic result may be presented through the report-card experience.
•	Attendance screens may use academic-group and calendar context.
•	A scheduled lesson may provide a route to relevant class or subject operations.
In each case, the owning domain remains authoritative. Navigation links and summaries must not create independent or conflicting versions of the same record.
7. Mobile-First and Low-End Device Usability
The experience must prioritize practical use on mobile devices, including low-end Android phones and constrained connectivity.
Requirements include responsive layouts, touch-friendly controls, short operational workflows, readable text, clear status indicators, minimal unnecessary data transfer, useful loading/error/empty/offline states, and preservation of entered work where practical.
Desktop experiences may provide denser administrative views where appropriate without breaking the shared design language.
8. Language, Clarity, and Accessibility
HIMSSON must support Swahili and English, with Swahili-first defaults appropriate to the Tanzanian initial market.
Language must be consistent within a workflow. Dates, amounts, statuses, validation messages, and instructions must be presented clearly and according to the applicable context.
The interface should use understandable terminology, meaningful labels, adequate contrast, keyboard accessibility where applicable, and clear feedback for actions and errors.
Localization must not alter the underlying meaning of financial, academic, or administrative records.
9. Status, Feedback, and Error Recovery
Users must be able to distinguish between actions that are available, in progress, successfully completed, awaiting confirmation or review, rejected or blocked, failed and eligible for retry, or completed with exceptions.
A button click must not be presented as proof that a consequential operation succeeded. The interface must reflect the authoritative operation outcome.
Errors should explain the issue and, where practical, how to resolve it. Retrying must not inadvertently duplicate payments, submissions, imports, or other consequential actions.
10. Forms, Lists, and Record Details
Common interface patterns should be reused across domains for data entry, search, filtering, lists, record details, validation, and confirmation.
Forms should request only necessary information, distinguish required from optional fields, and explain validation failures clearly.
Lists and record details must preserve the correct school and business context, show relevant statuses, and provide only actions the user is authorized to perform.
Bulk operations must use the controlled data-management standards defined in Section 0.9.
11. Dashboards and Reporting Experience
Dashboards must prioritize information relevant to the user's responsibilities and decisions.
Summaries must identify their scope and time context where relevant. They must derive from authoritative records and must not imply that incomplete, unverified, or unpublished information is official.
Organization-wide summaries and individual-school summaries must be clearly distinguished.
Dashboards must support action and understanding rather than becoming collections of decorative metrics.
12. Design-System Consistency
HIMSSON must use a shared design system for typography, spacing, colors, navigation, forms, buttons, tables, dialogs, notifications, and status presentation.
The design system must support role-aware and domain-specific workflows without allowing each module to develop an unrelated visual language.
Visual consistency must not override usability, accessibility, or the need to distinguish high-risk actions.
13. Deferred Implementation Decisions
The following remain for UX design and implementation:
•	Exact page hierarchy, menus, and navigation components.
•	Final visual tokens and component specifications.
•	Detailed dashboards and role-specific home screens.
•	Page-level workflow and interaction designs.
•	Offline interaction patterns for individual operations.
•	Accessibility acceptance criteria and device/browser support.
•	Exact Swahili and English terminology and translation review.
•	User research, usability testing, and performance targets.
14. Final Architectural Rule
HIMSSON must present one coherent, role-aware, school-aware, mobile-first operating experience.
The interface should make valid tasks easy, consequential actions clear, errors recoverable, and authoritative information consistent—while backend security and domain ownership remain in control.
Document Status: LOCKED — APPROVED
 
HIMSSON SCHOOL MANAGEMENT SYSTEM
Architecture Lock Document 0.11 — Configuration, Customization & School-Level Rules Architecture
Document ID: HIMSSON-ARCH-0.11
Status: LOCKED — APPROVED
Product: HIMSSON
Parent Company: MANKO Innovations
Version: 1.0
1. Purpose
Establish a consistent configuration architecture that accommodates differences between schools without fragmenting HIMSSON into incompatible implementations.
HIMSSON must provide sensible defaults, authorized school-level customization, controlled organization-level settings, and predictable behavior when configurations change.
2. Configuration Scope and Hierarchy
Configuration must respect the established Organization and School architecture.
The conceptual hierarchy is:
HIMSSON Platform Defaults → Organization-Level Settings, Where Applicable → School-Level Settings → Context-Specific Rules, Where Supported
Not every setting must exist at every level. Each setting must have a defined owner, permitted scope, and inheritance behavior.
A school must not be able to override platform-level security controls or another school's configuration.
3. Defaults and Overrides
1.	HIMSSON should provide sensible defaults for common school operations.
2.	Schools should configure supported settings without requiring custom development.
3.	An override must apply only to its authorized scope.
4.	Changing a shared default must not silently overwrite intentional school-specific settings.
5.	Inherited and overridden values must be distinguishable where relevant.
6.	Invalid or incompatible configurations must be rejected or clearly flagged.
7.	Missing configuration must have a defined fallback or produce a clear setup requirement; it must not silently produce misleading results.
4. Configurable School Structures and Academic Rules
Schools must be able to configure supported academic structures and operating rules appropriate to their education levels and academic contexts.
Examples include academic years, periods, class or form structures, streams, subject combinations, subject offerings, assessment arrangements, grading scales, and result calculation methods.
Configuration must respect the academic architecture established in Sections 0.5 and 0.6.
HIMSSON must not assume that every school follows the same calendar, number of terms, class progression, assessment model, or subject structure.
5. Financial and Operational Configuration
Supported school-level settings may include fee structures, permitted payment methods, financial categories, receipt presentation, attendance rules, timetable structures, room capacities, communication preferences, and document templates.
Configuration must not bypass authoritative domain validation, financial integrity, scheduling constraints, or required audit controls.
The availability of a setting must depend on the user's permissions and the applicable operational context.
6. Permissions and Configuration Authority
Configuration changes must be restricted to authorized roles and scopes.
HIMSSON must distinguish between viewing configuration, proposing or editing configuration, approving consequential changes where required, activating configuration, and reviewing configuration history.
The exact permission matrix and approval workflows remain implementation decisions.
A user must not gain additional privileges merely by changing a setting, editing a template, or accessing a configuration interface.
7. Configuration Versioning and Effective Dates
Configuration changes must have a clear effective point where timing affects operational correctness.
Changes to consequential rules—such as grading scales, result calculations, fee structures, calendars, or scheduling constraints—must not silently reinterpret completed historical activity.
Where historical interpretation depends on a rule, HIMSSON must preserve or reference the applicable rule version or historical configuration context.
The system must distinguish between a change intended for future operations and an authorized correction to an existing configuration or historical record.
8. Validation and Dependency Management
Before activation, configuration must be checked for missing requirements, invalid values, incompatible settings, and conflicts with dependent records.
Examples include academic periods that do not fit the configured academic year, assessment rules incompatible with result calculations, subject combinations referencing unavailable subjects, capacity changes affecting future bookings, fee changes affecting existing or future charges, and notification settings conflicting with mandatory security requirements.
The owning domain must determine the relevant validation rules.
Where existing records are affected, HIMSSON must identify the impact and apply the relevant controlled workflow rather than silently rewriting records.
9. Configuration History and Reversibility
Consequential configuration changes must be traceable to the authorized actor, affected scope, change, and time.
Where practical, users should be able to review previous configurations and restore a previous setting through an authorized process.
Reverting a configuration must not automatically reverse completed payments, erase published results, remove attendance history, or undo other business events.
A configuration rollback and a business-record correction are distinct operations.
10. Setup, Readiness, and Incomplete Configuration
HIMSSON must distinguish between required configuration missing, initial setup in progress, configuration valid but not yet activated, configuration active, configuration invalid or conflicting, and configuration requiring review.
The system should guide authorized users through required setup in a practical order.
Optional features must remain optional. A school should not be blocked from using independent functionality merely because an unrelated optional module has not been configured.
Operations that genuinely require missing configuration must be blocked or explicitly limited rather than producing unreliable outcomes.
11. Configuration and Multi-School Organizations
An Organization may operate multiple schools with different valid configurations.
Shared organization-level settings may be supported where appropriate, but each school's operational rules must remain distinct unless a setting is explicitly designed to be shared.
Changing one school's configuration must not unintentionally alter another school's behavior.
Organization-wide reporting or administration must respect the applicable configuration context of each school.
12. Localization and Templates
Supported configuration may include school names and identity assets, document and receipt presentation, message templates, language preferences, and other presentation settings.
Presentation customization must not change the underlying meaning, amount, status, authorization, or official nature of a record.
Swahili and English support must remain consistent with the localization principles in Section 0.10.
13. Deferred Implementation Decisions
The following remain for technical design and implementation:
•	Exact configuration registry and storage model.
•	Which settings are platform-, organization-, school-, or context-scoped.
•	Exact inheritance and override mechanics.
•	Detailed role permissions and approval workflows.
•	Versioning, effective-date, and migration mechanics.
•	Configuration screens and guided setup.
•	Exact validation rules for each domain.
•	Which settings support rollback and under what conditions.
14. Final Architectural Rule
HIMSSON must be configurable enough to support real differences between schools without sacrificing consistency, security, historical integrity, or ease of operation.
Every configuration must have an explicit owner, scope, validation rule, and defined effect on existing and future operations. Consequential changes must not silently rewrite historical truth.
