HIMSSON SCHOOL MANAGEMENT SYSTEM
Architecture Lock Document 0.12 — Integrations & External Services Architecture
Document ID: HIMSSON-ARCH-0.12
Status: LOCKED — APPROVED
Product: HIMSSON
Parent Company: MANKO Innovations
Version: 1.0
1. Purpose
Establish a consistent architecture for integrating HIMSSON with external providers, platforms, and services.
The architecture must support reliable operations while protecting school data, preserving authoritative records, preventing duplicate transactions, and reducing unnecessary dependence on individual vendors.
2. Integration Principles and Ownership
1.	HIMSSON retains authority over its internal business records, permissions, and domain rules.
2.	External services provide specialized capabilities; they do not automatically become the source of truth for HIMSSON's internal records.
3.	Every integration must have a defined purpose, owner, data scope, security model, and failure-handling strategy.
4.	Provider responses must be validated before they influence authoritative records.
5.	External service failures must not silently produce false success states or corrupt internal data.
6.	Integrations must respect organization and school isolation, data minimization, auditability, and applicable legal requirements.
3. Integration Categories
The architecture must accommodate relevant external services, including:
•	Payments and financial services: Mobile-money providers, payment gateways, and financial transaction services.
•	Communication services: SMS gateways and other supported messaging providers.
•	Identity and authentication services: External identity capabilities where deliberately adopted.
•	Maps and location services: Geographic services used by relevant modules.
•	File and document services: External storage or document-processing capabilities where appropriate.
•	Other APIs and services: Future integrations introduced through an authorized, controlled design.
Not every category must be implemented in the initial release. Each integration must be justified by a product requirement.
4. Payment Integration Architecture
Payment integrations must preserve the distinction between a payment attempt, a provider's response, a confirmed financial transaction, and the corresponding HIMSSON financial record.
The conceptual flow is:
Payment Initiation → Provider Interaction → Response Validation → Transaction Reconciliation → Authoritative Financial Record → Receipt/Ledger Update → Notification
Requirements:
1.	Payment initiation must create a traceable internal reference.
2.	Provider transaction references must be retained where available and appropriate.
3.	A payment must not be marked confirmed solely because the user clicked a payment button or the provider accepted an initiation request.
4.	Callbacks, webhooks, polling responses, and other provider messages must be authenticated and validated as supported by the provider.
5.	Duplicate, delayed, out-of-order, and repeated provider messages must not create duplicate financial transactions.
6.	Uncertain outcomes must remain distinguishable from confirmed success or confirmed failure.
7.	Reconciliation must identify unresolved transactions, discrepancies, reversals, and other relevant exceptions.
8.	Receipts, balances, and student ledgers must follow HIMSSON's authoritative financial rules.
9.	A communication failure after a confirmed payment must not invalidate the payment itself.
Payment providers facilitate payment processing. HIMSSON's Money domain remains responsible for its internal financial records and applicable reconciliation rules.
5. Communication Provider Integration
Communication integrations must follow Section 0.7.
The Communication domain owns notification orchestration, recipient resolution, channel selection, delivery tracking, retry rules, and message history. External providers perform the relevant delivery service.
Requirements include:
•	Validate destination information and applicable permissions before sending.
•	Minimize the personal and school information included in outgoing messages.
•	Distinguish queued, attempted, provider-accepted, failed, and confirmed-delivery states where supported.
•	Handle provider callbacks and delivery reports safely.
•	Prevent uncontrolled duplicate messages during retries.
•	Respect school-level communication settings, language preferences, applicable limits, and cost controls.
•	Preserve the underlying business event when a message fails.
Provider acceptance must not be represented as proof that a recipient read or received a message unless the available evidence supports that claim.
6. API, Webhook, and Callback Security
All externally initiated requests must be treated as untrusted until validated.
The architecture must support, as appropriate:
•	Provider authentication and request verification.
•	Secure transport and protected credentials.
•	Input validation and schema checks.
•	Replay protection and duplicate-event detection.
•	Idempotent processing of repeat requests.
•	Rate limiting and abuse protection.
•	Authorization and scope checks.
•	Safe handling of malformed, unexpected, or unauthorized requests.
•	Audit records for consequential integration events.
A provider callback must not be allowed to update arbitrary records merely because it contains a valid-looking transaction or record identifier.
7. Data Mapping and Reconciliation
Each integration must define how external identifiers, statuses, amounts, timestamps, and other relevant fields map to HIMSSON's internal concepts.
External and internal status models may differ. Mapping must be explicit and must not treat superficially similar statuses as equivalent without validation.
Reconciliation must be available where provider activity can diverge from HIMSSON's internal records.
Examples include payment confirmations, transaction reversals, failed messages, interrupted synchronization, and delayed provider notifications.
Unresolved discrepancies must remain visible for investigation. The system must not silently overwrite authoritative records to make internal and external states appear consistent.
8. Reliability, Retries, and Idempotency
Integrations must account for timeouts, temporary outages, delayed responses, duplicate events, rate limits, and partial failures.
The architecture must distinguish between:
•	A request that was never sent.
•	A request that failed before provider acceptance.
•	A request accepted by the provider but not yet confirmed.
•	An outcome that remains uncertain.
•	A confirmed successful operation.
•	A confirmed failed or reversed operation.
•	An operation requiring reconciliation or manual review.
Retries must follow operation-specific rules. They must not create duplicate payments, duplicate records, or uncontrolled message delivery.
Where an operation's outcome is uncertain, HIMSSON must establish its state before blindly initiating a potentially duplicate operation.
9. Provider Independence and Replacement
HIMSSON should minimize unnecessary coupling to a single provider.
Where practical, integrations should use defined internal interfaces and provider-specific adapters so that provider details do not spread throughout unrelated business domains.
Replacing a provider must account for existing transaction and message references, outstanding operations, unresolved transactions, credential rotation, data mapping, reconciliation, migration, testing, and controlled activation.
Provider independence does not mean every service must be interchangeable. Differences in capabilities, contractual terms, costs, and technical behavior must be acknowledged.
10. Outages and Degraded Operation
HIMSSON must handle external service outages without compromising internal data integrity.
Depending on the operation, the system may queue eligible work, delay processing, permit unaffected functionality to continue, or present a clear unavailable or pending state.
Examples:
•	A messaging outage must not reverse an already confirmed payment.
•	A payment-provider timeout must not automatically imply payment failure.
•	A map-service outage must not corrupt operational records.
•	A delayed callback must not create a second transaction when it eventually arrives.
Queued operations must be processed safely when service resumes. Offline capability does not justify bypassing provider confirmation or domain validation.
11. Monitoring, Audit, and Operational Support
HIMSSON must provide sufficient operational visibility to investigate integration failures and discrepancies.
Where appropriate, monitoring should cover provider availability, request failures, latency, retries, callback processing, unresolved transactions, reconciliation exceptions, and message-delivery outcomes.
Audit trails must preserve relevant references, timestamps, outcomes, and processing history without unnecessarily recording sensitive information.
Credentials, authentication secrets, and payment-sensitive data must not be exposed in ordinary logs or diagnostic responses.
12. Environment Separation and Change Control
Integrations must support controlled development, testing, and production deployment where the provider offers the necessary environments.
Test activity must not unintentionally create real payments, send unintended production messages, or modify live school records.
Credential management, endpoint configuration, provider activation, and changes to integration behavior must be controlled and authorized.
Before production activation, each integration must have appropriate verification of successful operation, failure handling, duplicate prevention, security, and reconciliation.
13. Deferred Implementation Decisions
The following remain for technical design and implementation:
•	Selected providers, contractual arrangements, and supported countries.
•	API specifications, authentication mechanisms, and webhook verification details.
•	Payment initiation, confirmation, reversal, and reconciliation procedures for each provider.
•	Retry schedules, timeouts, rate limits, and queue implementation.
•	Provider adapters and internal integration interfaces.
•	Credential storage, rotation, and operational access procedures.
•	Monitoring tools, alert thresholds, and support responsibilities.
•	Provider-specific data mappings and retention requirements.
•	Integration testing plans and production acceptance criteria.
14. Final Architectural Rule
HIMSSON must integrate external capabilities without surrendering control of its internal business rules, authoritative records, school boundaries, or security requirements.
Every integration must be purpose-defined, secured, validated, traceable, resilient to failure, and reconcilable where external and internal states may diverge.
