HIMSSON SCHOOL PROJECT
Architecture Lock 0.13 — Performance, Scalability & Operational Reliability
Status: LOCKED — APPROVED
Section: 0.13
Product: HIMSSON School Management SaaS
Parent Organization: MANKO Innovations
1. Purpose
Define the architectural principles that allow HIMSSON to remain responsive, reliable, maintainable, and operationally safe as the number of organizations, schools, users, students, records, and transactions increases.
Performance improvements must preserve authorization, school isolation, domain ownership, transaction integrity, and recoverability.
2. Core Decision
HIMSSON will use a measured, workload-aware, reliability-first architecture. Performance and scalability will be evaluated against real school workflows rather than appearance or isolated technical benchmarks.
The architecture must support growth without unnecessary redesign, isolate failures where practical, and make operational problems detectable and recoverable.
3. Performance Principles
•	Prioritize frequent and operationally important workflows, including attendance marking, student search, payment processing, fee-balance review, and marks entry.
•	Retrieve only the records and fields required for the task.
•	Use appropriate pagination, filtering, and server-side queries for growing datasets.
•	Avoid unnecessary repeated requests, duplicate processing, and unrelated recalculations.
•	Provide clear progress, completion, failure, and pending states for consequential operations.
•	Never present incomplete, unverified, or unconfirmed data as authoritative.
•	Do not trade security or data integrity for apparent speed.
4. Growth and Scalability
The architecture must account for growth in:
•	Organizations, schools, users, students, and academic enrollments.
•	Academic records, assessments, results, and report cards.
•	Fees, payments, receipts, ledgers, and financial reports.
•	Notifications, message history, and delivery attempts.
•	Timetables, scheduled lessons, and operational records.
•	Documents, imports, exports, and audit history.
Implementations must avoid unnecessary full-dataset retrieval and processing. Growth must not silently weaken organization-level or school-level data isolation.
5. Low-End Devices and Unreliable Connectivity
HIMSSON must remain practical on low-end Android devices and unreliable or expensive networks.
The system should:
•	Minimize unnecessary data transfer, oversized responses, heavy assets, and background requests.
•	Keep frequent workflows efficient and usable on constrained devices.
•	Provide understandable offline, syncing, pending, failed, and completed states where applicable.
•	Apply the established offline and synchronization rules, including revalidation of permissions and consequential operations.
•	Avoid duplicate records or transactions when connectivity returns.
•	Preserve local-data protection and backend authorization requirements.
6. Concurrency and Data Integrity
The system must support multiple authorized users working concurrently without silent overwrites or inconsistent authoritative records.
Concurrent changes must follow appropriate validation and conflict-handling rules.
Stronger integrity controls are required for consequential operations, especially:
•	Payments, receipts, financial ledgers, and reconciliation.
•	Official result calculation, verification, locking, and publication.
•	Timetable commitments, conflict validation, and compliance resolution.
•	Imports, recovery operations, and other changes that affect multiple records.
A successful user action must not be assumed merely because a request was submitted. The system must distinguish a requested action from a committed and verified outcome.
7. Background Processing
Operations that may take significant time or resources should use appropriate background processing where justified. Examples include:
•	Large imports and exports.
•	Report and document generation.
•	Notification delivery and controlled retries.
•	Payment and external-provider reconciliation.
•	Eligible offline synchronization and batch processing.
Background work must have identifiable status, traceable outcomes, controlled retries, and actionable failure handling. Retrying a job must not duplicate its underlying business events or consequential records.
8. Failure Isolation and Graceful Degradation
Failure in one component or external service should not unnecessarily disable unrelated HIMSSON functions.
Examples:
•	A messaging-provider outage must not invalidate an otherwise confirmed payment.
•	A map-service outage must not disable unrelated school-administration workflows.
•	A delayed report-generation job must not corrupt academic records.
•	A temporary connectivity failure must not create duplicate payments or attendance records during recovery.
When functionality is degraded, HIMSSON must communicate the relevant state, preserve recoverable work where possible, and provide a safe recovery path.
Graceful degradation must never bypass authorization, required validation, or critical data-integrity controls.
9. Monitoring and Operational Visibility
HIMSSON must be designed to make material operational problems detectable.
Monitoring should cover relevant indicators such as:
•	Response times and error rates.
•	Failed or delayed background jobs.
•	Queue delays and processing failures.
•	External integration failures.
•	Offline synchronization exceptions.
•	Resource pressure and capacity concerns.
Logs and operational records must respect security and data-minimization requirements. Alerts should be actionable, appropriately routed, and controlled to avoid excessive noise or duplicate escalation.
10. Capacity Planning and Verification
Before production deployment, performance and reliability must be evaluated against representative usage conditions.
Verification should consider:
•	Representative school sizes and data volumes.
•	Concurrent users and competing operations.
•	Low-end devices and constrained connectivity.
•	Normal operating conditions and relevant degraded conditions.
•	Recovery after failed jobs, interrupted requests, or connectivity loss.
•	Correctness and integrity under load, not performance alone.
Production readiness must be based on recorded verification evidence, not assumptions that the system is scalable because it works with a small demonstration dataset.
11. Maintainability and Performance Optimization
Performance improvements must be based on evidence and must preserve established architecture boundaries.
Optimization must not create uncontrolled coupling between domains, bypass backend security, weaken school isolation, or introduce ambiguous ownership of authoritative records.
Caching, batching, asynchronous processing, and other optimization techniques must preserve the correctness of consequential data and the validity of access permissions.
12. Decisions Deferred
The following remain implementation or deployment decisions:
•	Specific response-time targets and service-level objectives.
•	Expected school sizes, peak concurrency, and capacity assumptions.
•	Hosting, database, and infrastructure choices.
•	Caching strategies and invalidation rules.
•	Background-job and queue technologies.
•	Monitoring, logging, and alerting tools.
•	Load, stress, endurance, and recovery test thresholds.
•	Availability targets and mobile/network performance budgets.
These decisions must be defined and verified before the relevant production requirements are considered complete.
13. Final Architectural Rule
HIMSSON must combine efficient workflows, measured scalability, controlled concurrency, observable operations, and safe failure handling. No performance or availability optimization may compromise authorization, authoritative records, transaction integrity, organization or school isolation, or recoverability.
14. Change Control
This section is locked. Any material change requires an explicit decision to reopen the section, evaluate its architectural implications, and update the approved document before implementation proceeds.
 
HIMSSON SCHOOL PROJECT
Architecture Lock 0.14 — Testing, Quality Assurance & Release Readiness
Status: LOCKED — APPROVED FOR NOW
Detail Level: Architectural baseline approved; detailed QA specification deferred
Section: 0.14
Product: HIMSSON School Management SaaS
Parent Organization: MANKO Innovations
1. Purpose
Establish a unified quality-assurance architecture for verifying HIMSSON's functionality, security, data integrity, usability, performance, integrations, and release readiness.
Testing must demonstrate that the system satisfies approved requirements and preserves previously verified behavior as the product evolves.
2. Core Decision
HIMSSON will use a risk-based, layered, evidence-driven testing strategy.
Testing must cover individual rules, interactions between domains, complete user workflows, security boundaries, failure conditions, and release readiness. A feature is not considered verified merely because its interface renders or a demonstration succeeds.
3. Testing Layers
The quality strategy must include appropriate levels of testing:
•	Unit testing: individual calculations, validation rules, permissions, and business logic.
•	Integration testing: interactions between domains, services, databases, background jobs, and external providers.
•	Contract testing: expected request, response, event, and status behavior between connected components.
•	End-to-end testing: complete user workflows across the interface and backend.
•	Security and authorization testing: role permissions, organization and school isolation, relationship-based access, and prohibited actions.
•	Operational testing: synchronization, recovery, failure handling, concurrency, and performance under representative conditions.
Testing depth must reflect risk. Consequential financial, academic, security, and scheduling rules require stronger verification than presentation-only changes.
4. Requirements Traceability
Every material requirement and architectural rule must have a corresponding verification approach appropriate to its risk.
Test cases, execution results, defects, fixes, and release decisions must remain distinguishable and traceable.
Testing records must identify:
•	What was tested and the expected outcome.
•	The actual result and supporting evidence.
•	Whether the test passed, failed, was blocked, or remains untested.
•	Related defects and their current status.
•	Retesting and regression results after a fix.
Untested functionality must never be represented as passed. A defect marked fixed must not be treated as verified until the relevant retest has succeeded.
5. Domain and Business-Rule Verification
Testing must verify approved source-of-truth boundaries and critical business rules across HIMSSON.
•	School and People: identity, enrollment lifecycle, role permissions, school context, and historical records.
•	Money: fee calculations, payment states, duplicate prevention, provider confirmation, receipts, ledgers, reconciliation, and financial reporting.
•	Academics: assessment validation, calculations, grading, absent/exempt/incomplete handling, result locking, publication, corrections, and historical interpretation.
•	Attendance: eligibility, attendance statuses, bulk marking, corrections, calculation rules, and authorized access.
•	Scheduling: teacher and group conflicts, room commitments, combined lessons, capacity classifications, exceptions, escalation, and resolution.
•	Communication: recipient resolution, language and channel rules, delivery states, retries, duplicate suppression, and failure isolation.
•	Data operations: imports, exports, reconciliation, corrections, archival, migration, backup restoration, and recovery.
6. Security, Privacy and Isolation Testing
Testing must establish that:
•	Users cannot access records outside their authorized organization, school, role, or relationship scope.
•	School switching correctly changes the active authorization context.
•	Frontend visibility restrictions are reinforced by backend authorization.
•	Direct API requests, manipulated identifiers, exports, background jobs, and integration callbacks cannot bypass access controls.
•	Consequential actions require the correct permissions and validations.
•	Sensitive information is not unnecessarily exposed through logs, errors, messages, or offline data.
•	Unauthorized actions fail safely without creating partial or inconsistent records.
Security testing must cover both permitted actions and explicit attempts to perform prohibited actions.
7. Financial and Integration Integrity
Payment and integration testing must cover successful, failed, delayed, duplicated, interrupted, and uncertain outcomes.
Verification must establish that:
•	Provider initiation is not mistaken for payment confirmation.
•	Repeated requests or callbacks do not create duplicate financial records.
•	Delayed and out-of-order events are handled safely.
•	Uncertain payment outcomes are reconciled before potentially duplicating an operation.
•	Receipts and ledger updates reflect authoritative financial records.
•	Provider failures do not falsely report successful completion.
•	Notification failures do not invalidate confirmed financial transactions.
Equivalent idempotency, validation, and recovery principles apply to other consequential integrations and background operations.
8. Academic and Scheduling Integrity
Academic tests must verify calculation correctness, applicable rule versions, official result states, publication permissions, correction history, and historical consistency.
Scheduling tests must verify conflict prevention, authorized exceptions, room-sharing rules, mandatory capacity violations, escalation behavior, rescheduling, and revalidation.
A user being able to bypass a required control is not evidence that the workflow works correctly.
9. Offline, Synchronization and Recovery Testing
Testing must include intermittent connectivity, interrupted operations, repeated submissions, delayed synchronization, conflicting edits, and expired or changed permissions.
Verification must establish that:
•	Synchronization does not silently overwrite authoritative data.
•	Repeated synchronization does not duplicate business events.
•	Conflicts and failures produce understandable, recoverable states.
•	Authorization is revalidated where required.
•	Recovery preserves committed records and identifies unresolved work.
•	Restored data is validated and reconciled before normal operation is assumed.
Offline support is not verified solely by confirming that screens open without connectivity.
10. Usability, Accessibility and Device Coverage
Testing must reflect HIMSSON's intended operating environment, including low-end Android devices, constrained networks, and Swahili/English usage.
Verification should cover:
•	Completion of frequent school workflows.
•	Clear validation, pending, success, and failure states.
•	Readability, navigation, and practical touch interaction.
•	Language selection, translated content, and text-length variations.
•	Appropriate accessibility and input handling.
•	Recovery from common user errors and interrupted workflows.
•	Reasonable responsiveness under representative device and network conditions.
Where applicable, school staff must be able to complete core tasks without relying on undocumented technical workarounds.
11. Regression Testing and Change Control
Previously verified behavior must be protected when changes are introduced.
Changes must be evaluated for their impact on dependent domains, shared rules, integrations, permissions, reports, and historical records.
Regression testing must cover affected functionality and relevant high-risk workflows. Shared components and architectural changes may require broader regression coverage.
A fix must not be accepted solely because the original symptom disappears; related behavior and possible side effects must also be evaluated.
12. Test Environments and Data
Testing must use appropriately controlled environments and representative test data.
The approach must:
•	Separate development, test, staging, and production responsibilities as appropriate.
•	Prevent test activity from corrupting production records or triggering unintended real payments and messages.
•	Protect personal and sensitive information in test datasets.
•	Provide repeatable test scenarios and identifiable test records.
•	Verify important configurations and integrations before production use.
•	Preserve evidence needed to reproduce and investigate defects.
Test-environment success must not automatically be treated as proof that production configuration is correct.
13. Defect Management and Release Gates
Defects must be assessed by severity, impact, scope, likelihood, and available workarounds.
Release decisions must consider unresolved defects, failed or incomplete tests, security risks, data-integrity risks, migration readiness, integration readiness, monitoring, and recovery capability.
Release gates must require, as applicable:
•	Completion of required tests with recorded results.
•	Resolution or explicit authorized acceptance of release-relevant defects.
•	Successful retesting of fixes and required regression checks.
•	Verification of critical permissions and school isolation.
•	Verification of consequential financial and academic workflows.
•	Confirmation of production configuration and integration readiness.
•	A documented deployment, rollback, and recovery approach appropriate to the change.
A release must not be declared ready merely because deployment succeeds.
14. Deployment, Rollback and Post-Release Verification
Release procedures must include controlled deployment, post-deployment checks, monitoring, and a defined response to failure.
Rollback decisions must account for database migrations, committed financial transactions, published academic results, queued messages, and other consequential operations.
Rolling back application code must not silently reverse or corrupt committed business events. Where a technical rollback cannot safely reverse data changes, an appropriate forward-fix or controlled recovery procedure must be available.
Material releases must be verified in the target environment before completion is declared.
15. Testing Evidence and Honest Status Reporting
HIMSSON's quality records must distinguish:
•	Planned versus executed tests.
•	Passed versus failed tests.
•	Blocked versus untested checks.
•	Defect identification versus fix implementation.
•	Fix implementation versus successful retesting.
•	Test completion versus release approval.
Claims about system quality, security, completeness, or production readiness must be supported by appropriate evidence.
16. Decisions Deferred
The following remain implementation or project-planning decisions:
•	Testing frameworks, automation tools, and test-management platforms.
•	Exact test coverage targets and quantitative quality thresholds.
•	Detailed test-case catalogues and execution schedules.
•	Defect severity definitions and service-level targets.
•	CI/CD tooling and automated release-gate implementation.
•	Exact device/browser compatibility matrices.
•	Penetration-testing scope and independent security-assessment requirements.
•	Production monitoring thresholds and release-specific acceptance criteria.
These decisions will be defined according to the implementation, risk profile, and deployment requirements.
17. Final Architectural Rule
HIMSSON must demonstrate quality through repeatable tests, recorded evidence, risk-based verification, regression control, and explicit release gates. No feature, fix, integration, or release may be represented as verified beyond the evidence actually obtained.
18. Change Control and Future Expansion
This section is approved and locked as the current architectural baseline. Its detailed implementation remains open for future specification.
Future QA documents may define test cases, automation strategy, coverage targets, execution procedures, defect workflows, and measurable release criteria without reopening this architectural lock, provided they remain consistent with its principles.
Any material architectural change requires an explicit decision to reopen the section, evaluate the implications, and update the approved document.



 
