HIMSSON SCHOOL MANAGEMENT SYSTEM
UI/UX ARCHITECTURE — MASTER DOCUMENT
Document Reference: 0.10
Document Title: User Experience, Navigation & Cross-Module Interaction Architecture
Product: HIMSSON School Management System
Parent Company: MANKO Innovations
Document Status: Consolidated Review Draft — Existing Architectural Decisions Preserved
Version: 1.0 — Development Readiness Review
Purpose: Establish the UI/UX architectural foundation for development prompts, interface implementation and subsequent verification.
 
1. PURPOSE
This document defines the user experience architecture for HIMSSON.
It establishes the principles, navigation structure, role-aware interaction model, school context handling, domain boundaries and implementation constraints that the user interface must follow.
The objective is to enable development to proceed without repeatedly reopening established architectural decisions or allowing individual development prompts to introduce inconsistent navigation, unauthorized functionality or uncontrolled design changes.
This document consolidates existing approved architecture and the provisional direction established during UI/UX Architecture Phase 1.
It does not replace or override existing lock documents.
2. RELATIONSHIP TO EXISTING ARCHITECTURE
UI/UX implementation must remain consistent with the following existing decisions:
•	0.0 — Product Identity & Core Definition: HIMSSON is a connected school operating system.
•	0.1 — Product Principles & Design Rules: Operations-first, mobile-first, low-end Android support, bilingual usability, paperless workflows, security and disciplined scope.
•	0.2 — Core Product Architecture: Five product domains and their respective sources of truth.
•	0.3 — Product Development & Architecture Discipline: Architecture-first development, controlled implementation, explicit verification and preservation of locked decisions.
•	0.4 — Organization, Authority & Multi-School Access: Organization-level ownership, linked schools, explicit school context and scoped access.
•	0.5 — School Structure & Operational Model: Flexible school and education-level structures.
•	0.6 series — Academic Architecture: Student lifecycle, enrollment, subjects, results, attendance, calendars and timetable rules.
•	0.7 — Communication & Notifications Architecture: Source events, recipient resolution, delivery and status handling.
•	0.8 — Security, Privacy & Data Governance Architecture: Authorization, school isolation, auditability and data protection.
•	0.9 — Data Management, Import, Export & Recovery Operations: Controlled data operations and historical integrity.
•	0.10 — User Experience, Navigation & Cross-Module Interaction Architecture: Primary UX architecture reference.
•	0.11 — Configuration, Customization & School-Level Rules Architecture: Configuration authority, scope, dependencies and effective dates.
•	0.12 — Integrations & External Services Architecture: Integration status and internal source-of-truth boundaries.
•	0.13 — Performance, Scalability & Operational Reliability Architecture: Efficient, resilient operation.
•	0.14 — Testing, QA & Release Readiness Architecture: Risk-based testing and release verification.
If a UI proposal conflicts with a locked architectural rule, the locked rule takes precedence. Any required change must follow the existing decision-reopening and documentation process.
3. CORE UX PRINCIPLES
3.1 Operational usability
HIMSSON must help authorized users complete real school-management tasks clearly and efficiently.
The interface must prioritize understandable workflows, meaningful status information, appropriate actions and clear outcomes over decorative complexity.
3.2 Mobile-first implementation
The experience must work effectively on mobile devices, particularly low-end Android devices.
Interfaces should minimize unnecessary navigation, excessive page weight, avoidable interactions and unnecessary network dependence.
Desktop must remain a fully considered experience rather than an enlarged mobile interface.
3.3 Role-aware experience
Users should encounter navigation, content and actions relevant to their roles and actual permissions.
A shared design framework should provide consistency across roles without implying that every role has identical responsibilities or access.
3.4 School-aware experience
The active school context must remain clear whenever school-specific operations are performed.
The interface must not imply that linked schools automatically share their operational records or that organization-level authority removes school-level access boundaries.
3.5 Domain integrity
The interface must respect the five product domains:
1.	School & People
2.	Money
3.	Academics
4.	Communication
5.	Documents & School Operations
Each domain remains responsible for its own authoritative data and business rules.
3.6 State clarity
Users must be able to distinguish relevant workflow states, including draft, validated, verified, locked, published, withdrawn, pending, failed and completed states where applicable.
The interface must not present an initiated action as a confirmed outcome.
3.7 Accessibility and language
The experience must support the established Swahili-first communication direction and English-language usability.
Text, controls, navigation labels and feedback must remain understandable on small screens and in ordinary school operating conditions.
Exact translation resources and detailed accessibility criteria remain subject to implementation planning and verification.
4. FIVE PRODUCT DOMAINS
The primary navigation architecture must represent the existing five domains.
4.1 School & People
Responsible for school and people-related workflows, including student identity and enrollment-related operations where authorized.
The interface must preserve the distinction between a student identity and a time-bound academic enrollment.
4.2 Money
Responsible for school financial workflows.
The interface must respect finance permissions, authoritative payment records, student ledgers, reconciliation and receipt-related rules.
Payment initiation or provider acceptance must not be represented as confirmed payment without authoritative confirmation.
4.3 Academics
Responsible for academic workflows, including relevant school structures, subjects, attendance, assessments, results and timetable-related operations.
The interface must respect academic context, enrollment, validation, result locking and publication rules.
Attendance must remain usable without requiring a timetable.
4.4 Communication
Responsible for communication orchestration, notification delivery and delivery status.
The interface must distinguish message creation, delivery state and recipient acknowledgment where applicable. Provider acceptance must not be treated as proof that a recipient has read a message.
4.5 Documents & School Operations
Responsible for document-related workflows and school operational functions assigned to this domain.
Exact page grouping and detailed navigation remain subject to the approved capabilities and subsequent implementation mapping.
5. PROVISIONAL APPLICATION SHELL
The following design direction was established during Phase 1. It is suitable to guide initial implementation planning but has not yet been formally locked.
5.1 Desktop layout
Provisional direction: hybrid sidebar and top header.
The sidebar provides primary access to the five product domains and the Home destination.
The top header provides persistent application identity, active school context and appropriate account-level controls.
The implementation should avoid unnecessary duplication between the sidebar, header and page-level navigation.
The exact sidebar grouping, labels, iconography, collapse behavior and responsive breakpoints remain open.
5.2 Mobile layout
Provisional direction: four bottom-navigation destinations.
The four-destination constraint is intended to preserve clarity and usability on small screens.
The current conceptual arrangement is:
•	Home
•	School & People
•	Academics
•	More
This is an unapproved working arrangement, not a final locked mapping.
The remaining domains, including Money, Communication and Documents & School Operations, must remain discoverable through appropriate secondary navigation.
No domain may be treated as unavailable merely because it is not represented directly in the bottom navigation.
The final grouping, labels, ordering and placement of frequently used actions must be validated against the actual implemented workflows.
5.3 Compact school header
Provisional direction: compact but unambiguous school context.
The interface should identify the active school without consuming excessive mobile screen space.
Where a user is authorized to access multiple schools, school switching must be explicit and must not silently change the target of an in-progress consequential operation.
Where the user has access to only one school, unnecessary switching controls may be omitted if doing so remains consistent with the implementation.
School identity, switching and all subsequent data access must remain subject to backend authorization.
5.4 Role-based Home
Provisional direction: shared framework with role-relevant content.
Home should use a consistent structural framework across the supported roles while presenting only relevant, authorized information and actions.
The architecture does not prescribe unapproved dashboard metrics, cards, alerts or new workflows.
Home content must be based on existing capabilities and the permissions of the active user.
6. ROLE AND AUTHORIZATION MODEL
The UI must accommodate the existing role model without creating a separate, inconsistent application for each role.
6.1 Owner/Director
May have organization-level authority over one or more linked schools, subject to the established access model.
The interface must make the active school context clear when the user enters school-specific operations.
Organization-level authority must not automatically merge school records or bypass the permissions governing individual operations.
6.2 School Administrator
Operates within the authorized school scope.
The interface should provide relevant school-management entry points while respecting the permissions applicable to finance, academics, configuration and other protected workflows.
6.3 Teacher
Access must respect the user's school and teaching-assignment scope.
The interface must not imply unrestricted access to all students, subjects, classes or academic records merely because the user is a teacher.
6.4 Accountant
Access must respect the applicable school and finance scope.
Financial navigation and operations must not be exposed as unrestricted capabilities to unrelated roles.
6.5 Parent
Access must remain focused on the parent's authorized child-related information and relevant school context.
The existence of a parent-child relationship must not, by itself, grant access to unrelated school records or unpublished academic results.
6.6 Mandatory authorization rule
Navigation visibility is not a security boundary.
Every protected operation must be authorized by the backend according to identity, role, scope and permissions. Direct URLs, API calls, exports and other access paths must not bypass those checks.
7. CROSS-MODULE INTERACTION RULES
The UI must preserve the existing domain workflows and source-of-truth relationships.
7.1 Academic results
The interface must distinguish mark entry from validated results and distinguish official result status from report-card publication status.
Published results must follow the established authorization and withdrawal rules.
7.2 Student attendance
Attendance interfaces must respect the applicable school, academic enrollment, date and attendance context.
Corrections must preserve the established audit and history requirements.
7.3 Finance
Payment initiation, provider confirmation, payment records, receipts and ledger updates must remain distinct stages in the authoritative financial workflow.
A visual success message must not be displayed before the relevant authoritative outcome is confirmed.
7.4 Communication
Communication interfaces must reflect the actual state of message processing and delivery.
Retries, duplicate prevention and stale-message suppression must remain consistent with the Communication Architecture.
7.5 Configuration
Configuration interfaces must respect the applicable authority, scope, effective dates, dependencies and audit requirements.
Changing a configuration must not silently rewrite historical events or bypass domain validation.
7.6 School switching
Changing the active school context must not silently transfer a user into an equivalent-looking workflow targeting another school's records.
Any in-progress consequential operation must retain a clear target context or require an appropriate revalidation before completion.
8. INTERACTION AND FEEDBACK STANDARDS
Across all domains, interfaces should provide clear feedback for:
•	Loading and processing.
•	Successful completion after confirmation.
•	Validation errors and corrective actions.
•	Permission-denied states.
•	Empty states where no relevant records exist.
•	Pending, failed or uncertain operations.
•	Offline conditions and synchronization outcomes where applicable.
•	Consequential changes and their recorded outcomes.
The UI must not invent successful outcomes, conceal validation failures or treat temporary communication failure as proof that the underlying business operation failed or succeeded.
Where an operation's result is uncertain, the interface must communicate that uncertainty and respect the underlying domain's reconciliation or recovery workflow.
9. VISUAL AND RESPONSIVE IMPLEMENTATION
The interface must use the established HIMSSON design direction and any applicable locked design-system decisions.
UI development prompts must not independently introduce a conflicting visual identity, navigation model or interaction pattern.
The implementation must consider:
•	Small-screen layouts and touch targets.
•	Desktop navigation and information density.
•	Readability and clear hierarchy.
•	Consistent labels, icons, controls and status presentation.
•	Responsive behavior without loss of essential functionality.
•	Efficient rendering and limited unnecessary assets.
•	Clear error, loading and offline states.
•	Consistent interaction patterns across domains.
Exact component specifications, typography, spacing, design tokens and visual details must follow the applicable design-system decisions rather than being independently invented by individual development prompts.
10. DEVELOPMENT PROMPT CONTROL
All UI development prompts must comply with this document and the existing architecture.
Each prompt must:
1.	Identify the exact scope and intended screens or components.
2.	Reference the relevant existing architectural decisions.
3.	Reuse the approved design system and shared application shell.
4.	Respect the five-domain model and the role/scope authorization model.
5.	Preserve existing functionality and established business rules.
6.	Avoid adding unapproved features or changing locked decisions.
7.	Define relevant loading, error, empty, pending and success states.
8.	Specify responsive behavior where the scope requires it.
9.	Identify relevant integration and backend dependencies.
10.	Include verifiable acceptance criteria.
11.	State what must not be changed.
12.	Require a report of implementation results, unresolved dependencies and verification outcomes.
UI components must not independently implement business rules that belong in the domain or backend architecture.
Where required functionality is not yet approved or sufficiently defined, the development prompt must identify the gap rather than silently inventing a solution.
11. DEVELOPMENT READINESS CHECKLIST
Before implementation of a particular screen or workflow, confirm the following:
•	The screen belongs to an existing approved domain or explicitly approved shared application layer.
•	The intended user roles and permissions are known.
•	The active school and data scope are clear.
•	The authoritative data source and relevant workflow states are understood.
•	Applicable validation, audit and history rules are identified.
•	Relevant loading, empty, error and pending states are specified.
•	The existing design system and shared components are reused.
•	Responsive and mobile requirements are addressed.
•	Required backend functions and integrations are identified.
•	Acceptance criteria and verification steps are included.
•	No locked architectural decision is being changed without authorization.
An unchecked item should be treated as a dependency or an explicit limitation of the development task, not silently assumed to be complete.
12. OPEN ITEMS
The following items remain unresolved or require confirmation. Their presence in this section does not authorize new features.
1.	Final desktop sidebar grouping and interaction details.
2.	Final four-destination mobile navigation labels, ordering and grouping.
3.	Detailed Home content for each supported role.
4.	Final compact school-header presentation and switching interaction.
5.	Detailed page-level navigation and shared component mapping.
6.	Exact visual specifications not already covered by locked design-system decisions.
7.	Implementation-specific accessibility and performance acceptance criteria.
These items should be resolved only to the level necessary for the relevant development phase. They do not all need to be finalized before every development prompt can be produced.
13. ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA FOR UI/UX ARCHITECTURE
The UI/UX architecture is ready to guide implementation when:
•	The shared application shell and role-aware structure are consistently applied.
•	The five existing domains remain represented and discoverable.
•	School context is clear wherever school-specific work is performed.
•	Role-based navigation does not substitute for backend authorization.
•	Academic, financial, communication and configuration states remain consistent with their authoritative domain rules.
•	Mobile and desktop interfaces preserve essential workflows.
•	Development prompts respect existing locks and explicitly report unresolved dependencies.
•	Implementation is verifiable against documented acceptance criteria.
These criteria establish architectural expectations; they do not certify that the current application has already met them.
14. FINAL ARCHITECTURAL POSITION
HIMSSON will use a consistent, role-aware and school-aware user experience built around the existing five product domains.
The provisional direction is a hybrid desktop shell, a shared role-based Home framework, four mobile bottom-navigation destinations and a compact school header.
The final mobile grouping and detailed screen-level decisions remain open. They must be resolved pragmatically during implementation planning without repeatedly reopening settled business and security architecture.
The priority is now to proceed from architecture into controlled development prompts, implementing the established model, verifying each meaningful increment and documenting genuine gaps when they arise.
Document control: This document consolidates existing architecture and provisional UI/UX direction. It does not independently lock open decisions, approve new functionality or supersede any existing locked document.

 


